.\n2345:- `playAfter(f: InputFrame): void`: Replay, after `beginFrame` (which polled no gamepads): sticks, pointer, wheel, gamepad connection.\n2354:- `held?: string[]`: Keys held, keyboard and gamepad buttons together, sorted.\n2356:- `analog?: [number, number]`: The touch joystick and the gamepad's left stick (after its deadzone).\n2361:- `gamepad?: boolean`: Whether a gamepad is connected (`gamepadConnected`), when that changes.\n2374:- `readonly gamepad: boolean`\n2609:- `vibrato?: { depth: number; rate: number }`: Vibrato depth in semitones and rate in Hz.\n----\nGUIDE.md:41:  (paths are relative to the page). To ship one file with no `assets/` folder, set\nGUIDE.md:135:    // The hero: the bundled model and clips on a Mario-64-style controller.\nGUIDE.md:1006:| Console: \"Multiple instances of Three.js being imported\" | three.js loaded twice | use `THREE` from the bundle, nothing else |\nCHANGELOG.md:5:`engine.state().version`. The engine kit (`npm run bundle`, docs/GUIDE.md) ships this file.\nCHANGELOG.md:7:When a change alters the public API (`src/bundle.ts` exports) or something a game can see,\nCHANGELOG.md:20:  no `node_modules`. In Node, Rapier's wasm comes from the data URL the kit inlines, and a reader\nCHANGELOG.md:408:  bundled; it resolves to a `ModelCatalog` (`into` also adds the defs to one of yours).\nCHANGELOG.md:412:  Anyone else's work, CC0 included and a single file too, only from a pack (`source.pack`) whose\nCHANGELOG.md:821:- **The engine kit**: `npm run bundle` builds `pixel-engine.js`, one self-contained ES module\nAPI.md:45:Models every bundle carries: `ctx.loadModel('assets/hero.glb')` works with no files next to it.\nAPI.md:2743:- `burst(preset: string | ParticlePreset, at: Vector3 | readonly [number, number, number], options: BurstOptions = {}): number`: Spawn a burst of `preset` (a name or a ParticlePreset) at `at`. Returns the number of particles spawned (fewer when the pool is full, 0 for an unknown preset). Inline preset objects share one emitter per distinct content, so `burst({ ...literal }, at)` every…\nAPI.md:3030:base URL; a page may put a data URL for it in `__PIXEL_ASSETS__`, as single-file builds do for\nAPI.md:3074:The library's families (one file each, src/engine/model/library/<family>.ts) in order: each","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01EUdx3pR8LLzbtmeX7Qek13","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgke5edr3wJuwpayrGXVH","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01UT6jnyMXbsqPMLiZi2HA19","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261105-162057__x4c6ec5/work/_research/engine/pixel-engine/ASSETS.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8553,"cache_read_input_tokens":97555,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":8553},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"66624378-a7bb-42a2-8c1d-fc63666b5654","uuid":"fcd627e1-5963-439c-a35a-08442c57b7fe","timestamp":"2026-11-05T23:21:49.866Z","request_id":"req_011Cgke5eQhtgnPPxQRHjk4Z","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01UT6jnyMXbsqPMLiZi2HA19":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261105-162057__x4c6ec5/work/_research/engine/pixel-engine/ASSETS.md"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01UT6jnyMXbsqPMLiZi2HA19","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Pixel Engine: models and textures\n2\t\n3\t> Pixel Engine **0.46.0**, build 2026-11-02 f75d4a6. Read `GUIDE.md` first. `LIBRARY.md` lists the built-in models and textures, `CREDITS.md` where they came from.\n4\t\n5\tFor an agent building a game on the kit; read `GUIDE.md` first. Objects and pixel-art textures\n6\tare **plain data** you write, check and look at, then fix: a `ModelDef` of keyed primitives that\n7\tthe engine turns into toon meshes, a `TextureDef` of layers it bakes into texels. No modelling\n8\ttool and no image files. `LIBRARY.md` lists the built-in models and textures and the imported\n9\tpacks (`library.json` holds the same as data), `check.mjs --sheets` draws what you write, and `examples/market.html` is a scene built this way\n10\t(section 6). Every name here comes from `./pixel-engine.js`; `API.md` has the signatures.\n11\t\n12\tContents: 1 The loop · 2 Models · 3 Textures · 4 Style rules · 5 In a game · 6 The example ·\n13\t7 Credits\n14\t\n15\t## 1. The loop\n16\t\n17\t1. **Start from the library.** Search `LIBRARY.md` (by family, then each pack; id, size, params,\n18\t   palette slots, tags, one row each, a model's variants like `rock-large-a` … `-f` sharing\n19\t   one), filter `library.json`, or search in code:\n20\t   `MODEL_LIBRARY.search('wooden chair')` ranks id words over tags over `about`, plurals and\n21\t   parts of words included; `MODEL_LIBRARY.get('fence')` takes an id. Place library models as\n22\t   they are, re-coloured or with other params, and `use` them inside your own: a stall with\n23\t   crates is a few lines.\n24\t2. **Write** your defs in a module of their own (`models.js`) that exports them; the game\n25\t   imports it. Keep the engine start out of that module.\n26\t3. **Check and look:** `node check.mjs --sheets models.js` draws a sheet of every def the module\n27\t   exports (by name, in an array or object, or in a catalog: `check/sheets/<id>.png`; textures\n28\t   `<id>.tex.png`) and prints its problems and warnings; it exits 1 on a problem. **Open the\n29\t   PNGs.** `--sheets fence,cobbles` draws library ids: look at one before you place it.\n30\t4. **Fix** the numbers the messages and the sheet point at, and run it again until there is no\n31\t   problem and the game-scale previews read. Warnings name what won't read at 480×270: fix them\n32\t   unless you mean it.\n33\t5. **Look in the game:** `node check.mjs index.html --cameras`, then the PNGs: the real\n34\t   renderer, with lights and looks.\n35\t\n36\tIn code, `checkModel(def, { catalog })` and `validateTexture(def)` return\n37\t`{ problems, warnings }`, and `modelSheet(def, { catalog })` and `textureSheet(def)` return the\n38\tsheet as RGBA pixels (`image`) with them.\n39\t\n40\t| sheet row | answers |\n41\t| --- | --- |\n42\t| turntable, 8 angles at one scale | Is the silhouette right from every side? |\n43\t| front / side / top on a 0.1 m grid, with the size in metres | Are the parts where the numbers say? Do they touch? Is it the right size? |\n44\t| game scale: an iso camera (yaw 45°, pitch 42°) at about 19 and 22 pixels a metre, one art pixel per pixel, ×4 | Does it read in a game? (The engine's iso camera at zoom 1 shows about 20.) |\n45\t| palette, check | Which slots, which colours, which are unused; the problems and warnings, each naming the part and the fix |\n46\t\n47\tSheets are drawn in software (three shading bands, outlines) without point lights or looks: the\n48\tgame is the truth.\n49\t\n50\t## 2. Models\n51\t\n52\t```js\n53\texport const crate = model({\n54\t  id: 'crate',\n55\t  about: 'Wooden crate with a frame and diagonal braces', // one line: find() searches it\n56\t  tags: ['container', 'wood'],\n57\t  palette: { wood: 'orange', band: 'red' }, // slots → the 16 palette names (GUIDE.md section 4)\n58\t  parts: [\n59\t    { box: [0.68, 0.68, 0.68], at: [0, 0.36, 0], c: 'wood' },\n60\t    boxFrame({ size: [0.72, 0.72, 0.72], beam: 0.09, at: [0, 0.36, 0], c: 'band' }),\n61\t    { mirror: [{ box: [0.8, 0.08, 0.04], at: [0, 0.36, 0.35], rot: [0, 0, 45], c: 'band' }], axis: 'z' },\n62\t  ],\n63\t});\n64\t```\n65\t\n66\tMetres and degrees; +Y is up, +Z is the front, the floor is y = 0. Parts are keyed, never\n67\tpositional. **Every primitive fills its box `[w, h, d]`, centred on `at`**, turned by `rot` (XYZ,\n68\tabout `at`), and round kinds stretch to it:\n69\t\n70\t- `box`; `cyl` (an n-sided prism, `seg` sides, default 6 to 10 by size; `top`: the top width ÷\n71\t  the bottom width); `cone` (point up); `ball` (`half`: the upper dome); `lump` (a rock, `seed`);\n72\t  `wedge` (a ramp, full height at the back, −Z); `torus` (a ring in XZ as thick as the box is tall).\n73\t- Profile kinds use their own coordinates, placed at `at`: `lathe` ((radius, height) points\n74\t  turned about Y), `slab` (an XY outline or SVG path, extruded `depth` along Z), `tube` (points\n75\t  and a radius `r`, or a pair that tapers).\n76\t- Every part takes `c` (its slot; default the first), `name`, `glow: true` (unlit: a flame's\n77\t  heart, eyes), `tex` (a texture id, section 3), `seg` and `collide: false` (no box of its own\n78\t  under `collider: 'parts'`: a doorway's mortar ring).\n79\t\n80\t**Composition:**\n81\t\n82\t- `{ group: [...], at, rot, scale, c }`: a frame whose `c` is its parts' default. `pivot: true`\n83\t  with a `name` keeps it a movable Group (a door, a lid: `build.object.getObjectByName('lid')`).\n84\t- `{ mirror: [...], axis: 'x' }`: the parts and their mirror image (`axis: 'z'` mirrors front to back).\n85\t- `{ use: 'barrel', at, rot, scale, params, seed, palette: { theirs: 'ours' } }`: another model\n86\t  by id. Its slots map onto yours through `palette`, else onto yours of the same name, else keep\n87\t  their own colours.\n88\t- `on: 'name'`: sit on the named part's top; `at` becomes an offset from there.\n89\t- `params: { h: [default, min, max] }` with `parts: (p, rng) => [...]`: parametric (`p.h`) and\n90\t  seeded (`rng.range(a, b)`). Helpers return parts: `around`, `rowOf`, `gridOf`, `scatterOf`,\n91\t  `boxFrame`.\n92\t- Model keys: `size` (declared bounds, checked), `anchors` (`{ grip: [x, y, z] }`), `light: { at,\n93\t  c, intensity, radius, flicker }`, `collider` (`'box'`, `'cyl'`, `'hull'`, `'parts'` (a box per\n94\t  part: doorways stay open) or `'none'`), `floats: true` (it hangs), `texel` (texels a metre for\n95\t  `tex` parts, default 8).\n96\t\n97\t**Problems** (`check.mjs --sheets` exits 1; `buildModel` throws a `ModelError` on those it can't\n98\tbuild): no `about` or `tags`; an undeclared slot or a colour that isn't a palette name; an unknown\n99\t`use`, `on` or texture; a size that isn't positive; `seg` outside 3–64; NaN; an empty model; below\n100\tthe floor (unless `floats`); over the declared `size`; params out of range; a part that both\n101\tglows and takes a `tex`; a `texel` outside 2–64. **Warnings:** a part floating more than 1 cm from the\n102\trest; rods under 0.06 m; over the triangle budget (300 under 0.5 m across, 1,000 under 2 m, else\n103\t3,000); over 8 materials; a part hidden inside another; coplanar faces of two colours; touching big\n104\tmasses too close in lightness; one colour over 40% of the model in four or more parts; glow over\n105\t15% of it. `checkModel` builds the defaults, every param at its min and at its max, and three\n106\tseeds when the parts read `rng`.\n107\t\n108\t## 3. Textures\n109\t\n110\t```js\n111\texport const bricks = textureDef({\n112\t  id: 'bricks',\n113\t  about: 'Stone bricks in courses over dark mortar',\n114\t  tags: ['stone', 'wall', 'surface'],\n115\t  size: [16, 16], // texels; powers of two when it tiles\n116\t  tile: true, // seamless: patterns wrap\n117\t  palette: { mortar: 'night', stone: 'slate', stone2: 'mist' },\n118\t  layers: [{ pattern: 'bricks', colors: ['mortar', 'stone', 'stone2'], w: 8, h: 4, seed: 2 }],\n119\t});\n120\t```\n121\t\n122\t**Layers** draw in order: `{ pixels: [...rows], legend: { k: 'slot' } }` (`.` or a space is clear;\n123\t`at`, `mirror: 'x'` or `'y'`), `{ pattern, colors, seed, dither, ...params }` (over a ramp of\n124\tslots, dark to light), `{ path: 'M … Z', fill, stroke }` (SVG in texel units) and `{ outline:\n125\tslot }`. `frames: n` makes a strip of n frames, left to right.\n126\t\n127\t**Patterns:** `noise bricks tiles planks cobbles stripes checker scatter cracks gradient`.\n128\tParameters: noise `scale`, `octaves`; bricks and tiles `w`, `h`, `mortar`, `offset`, `bevel`;\n129\tplanks `w`, `gap`, `length`, `grain`; cobbles `cells`, `gap`; stripes `width`, `dir`; checker\n130\t`cell`; scatter `count`, `size`, `shape`; cracks `count`, `length`; gradient `dir`. A problem\n131\tnames the range of a bad one.\n132\t\n133\t**Density:** surfaces at 8 texels a metre (`DEFAULT_TEXEL`); denser ones shimmer as the camera\n134\tzooms. A 16-texel tile covers 2 m, so draw a brick of 8 × 4 texels for 1 × 0.5 m. **Warnings:** a\n135\tseam across the wrap, salt and pepper (over 15% single texels), a tiling size that isn't a power\n136\tof two, unused slots.\n137\t\n138\t## 4. Style rules\n139\t\n140\t- **Scale:** the hero is 1.6 m (1.75 m with the cap); a door 2.2 m, a table 0.75 m, a chair seat 0.45 m.\n141\t- **Silhouette first.** The iso camera sees the top and two sides: big masses must read; put\n142\t  detail on the top and the front.\n143\t- **Thickness:** 0.06 m at least; 0.12 m or more for parts that carry the silhouette.\n144\t- **Colour separates parts:** touching parts take different colours or shades (planks and their\n145\t  frame, stones and mortar), up to 8, one an accent; touching big masses differ in lightness.\n146\t- **Glow is flat:** keep it for small hot spots. For fire `use: 'flame'`, and never turn it about\n147\t  Y: its tongues face the camera. Below `h` 0.5 its 2–4 tongues read as a dark smudge: use\n148\t  `tongues: 1` for small fires.\n149\t- **Low poly:** 6 to 10 sides on round kinds, no bevels under 2 cm.\n150\t- **Grounded:** the base on y = 0, nothing floating unless `floats`.\n151\t- **Compose before you create:** `use` a library model before you write a new crate.\n152\t\n153\t## 5. In a game\n154\t\n155\t- `buildModel(idOrDef, { params, seed, palette, light, catalog, textures })` returns `object`\n156\t  (one merged mesh per material; pivots are named Groups), `bounds`, `anchors`, `colliders`\n157\t  (data: `addModelColliders(physics, build, at, yaw)`), `light` (in model space: place it with\n158\t  the model, as the example does) and `report`. Builds whose parts don't read `rng` are cached and\n159\t  share geometry: place as many as you like.\n160\t- **Your own models:** a `ModelCatalog` of the library and yours, passed as `catalog` (or\n161\t  `MODEL_LIBRARY.add(def)`); your own textures likewise in a `TextureCatalog`, passed as `textures`.\n162\t- **Textures:** `toonMaterial(PALETTE.white, { map: textureMap('bricks') })` on any mesh (the\n163\t  map is cached and shared: scale UVs, never the map); `new SpriteBatch(spriteSheet('coin-spin'),\n164\t  { capacity: 64 })`; `hudSprite('gem-icon')` gives rows and colours for `ctx.hud.sprite`.\n165\t- **Packs** (`packs/`, listed in `packs/index.json` and LIBRARY.md): kits by other artists,\n166\t  imported into this format and palette. `const kit = await loadPack('packs/<id>.json',\n167\t  { textures: TEXTURE_LIBRARY })`, then `buildModel('<id>/<name>', { catalog: kit })`; its\n168\t  textures then work by id like the library's. `kit.search('tree')` searches the pack. To vary\n169\t  one of its models, load the pack into your catalog (`loadPack(url, { into: MODEL_LIBRARY })`)\n170\t  and add a def there that `use`s it with a `scale` or a `palette`: nothing is copied.\n171\t\n172\t## 6. The example\n173\t\n174\tMarket Corner, the kit's `examples/market.html`: library models (one re-coloured, three with\n175\tparams, the lamp post's light made brighter), a cobbled floor from `textureMap`, and a stall of\n176\tthe game's own in a module of its own. `node check.mjs examples/market.html` plays it;\n177\t`node check.mjs --sheets examples/market-models.js` draws its stall.\n178\t\n179\t```js market-models\n180\t// Market Corner's own models: plain data in a module of their own, so `node check.mjs --sheets\n181\t// examples/market-models.js` can draw them.\n182\timport { model, MODEL_LIBRARY, ModelCatalog } from './pixel-engine.js';\n183\t\n184\texport const fruitStall = model({\n185\t  id: 'fruit-stall',\n186\t  about: 'The market stall under a green awning, two baskets of fruit on its counter',\n187\t  tags: ['town', 'market'],\n188\t  palette: { wood: 'orange', cloth: 'green', cloth2: 'white', top: 'sand', basket: 'red', fruit: 'orange', fruit2: 'lime' },\n189\t  parts: [\n190\t    { use: 'market-stall' }, // its slots take ours of the same name: the awning turns green\n191\t    ...[-0.45, 0.4].flatMap((x, i) => [\n192\t      { name: `basket${i}`, cyl: [0.56, 0.14, 0.4], at: [x, 1.01, 0.05], top: 1.25, c: 'basket' },\n193\t      ...[-0.17, 0, 0.17].map((dx, k) => ({ ball: [0.18, 0.18, 0.18], on: `basket${i}`, at: [dx, 0, 0], c: (i + k) % 2 ? 'fruit2' : 'fruit' })),\n194\t    ]),\n195\t  ],\n196\t});\n197\t\n198\t// Where the game's ids resolve: the library and ours (buildModel's `catalog`).\n199\texport const MODELS = new ModelCatalog([...MODEL_LIBRARY.all(), fruitStall]);\n200\t```\n201\t\n202\t```js market\n203\t// Market Corner: library models and the game's own fruit stall on a cobbled square at dusk.\n204\t// Nothing to play: a scene to look at, and to build yours from (ASSETS.md).\n205\timport { addModelColliders, buildModel, DEFAULT_TEXEL, Engine, hudSprite, PALETTE, TEXTURE_LIBRARY, textureMap, THREE, toonMaterial, withUrlOptions } from './pixel-engine.js';\n206\timport { MODELS } from './market-models.js';\n207\t\n208\tconst { Color, Mesh, PlaneGeometry, Vector3 } = THREE;\n209\t\n210\t// [id, position, options]: buildModel's options (params, palette, seed, light) and a turn in degrees\n211\tconst SCENE = [\n212\t  ['fruit-stall', [0, 0, -1.4]], // ours (market-models.js)\n213\t  ['market-stall', [-4, 0, 0.6], { turn: 90, palette: { cloth: 'blue' } }], // re-coloured\n214\t  ['lamp-post', [1, 0, 1.6], { light: { intensity: 10 } }], // it carries a light: brighter\n215\t  ['well', [3.8, 0, -2.2]],\n216\t  ['fence', [-0.5, 0, -3.4], { params: { length: 7 } }],\n217\t  ['barrel', [-1.8, 0, 1.2]],\n218\t  ['crate', [-1.1, 0, 1.7], { turn: 20 }],\n219\t  ['sack', [2.4, 0, 1], { params: { h: 0.6 } }],\n220\t  ['oak', [-3.2, 0, -3.4], { params: { h: 3 }, seed: 2 }],\n221\t];\n222\t\n223\tclass MarketCorner {\n224\t  name = 'Market Corner';\n225\t  placed = [];\n226\t  lights = [];\n227\t  target = new Vector3(-0.3, 1, -0.9);\n228\t\n229\t  setup(ctx) {\n230\t    const { scene, physics, engine } = ctx;\n231\t    engine.sun.intensity = 1.4; // dusk, so the lamp shows (the engine resets it for the next game)\n232\t    engine.ambient.color.setHex(PALETTE.navy);\n233\t    scene.background = new Color(PALETTE.night);\n234\t\n235\t    // The floor: a library surface on a plain mesh. A 32-texel tile at 8 texels a metre covers\n236\t    // 4 m, so the UVs count tiles (the map is cached and shared: never scale it).\n237\t    const tile = TEXTURE_LIBRARY.get('cobbles').size[0] / DEFAULT_TEXEL;\n238\t    const ground = new PlaneGeometry(16, 16).rotateX(-Math.PI / 2);\n239\t    ground.attributes.uv.array.forEach((v, i, uv) => (uv[i] = (v * 16) / tile));\n240\t    const floor = new Mesh(ground, toonMaterial(PALETTE.white, { map: textureMap('cobbles') }));\n241\t    floor.receiveShadow = true;\n242\t    scene.add(floor);\n243\t    physics.addStaticBox({ position: [0, -0.5, 0], halfExtents: [8, 0.5, 8] });\n244\t\n245\t    for (const [id, at, { turn = 0, ...options } = {}] of SCENE) {\n246\t      const m = buildModel(id, { ...options, catalog: MODELS });\n247\t      m.object.position.set(...at);\n248\t      m.object.rotation.y = (turn * Math.PI) / 180;\n249\t      m.object.updateMatrixWorld();\n250\t      scene.add(m.object);\n251\t      addModelColliders(physics, m, at, m.object.rotation.y);\n252\t      // a model's light is in its own space: place it with the model\n253\t      if (m.light) this.lights.push(ctx.lights.request({ ...m.light, position: m.object.localToWorld(new Vector3(...m.light.at)) }));\n254\t      this.placed.push(m);\n255\t    }\n256\t    this.icon = hudSprite('gold-icon');\n257\t  }\n258\t\n259\t  update(ctx) {\n260\t    ctx.hud.clear();\n261\t    ctx.hud.sprite(6, 6, this.icon.rows, this.icon.colors);\n262\t    ctx.hud.text(26, 10, 'MARKET CORNER', { color: 'sand' });\n263\t  }\n264\t\n265\t  cameraTarget() {\n266\t    return this.target;\n267\t  }\n268\t\n269\t  status() {\n270\t    return `market: ${this.placed.length} models, ${this.lights.length} light`;\n271\t  }\n272\t}\n273\t\n274\tawait Engine.start(new MarketCorner(), withUrlOptions({ container: document.getElementById('app'), camera: { preset: 'iso', zoom: 1.6 } }));\n275\t```\n276\t\n277\t## 7. Credits\n278\t\n279\t`CREDITS.md` says where everything the kit carries came from: the library and the built-in\n280\tmodels are the engine's own work, three.js and Rapier ship under their licences (their texts are\n281\tin it), and each pack's author and licence with every id it holds. Ship it with your game. A def\n282\tyou import or copy from someone else records it in `source: { url, licence, author }`; credit it\n283\tas its licence asks.\n284\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"66624378-a7bb-42a2-8c1d-fc63666b5654","uuid":"e613a30d-8aa5-4f56-bc83-b815947d338b","timestamp":"2026-11-05T23:21:49.872Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261105-162057__x4c6ec5/work/_research/engine/pixel-engine/ASSETS.md","content":"# Pixel Engine: models and textures\n\n> Pixel Engine **0.46.0**, build 2026-11-02 f75d4a6. Read `GUIDE.md` first. `LIBRARY.md` lists the built-in models and textures, `CREDITS.md` where they came from.\n\nFor an agent building a game on the kit; read `GUIDE.md` first. Objects and pixel-art textures\nare **plain data** you write, check and look at, then fix: a `ModelDef` of keyed primitives that\nthe engine turns into toon meshes, a `TextureDef` of layers it bakes into texels. No modelling\ntool and no image files. `LIBRARY.md` lists the built-in models and textures and the imported\npacks (`library.json` holds the same as data), `check.mjs --sheets` draws what you write, and `examples/market.html` is a scene built this way\n(section 6). Every name here comes from `./pixel-engine.js`; `API.md` has the signatures.\n\nContents: 1 The loop · 2 Models · 3 Textures · 4 Style rules · 5 In a game · 6 The example ·\n7 Credits\n\n## 1. The loop\n\n1. **Start from the library.** Search `LIBRARY.md` (by family, then each pack; id, size, params,\n   palette slots, tags, one row each, a model's variants like `rock-large-a` … `-f` sharing\n   one), filter `library.json`, or search in code:\n   `MODEL_LIBRARY.search('wooden chair')` ranks id words over tags over `about`, plurals and\n   parts of words included; `MODEL_LIBRARY.get('fence')` takes an id. Place library models as\n   they are, re-coloured or with other params, and `use` them inside your own: a stall with\n   crates is a few lines.\n2. **Write** your defs in a module of their own (`models.js`) that exports them; the game\n   imports it. Keep the engine start out of that module.\n3. **Check and look:** `node check.mjs --sheets models.js` draws a sheet of every def the module\n   exports (by name, in an array or object, or in a catalog: `check/sheets/<id>.png`; textures\n   `<id>.tex.png`) and prints its problems and warnings; it exits 1 on a problem. **Open the\n   PNGs.** `--sheets fence,cobbles` draws library ids: look at one before you place it.\n4. **Fix** the numbers the messages and the sheet point at, and run it again until there is no\n   problem and the game-scale previews read. Warnings name what won't read at 480×270: fix them\n   unless you mean it.\n5. **Look in the game:** `node check.mjs index.html --cameras`, then the PNGs: the real\n   renderer, with lights and looks.\n\nIn code, `checkModel(def, { catalog })` and `validateTexture(def)` return\n`{ problems, warnings }`, and `modelSheet(def, { catalog })` and `textureSheet(def)` return the\nsheet as RGBA pixels (`image`) with them.\n\n| sheet row | answers |\n| --- | --- |\n| turntable, 8 angles at one scale | Is the silhouette right from every side? |\n| front / side / top on a 0.1 m grid, with the size in metres | Are the parts where the numbers say? Do they touch? Is it the right size? |\n| game scale: an iso camera (yaw 45°, pitch 42°) at about 19 and 22 pixels a metre, one art pixel per pixel, ×4 | Does it read in a game? (The engine's iso camera at zoom 1 shows about 20.) |\n| palette, check | Which slots, which colours, which are unused; the problems and warnings, each naming the part and the fix |\n\nSheets are drawn in software (three shading bands, outlines) without point lights or looks: the\ngame is the truth.\n\n## 2. Models\n\n```js\nexport const crate = model({\n  id: 'crate',\n  about: 'Wooden crate with a frame and diagonal braces', // one line: find() searches it\n  tags: ['container', 'wood'],\n  palette: { wood: 'orange', band: 'red' }, // slots → the 16 palette names (GUIDE.md section 4)\n  parts: [\n    { box: [0.68, 0.68, 0.68], at: [0, 0.36, 0], c: 'wood' },\n    boxFrame({ size: [0.72, 0.72, 0.72], beam: 0.09, at: [0, 0.36, 0], c: 'band' }),\n    { mirror: [{ box: [0.8, 0.08, 0.04], at: [0, 0.36, 0.35], rot: [0, 0, 45], c: 'band' }], axis: 'z' },\n  ],\n});\n```\n\nMetres and degrees; +Y is up, +Z is the front, the floor is y = 0. Parts are keyed, never\npositional. **Every primitive fills its box `[w, h, d]`, centred on `at`**, turned by `rot` (XYZ,\nabout `at`), and round kinds stretch to it:\n\n- `box`; `cyl` (an n-sided prism, `seg` sides, default 6 to 10 by size; `top`: the top width ÷\n  the bottom width); `cone` (point up); `ball` (`half`: the upper dome); `lump` (a rock, `seed`);\n  `wedge` (a ramp, full height at the back, −Z); `torus` (a ring in XZ as thick as the box is tall).\n- Profile kinds use their own coordinates, placed at `at`: `lathe` ((radius, height) points\n  turned about Y), `slab` (an XY outline or SVG path, extruded `depth` along Z), `tube` (points\n  and a radius `r`, or a pair that tapers).\n- Every part takes `c` (its slot; default the first), `name`, `glow: true` (unlit: a flame's\n  heart, eyes), `tex` (a texture id, section 3), `seg` and `collide: false` (no box of its own\n  under `collider: 'parts'`: a doorway's mortar ring).\n\n**Composition:**\n\n- `{ group: [...], at, rot, scale, c }`: a frame whose `c` is its parts' default. `pivot: true`\n  with a `name` keeps it a movable Group (a door, a lid: `build.object.getObjectByName('lid')`).\n- `{ mirror: [...], axis: 'x' }`: the parts and their mirror image (`axis: 'z'` mirrors front to back).\n- `{ use: 'barrel', at, rot, scale, params, seed, palette: { theirs: 'ours' } }`: another model\n  by id. Its slots map onto yours through `palette`, else onto yours of the same name, else keep\n  their own colours.\n- `on: 'name'`: sit on the named part's top; `at` becomes an offset from there.\n- `params: { h: [default, min, max] }` with `parts: (p, rng) => [...]`: parametric (`p.h`) and\n  seeded (`rng.range(a, b)`). Helpers return parts: `around`, `rowOf`, `gridOf`, `scatterOf`,\n  `boxFrame`.\n- Model keys: `size` (declared bounds, checked), `anchors` (`{ grip: [x, y, z] }`), `light: { at,\n  c, intensity, radius, flicker }`, `collider` (`'box'`, `'cyl'`, `'hull'`, `'parts'` (a box per\n  part: doorways stay open) or `'none'`), `floats: true` (it hangs), `texel` (texels a metre for\n  `tex` parts, default 8).\n\n**Problems** (`check.mjs --sheets` exits 1; `buildModel` throws a `ModelError` on those it can't\nbuild): no `about` or `tags`; an undeclared slot or a colour that isn't a palette name; an unknown\n`use`, `on` or texture; a size that isn't positive; `seg` outside 3–64; NaN; an empty model; below\nthe floor (unless `floats`); over the declared `size`; params out of range; a part that both\nglows and takes a `tex`; a `texel` outside 2–64. **Warnings:** a part floating more than 1 cm from the\nrest; rods under 0.06 m; over the triangle budget (300 under 0.5 m across, 1,000 under 2 m, else\n3,000); over 8 materials; a part hidden inside another; coplanar faces of two colours; touching big\nmasses too close in lightness; one colour over 40% of the model in four or more parts; glow over\n15% of it. `checkModel` builds the defaults, every param at its min and at its max, and three\nseeds when the parts read `rng`.\n\n## 3. Textures\n\n```js\nexport const bricks = textureDef({\n  id: 'bricks',\n  about: 'Stone bricks in courses over dark mortar',\n  tags: ['stone', 'wall', 'surface'],\n  size: [16, 16], // texels; powers of two when it tiles\n  tile: true, // seamless: patterns wrap\n  palette: { mortar: 'night', stone: 'slate', stone2: 'mist' },\n  layers: [{ pattern: 'bricks', colors: ['mortar', 'stone', 'stone2'], w: 8, h: 4, seed: 2 }],\n});\n```\n\n**Layers** draw in order: `{ pixels: [...rows], legend: { k: 'slot' } }` (`.` or a space is clear;\n`at`, `mirror: 'x'` or `'y'`), `{ pattern, colors, seed, dither, ...params }` (over a ramp of\nslots, dark to light), `{ path: 'M … Z', fill, stroke }` (SVG in texel units) and `{ outline:\nslot }`. `frames: n` makes a strip of n frames, left to right.\n\n**Patterns:** `noise bricks tiles planks cobbles stripes checker scatter cracks gradient`.\nParameters: noise `scale`, `octaves`; bricks and tiles `w`, `h`, `mortar`, `offset`, `bevel`;\nplanks `w`, `gap`, `length`, `grain`; cobbles `cells`, `gap`; stripes `width`, `dir`; checker\n`cell`; scatter `count`, `size`, `shape`; cracks `count`, `length`; gradient `dir`. A problem\nnames the range of a bad one.\n\n**Density:** surfaces at 8 texels a metre (`DEFAULT_TEXEL`); denser ones shimmer as the camera\nzooms. A 16-texel tile covers 2 m, so draw a brick of 8 × 4 texels for 1 × 0.5 m. **Warnings:** a\nseam across the wrap, salt and pepper (over 15% single texels), a tiling size that isn't a power\nof two, unused slots.\n\n## 4. Style rules\n\n- **Scale:** the hero is 1.6 m (1.75 m with the cap); a door 2.2 m, a table 0.75 m, a chair seat 0.45 m.\n- **Silhouette first.** The iso camera sees the top and two sides: big masses must read; put\n  detail on the top and the front.\n- **Thickness:** 0.06 m at least; 0.12 m or more for parts that carry the silhouette.\n- **Colour separates parts:** touching parts take different colours or shades (planks and their\n  frame, stones and mortar), up to 8, one an accent; touching big masses differ in lightness.\n- **Glow is flat:** keep it for small hot spots. For fire `use: 'flame'`, and never turn it about\n  Y: its tongues face the camera. Below `h` 0.5 its 2–4 tongues read as a dark smudge: use\n  `tongues: 1` for small fires.\n- **Low poly:** 6 to 10 sides on round kinds, no bevels under 2 cm.\n- **Grounded:** the base on y = 0, nothing floating unless `floats`.\n- **Compose before you create:** `use` a library model before you write a new crate.\n\n## 5. In a game\n\n- `buildModel(idOrDef, { params, seed, palette, light, catalog, textures })` returns `object`\n  (one merged mesh per material; pivots are named Groups), `bounds`, `anchors`, `colliders`\n  (data: `addModelColliders(physics, build, at, yaw)`), `light` (in model space: place it with\n  the model, as the example does) and `report`. Builds whose parts don't read `rng` are cached and\n  share geometry: place as many as you like.\n- **Your own models:** a `ModelCatalog` of the library and yours, passed as `catalog` (or\n  `MODEL_LIBRARY.add(def)`); your own textures likewise in a `TextureCatalog`, passed as `textures`.\n- **Textures:** `toonMaterial(PALETTE.white, { map: textureMap('bricks') })` on any mesh (the\n  map is cached and shared: scale UVs, never the map); `new SpriteBatch(spriteSheet('coin-spin'),\n  { capacity: 64 })`; `hudSprite('gem-icon')` gives rows and colours for `ctx.hud.sprite`.\n- **Packs** (`packs/`, listed in `packs/index.json` and LIBRARY.md): kits by other artists,\n  imported into this format and palette. `const kit = await loadPack('packs/<id>.json',\n  { textures: TEXTURE_LIBRARY })`, then `buildModel('<id>/<name>', { catalog: kit })`; its\n  textures then work by id like the library's. `kit.search('tree')` searches the pack. To vary\n  one of its models, load the pack into your catalog (`loadPack(url, { into: MODEL_LIBRARY })`)\n  and add a def there that `use`s it with a `scale` or a `palette`: nothing is copied.\n\n## 6. The example\n\nMarket Corner, the kit's `examples/market.html`: library models (one re-coloured, three with\nparams, the lamp post's light made brighter), a cobbled floor from `textureMap`, and a stall of\nthe game's own in a module of its own. `node check.mjs examples/market.html` plays it;\n`node check.mjs --sheets examples/market-models.js` draws its stall.\n\n```js market-models\n// Market Corner's own models: plain data in a module of their own, so `node check.mjs --sheets\n// examples/market-models.js` can draw them.\nimport { model, MODEL_LIBRARY, ModelCatalog } from './pixel-engine.js';\n\nexport const fruitStall = model({\n  id: 'fruit-stall',\n  about: 'The market stall under a green awning, two baskets of fruit on its counter',\n  tags: ['town', 'market'],\n  palette: { wood: 'orange', cloth: 'green', cloth2: 'white', top: 'sand', basket: 'red', fruit: 'orange', fruit2: 'lime' },\n  parts: [\n    { use: 'market-stall' }, // its slots take ours of the same name: the awning turns green\n    ...[-0.45, 0.4].flatMap((x, i) => [\n      { name: `basket${i}`, cyl: [0.56, 0.14, 0.4], at: [x, 1.01, 0.05], top: 1.25, c: 'basket' },\n      ...[-0.17, 0, 0.17].map((dx, k) => ({ ball: [0.18, 0.18, 0.18], on: `basket${i}`, at: [dx, 0, 0], c: (i + k) % 2 ? 'fruit2' : 'fruit' })),\n    ]),\n  ],\n});\n\n// Where the game's ids resolve: the library and ours (buildModel's `catalog`).\nexport const MODELS = new ModelCatalog([...MODEL_LIBRARY.all(), fruitStall]);\n```\n\n```js market\n// Market Corner: library models and the game's own fruit stall on a cobbled square at dusk.\n// Nothing to play: a scene to look at, and to build yours from (ASSETS.md).\nimport { addModelColliders, buildModel, DEFAULT_TEXEL, Engine, hudSprite, PALETTE, TEXTURE_LIBRARY, textureMap, THREE, toonMaterial, withUrlOptions } from './pixel-engine.js';\nimport { MODELS } from './market-models.js';\n\nconst { Color, Mesh, PlaneGeometry, Vector3 } = THREE;\n\n// [id, position, options]: buildModel's options (params, palette, seed, light) and a turn in degrees\nconst SCENE = [\n  ['fruit-stall', [0, 0, -1.4]], // ours (market-models.js)\n  ['market-stall', [-4, 0, 0.6], { turn: 90, palette: { cloth: 'blue' } }], // re-coloured\n  ['lamp-post', [1, 0, 1.6], { light: { intensity: 10 } }], // it carries a light: brighter\n  ['well', [3.8, 0, -2.2]],\n  ['fence', [-0.5, 0, -3.4], { params: { length: 7 } }],\n  ['barrel', [-1.8, 0, 1.2]],\n  ['crate', [-1.1, 0, 1.7], { turn: 20 }],\n  ['sack', [2.4, 0, 1], { params: { h: 0.6 } }],\n  ['oak', [-3.2, 0, -3.4], { params: { h: 3 }, seed: 2 }],\n];\n\nclass MarketCorner {\n  name = 'Market Corner';\n  placed = [];\n  lights = [];\n  target = new Vector3(-0.3, 1, -0.9);\n\n  setup(ctx) {\n    const { scene, physics, engine } = ctx;\n    engine.sun.intensity = 1.4; // dusk, so the lamp shows (the engine resets it for the next game)\n    engine.ambient.color.setHex(PALETTE.navy);\n    scene.background = new Color(PALETTE.night);\n\n    // The floor: a library surface on a plain mesh. A 32-texel tile at 8 texels a metre covers\n    // 4 m, so the UVs count tiles (the map is cached and shared: never scale it).\n    const tile = TEXTURE_LIBRARY.get('cobbles').size[0] / DEFAULT_TEXEL;\n    const ground = new PlaneGeometry(16, 16).rotateX(-Math.PI / 2);\n    ground.attributes.uv.array.forEach((v, i, uv) => (uv[i] = (v * 16) / tile));\n    const floor = new Mesh(ground, toonMaterial(PALETTE.white, { map: textureMap('cobbles') }));\n    floor.receiveShadow = true;\n    scene.add(floor);\n    physics.addStaticBox({ position: [0, -0.5, 0], halfExtents: [8, 0.5, 8] });\n\n    for (const [id, at, { turn = 0, ...options } = {}] of SCENE) {\n      const m = buildModel(id, { ...options, catalog: MODELS });\n      m.object.position.set(...at);\n      m.object.rotation.y = (turn * Math.PI) / 180;\n      m.object.updateMatrixWorld();\n      scene.add(m.object);\n      addModelColliders(physics, m, at, m.object.rotation.y);\n      // a model's light is in its own space: place it with the model\n      if (m.light) this.lights.push(ctx.lights.request({ ...m.light, position: m.object.localToWorld(new Vector3(...m.light.at)) }));\n      this.placed.push(m);\n    }\n    this.icon = hudSprite('gold-icon');\n  }\n\n  update(ctx) {\n    ctx.hud.clear();\n    ctx.hud.sprite(6, 6, this.icon.rows, this.icon.colors);\n    ctx.hud.text(26, 10, 'MARKET CORNER', { color: 'sand' });\n  }\n\n  cameraTarget() {\n    return this.target;\n  }\n\n  status() {\n    return `market: ${this.placed.length} models, ${this.lights.length} light`;\n  }\n}\n\nawait Engine.start(new MarketCorner(), withUrlOptions({ container: document.getElementById('app'), camera: { preset: 'iso', zoom: 1.6 } }));\n```\n\n## 7. Credits\n\n`CREDITS.md` says where everything the kit carries came from: the library and the built-in\nmodels are the engine's own work, three.js and Rapier ship under their licences (their texts are\nin it), and each pack's author and licence with every id it holds. Ship it with your game. A def\nyou import or copy from someone else records it in `source: { url, licence, author }`; credit it\nas its licence asks.\n","numLines":284,"startLine":1,"totalLines":284}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01UT6jnyMXbsqPMLiZi2HA19","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgke6HSAkMg7YsQSMohXb","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Do6L3AzahyqRtrEuujJdvd","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261105-162057__x4c6ec5/work/_research/engine/pixel-engine/API.md","offset":2020,"limit":60},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":13039,"cache_read_input_tokens":106108,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":13039},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"66624378-a7bb-42a2-8c1d-fc63666b5654","uuid":"50397a02-7d07-4916-8da0-3a7ad76a28fb","timestamp":"2026-11-05T23:21:52.427Z","request_id":"req_011Cgke6HCHNhcqDRLmSZmXm","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01Do6L3AzahyqRtrEuujJdvd":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261105-162057__x4c6ec5/work/_research/engine/pixel-engine/API.md","offset":2020,"limit":60}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Do6L3AzahyqRtrEuujJdvd","type":"tool_result","content":"2020\t```\n2021\t\n2022\tTrigger volume shape (half extents / radius in world units).\n2023\t\n2024\t### class Vehicle\n2025\t\n2026\t- `new Vehicle(physics: Physics, o: VehicleOptions)`\n2027\t- `readonly body: RAPIER.RigidBody`\n2028\t- `readonly controller: RAPIER.DynamicRayCastVehicleController`\n2029\t- `readonly o: Required<Omit<VehicleOptions, 'at' | 'yaw'>>`\n2030\t- `readonly mounts: [number, number, number][]`: Wheels' chassis-space mount points (front left, front right, rear left, rear right).\n2031\t- `steering: number`: Smoothed steering (-1..1).\n2032\t- `slip: number`: How fast the car slides sideways (m/s), and the angle between where it points and where it goes (radians): drifting when high.\n2033\t- `slipAngle: number`\n2034\t- `setSuspension(stiffness: number): void`: Spring stiffness of every wheel's suspension (live).\n2035\t- `get speed: number`: Speed along the car's heading (m/s, negative reversing). Not Rapier's `currentVehicleSpeed`, which counts the up-and-down too (the suspension holding the body up against gravity every step reads as a crawl when parked).\n2036\t- `drive(input: DriveInput, dt: number): void`: One fixed step of driving.\n2037\t- `wheelPose(i: number, pos: Vector3, rot: Quaternion, local = false): void`: Where wheel `i` is drawn: on its suspension, turned by steering and spin. In the world, or with `local` in the chassis' own space (a wheel mesh parented to a chassis mesh that a physics binding draws between steps).\n2038\t- `get yaw: number`: The chassis' heading (radians about Y; 0 drives toward +Z).\n2039\t- `reset(at: readonly [number, number, number], yaw = 0): void`: Put it back on its wheels at `at` (a reset after a roll).\n2040\t- `dispose(): void`\n2041\t\n2042\t### interface VehicleOptions\n2043\t\n2044\t- `at: readonly [number, number, number]`\n2045\t- `yaw?: number`: Heading (radians about Y; 0 drives toward +Z).\n2046\t- `size?: readonly [number, number, number]`: Chassis half extents (m). Default [0.9, 0.35, 1.8].\n2047\t- `mass?: number`: Chassis mass in world units. Default 8.\n2048\t- `wheelRadius?: number`\n2049\t- `suspension?: number`: Suspension rest length and stiffness.\n2050\t- `stiffness?: number`\n2051\t- `power?: number`: Forward drive force per driven wheel at full throttle. Default 30.\n2052\t- `topSpeed?: number`: The engine gives out toward this speed (m/s). Default 16.\n2053\t- `steer?: number`: Most steering angle (radians). Default 0.55.\n2054\t- `grip?: number`: Grip (friction slip): how much sideways force the tyres hold before sliding. Default 2.2.\n2055\t- `driftYaw?: number`: Most spin (rad/s) the handbrake's drift push builds up. Default 2.4; 0 turns the assist off.\n2056\t\n2057\t### class VerletBody\n2058\t\n2059\t- `new VerletBody(count: number, o: VerletOptions = {})`\n2060\t- `readonly count: number`\n2061\t- `readonly pos: Float32Array`: Positions, xyz per particle.\n2062\t- `readonly prev: Float32Array`: Last step's positions (velocity = pos − prev).\n2063\t- `readonly invMass: Float32Array`: 0 for pinned particles.\n2064\t- `readonly pinAt: Float32Array`: Where pinned particles are held (move them to drag a cloth by its corner).\n2065\t- `ea: Uint32Array<ArrayBuffer>`: Distance constraints: particle pairs, rest lengths and stiffness 0..1.\n2066\t- `eb: Uint32Array<ArrayBuffer>`\n2067\t- `rest: Float32Array<ArrayBuffer>`\n2068\t- `stiff: Float32Array<ArrayBuffer>`\n2069\t- `tris: Uint32Array<ArrayBuffer>`: Triangles (cloth and blobs): wind drag, volume and drawing.\n2070\t- `gravity: [number, number, number]`\n2071\t- `wind: [number, number, number]`: Wind velocity (m/s): drags triangles (by their facing) or particles (ropes).\n2072\t- `gust: number`: How much the wind varies in time and space (0..1).\n2073\t- `drag: number`: How hard the wind pushes. Cloth: acceleration per (m/s)² of wind hitting it squarely (light cloth: high; canvas: low). Ropes: per m/s of speed relative to the wind.\n2074\t- `skin: number`: Cloth: drag along its surface, as a share of `drag` (it streams a flag out).\n2075\t- `iterations: number`\n2076\t- `damping: number`\n2077\t- `floor: number | null`: A floor plane every particle stays above (null: none).\n2078\t- `friction: number`: Ground friction: how much sideways motion a particle on the floor keeps.\n2079\t- `readonly spheres: Sphere[]`"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"66624378-a7bb-42a2-8c1d-fc63666b5654","uuid":"39c43ad8-bc52-4730-9312-31e2806bb743","timestamp":"2026-11-05T23:21:52.439Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261105-162057__x4c6ec5/work/_research/engine/pixel-engine/API.md","content":"```\n\nTrigger volume shape (half extents / radius in world units).\n\n### class Vehicle\n\n- `new Vehicle(physics: Physics, o: VehicleOptions)`\n- `readonly body: RAPIER.RigidBody`\n- `readonly controller: RAPIER.DynamicRayCastVehicleController`\n- `readonly o: Required<Omit<VehicleOptions, 'at' | 'yaw'>>`\n- `readonly mounts: [number, number, number][]`: Wheels' chassis-space mount points (front left, front right, rear left, rear right).\n- `steering: number`: Smoothed steering (-1..1).\n- `slip: number`: How fast the car slides sideways (m/s), and the angle between where it points and where it goes (radians): drifting when high.\n- `slipAngle: number`\n- `setSuspension(stiffness: number): void`: Spring stiffness of every wheel's suspension (live).\n- `get speed: number`: Speed along the car's heading (m/s, negative reversing). Not Rapier's `currentVehicleSpeed`, which counts the up-and-down too (the suspension holding the body up against gravity every step reads as a crawl when parked).\n- `drive(input: DriveInput, dt: number): void`: One fixed step of driving.\n- `wheelPose(i: number, pos: Vector3, rot: Quaternion, local = false): void`: Where wheel `i` is drawn: on its suspension, turned by steering and spin. In the world, or with `local` in the chassis' own space (a wheel mesh parented to a chassis mesh that a physics binding draws between steps).\n- `get yaw: number`: The chassis' heading (radians about Y; 0 drives toward +Z).\n- `reset(at: readonly [number, number, number], yaw = 0): void`: Put it back on its wheels at `at` (a reset after a roll).\n- `dispose(): void`\n\n### interface VehicleOptions\n\n- `at: readonly [number, number, number]`\n- `yaw?: number`: Heading (radians about Y; 0 drives toward +Z).\n- `size?: readonly [number, number, number]`: Chassis half extents (m). Default [0.9, 0.35, 1.8].\n- `mass?: number`: Chassis mass in world units. Default 8.\n- `wheelRadius?: number`\n- `suspension?: number`: Suspension rest length and stiffness.\n- `stiffness?: number`\n- `power?: number`: Forward drive force per driven wheel at full throttle. Default 30.\n- `topSpeed?: number`: The engine gives out toward this speed (m/s). Default 16.\n- `steer?: number`: Most steering angle (radians). Default 0.55.\n- `grip?: number`: Grip (friction slip): how much sideways force the tyres hold before sliding. Default 2.2.\n- `driftYaw?: number`: Most spin (rad/s) the handbrake's drift push builds up. Default 2.4; 0 turns the assist off.\n\n### class VerletBody\n\n- `new VerletBody(count: number, o: VerletOptions = {})`\n- `readonly count: number`\n- `readonly pos: Float32Array`: Positions, xyz per particle.\n- `readonly prev: Float32Array`: Last step's positions (velocity = pos − prev).\n- `readonly invMass: Float32Array`: 0 for pinned particles.\n- `readonly pinAt: Float32Array`: Where pinned particles are held (move them to drag a cloth by its corner).\n- `ea: Uint32Array<ArrayBuffer>`: Distance constraints: particle pairs, rest lengths and stiffness 0..1.\n- `eb: Uint32Array<ArrayBuffer>`\n- `rest: Float32Array<ArrayBuffer>`\n- `stiff: Float32Array<ArrayBuffer>`\n- `tris: Uint32Array<ArrayBuffer>`: Triangles (cloth and blobs): wind drag, volume and drawing.\n- `gravity: [number, number, number]`\n- `wind: [number, number, number]`: Wind velocity (m/s): drags triangles (by their facing) or particles (ropes).\n- `gust: number`: How much the wind varies in time and space (0..1).\n- `drag: number`: How hard the wind pushes. Cloth: acceleration per (m/s)² of wind hitting it squarely (light cloth: high; canvas: low). Ropes: per m/s of speed relative to the wind.\n- `skin: number`: Cloth: drag along its surface, as a share of `drag` (it streams a flag out).\n- `iterations: number`\n- `damping: number`\n- `floor: number | null`: A floor plane every particle stays above (null: none).\n- `friction: number`: Ground friction: how much sideways motion a particle on the floor keeps.\n- `readonly spheres: Sphere[]`","numLines":60,"startLine":2020,"totalLines":5144}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01Do6L3AzahyqRtrEuujJdvd","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgke6HSAkMg7YsQSMohXb","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01WooGpTHY6VpQJUnKDcgPgU","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261105-162057__x4c6ec5/work/_research/engine/pixel-engine/API.md","offset":2262,"limit":120},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":13039,"cache_read_input_tokens":106108,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":13039},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"66624378-a7bb-42a2-8c1d-fc63666b5654","uuid":"53eb0f3e-7290-42df-975d-4abd5e21d200","timestamp":"2026-11-05T23:21:54.368Z","request_id":"req_011Cgke6HCHNhcqDRLmSZmXm","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01WooGpTHY6VpQJUnKDcgPgU":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261105-162057__x4c6ec5/work/_research/engine/pixel-engine/API.md","offset":2262,"limit":120}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WooGpTHY6VpQJUnKDcgPgU","type":"tool_result","content":"2262\t```ts\n2263\tconst DEFAULT_TOUCH_BUTTONS: readonly TouchButton[]\n2264\t```\n2265\t\n2266\tDefault buttons for PlatformerCharacter (see character/controls.ts KEYMAP).\n2267\t\n2268\t### GAMEPAD_BUTTONS\n2269\t\n2270\t```ts\n2271\tconst GAMEPAD_BUTTONS: Readonly<Record<number, string>>\n2272\t```\n2273\t\n2274\tStandard-mapping gamepad buttons → the key codes they press (see character/controls.ts\n2275\tKEYMAP). Change `input.gamepadButtons` to remap. Indices follow the W3C \"standard\" layout:\n2276\t0 A · 1 B · 2 X · 3 Y · 4 LB · 5 RB · 6 LT · 7 RT · 8 Back · 9 Start · 12–15 d-pad.\n2277\t\n2278\t### interface GamepadLike\n2279\t\n2280\tThe parts of the Gamepad API that Input reads (tests can pass plain objects).\n2281\t\n2282\t- `readonly connected?: boolean`\n2283\t- `readonly mapping?: string`\n2284\t- `readonly axes: readonly number[]`\n2285\t- `readonly buttons: readonly { readonly pressed: boolean; readonly value?: number }[]`\n2286\t\n2287\t### class Input\n2288\t\n2289\tKeyboard + pointer + gamepad input.\n2290\t- Keys: held state, per-frame presses (`wasPressed`) and queued presses for fixed-step\n2291\t  code (`consumePress`). Queued presses expire after `pressWindow` s of game time.\n2292\t- Gamepads (standard mapping): the left stick feeds `moveAxis()` (radial deadzone), the\n2293\t  right stick feeds the pointer delta (camera look), buttons press key codes\n2294\t  (`gamepadButtons`). Polled once per frame by the engine (`beginFrame`).\n2295\t- Pointer: accumulated movement per frame (while a button is held or the pointer is\n2296\t  locked), wheel delta per frame, optional pointer lock (first-person). Mouse buttons on\n2297\t  the canvas are keys too (`MOUSE_BUTTONS`: `MouseLeft`, `MouseRight`, `MouseMiddle`), and a\n2298\t  finger is `Touch` (`TOUCH_KEY`), held and pressed like any other, so a click shorter than\n2299\t  a frame is not lost.\n2300\t- Commands (`send`): data from outside the canvas, delivered to the game…\n2301\t\n2302\t- `new Input(target: Window | null = window)`\n2303\t- `readonly mouseDelta: { x: number; y: number; }`: Pointer movement this frame, in CSS pixels (drag or pointer lock).\n2304\t- `readonly pointer: { x: number; y: number; over: boolean; touch: boolean; }`: Where the pointer last was over the canvas, in normalised device coordinates (−1..1, y up: `camera.rayAt(pointer.x, pointer.y, …)` turns it into a ray), and whether it is over it now. Under pointer lock it is the screen's centre (the crosshair).\n2305\t- `wheel: number`: Wheel ticks this frame (+1 = scroll down / zoom out).\n2306\t- `wantsPointerLock: boolean`: When true, clicking the canvas requests pointer lock (first-person / free camera).\n2307\t- `readonly analog: { x: number; y: number; }`: Touch joystick, -1..1 per axis; overrides keys when active.\n2308\t- `readonly padAxis: { x: number; y: number; }`: Gamepad left stick after the deadzone, -1..1 (y = forward).\n2309\t- `gamepadConnected: boolean`: True while any gamepad is connected.\n2310\t- `readonly preventKeys: Set<string>`: Extra key codes whose browser default is blocked (arrows and Space always are): a game's own F1 / F2 / Tab panels, so the browser's help page or focus change doesn't fire.\n2311\t- `gamepadButtons: Record<number, string>`: Gamepad button index → key code (defaults: GAMEPAD_BUTTONS).\n2312\t- `gamepadDeadzone: number`: Stick deadzone (radial, 0..1).\n2313\t- `gamepadLookSpeed: number`: Right stick look speed: pointer pixels per second at full tilt.\n2314\t- `now: number`: Game time (s) of the current frame; set by the engine through `beginFrame`.\n2315\t- `get playing: boolean`: True while a replay drives input (`engine.replay()`): keyboard, pointer and wheel events, `setKey` / `setPointer` / `setButtons` / `addPointer` (touch buttons, scripts) and `send` are ignored, and `playBefore` / `playAfter` set everything.\n2316\t- `pressWindow: number`: Queued presses older than this many seconds of game time are dropped.\n2317\t- `attachPointer(el: HTMLElement): void`: Listen for pointer input on the game canvas. Only pointers that went down on the canvas drive the camera (so a touch joystick doesn't orbit it). Two canvas touches pinch to zoom (emitted as wheel ticks).\n2318\t- `dispose(): void`: Remove every listener this Input added (window and canvas).\n2319\t- `release(): void`: Let go of everything held (keys, gamepad buttons, sticks, pointer buttons); queued presses stay.\n2320\t- `reset(): void`: Forget held keys, presses, sticks and pointer movement (e.g. when a level loads).\n2321\t- `beginFrame(now: number, dt: number, pads?: readonly (GamepadLike | null)[]): void`: Engine: begin a frame at game time `now` (seconds). Polls gamepads and drops expired queued presses. `pads` defaults to `navigator.getGamepads()`; tests can pass fakes.\n2322\t- `get pointerLocked: boolean`\n2323\t- `isDown(...codes: string[]): boolean`\n2324\t- `anyDown(codes: readonly string[]): boolean`: `isDown` for a list of codes, without building a rest-argument array (fixed-step code).\n2325\t- `wasPressed(...codes: string[]): boolean`: True once per physical key press (valid for the current render frame).\n2326\t- `consumePress(...codes: string[]): boolean`: True once per physical key press, kept until consumed or until it is older than `pressWindow` (150 ms of game time). Use from fixed-step code (which may run zero or several times per frame) so no press is dropped, while a stale press (made during a pause, a l…\n2327\t- `consumeAny(codes: readonly string[]): boolean`: `consumePress` for a list of codes, without building a rest-argument array.\n2328\t- `state(): InputState`: Held keys, queued presses and the pointer as data (`engine.snapshot()`), between frames.\n2329\t- `setState(s: InputState, now: number): void`: Back to `state()` at game time `now` (a restore): held keys are those the snapshot held, live ones let go.\n2330\t- `get queuedPresses: string[]`: Presses waiting to be consumed (tooling / tests).\n2331\t- `clearQueued(...codes: string[]): void`: Drop queued presses (e.g. when a menu or mode swallows input).\n2332\t- `moveAxis(): { x: number; y: number }`: -1..1 on each axis (x = right, y = forward): the touch joystick or a gamepad's left stick (whichever is tilted further), else WASD / arrow keys / d-pad.\n2333\t- `endFrame(): void`: Call at the end of each rendered frame.\n2334\t- `setKey(code: string, down: boolean): void`: Test hook: simulate a key being held (true) or released (false).\n2335\t- `setPointer(x: number, y: number, down?: boolean): void`: Test hook: put the pointer at (x, y) in normalised device coordinates, pressed or not.\n2336\t- `setButtons(bits: number): void`: Test hook: hold mouse buttons (`PointerEvent.buttons` bits: 1 left, 2 right, 4 middle) as the canvas would.\n2337\t- `get mouseButtons: number`: Buttons held, as `PointerEvent.buttons` bits (1 left, 2 right, 4 middle; a finger is left): the same keys as `isDown('MouseLeft')`.\n2338\t- `get buttonEvents: readonly ButtonEvent[]`: This frame's pointer button presses and releases, in order, each with where the pointer was: a drag shorter than a frame (a script's) still starts where it started and ends where it ended. Recorded with the frame. UI that acts on clicks reads these; play read…\n2339\t- `send(command: unknown): void`: Hand the game data from outside the canvas: a DOM widget's value, a file the player picked, a save read from storage, anything a game would otherwise take straight from the page. It arrives in `commands` at the next frame the game runs, and the recording keep…\n2340\t- `get commands: readonly unknown[]`: This frame's commands (`send`), oldest first; empty on most frames. They last one rendered frame: read them in `update` (`fixedUpdate` runs zero or more times a frame).\n2341\t- `deliver(): void`: Engine: a live frame begins to run: what was sent since the last one becomes its `commands`.\n2342\t- `addPointer(dx: number, dy: number, wheel = 0): void`: Test hook: inject pointer movement / wheel for the next frame.\n2343\t- `snapshot(dt: number, last?: SnapshotMemory): InputFrame`: This frame's input as data (after `beginFrame`), for a recording. `last` is the frame before: unchanged held keys, pointer and gamepad connection are left out.\n2344\t- `playBefore(f: InputFrame): void`: Replay, before the engine's `beginFrame`: the frame's held keys and presses (presses queue at the time they would have arrived, the frame before's game time).\n2345\t- `playAfter(f: InputFrame): void`: Replay, after `beginFrame` (which polled no gamepads): sticks, pointer, wheel, gamepad connection.\n2346\t\n2347\t### interface InputFrame\n2348\t\n2349\tWhat the game could read from input in one frame (a recorded frame, see src/engine/replay.ts).\n2350\tOptional fields are left out when empty or zero; `held` and `pointer` also when unchanged\n2351\tfrom the frame before.\n2352\t\n2353\t- `dt: number`: Real seconds this frame lasted (game time follows from it).\n2354\t- `held?: string[]`: Keys held, keyboard and gamepad buttons together, sorted.\n2355\t- `pressed?: string[]`: Keys pressed since the frame before (a tap can be pressed and not held).\n2356\t- `analog?: [number, number]`: The touch joystick and the gamepad's left stick (after its deadzone).\n2357\t- `pad?: [number, number]`\n2358\t- `delta?: [number, number]`: Pointer movement (CSS pixels) and wheel ticks this frame.\n2359\t- `wheel?: number`\n2360\t- `pointer?: [number, number, number, number]`: Pointer position (normalised device coordinates), over the canvas (1/0), buttons (`mouseButtons`: what `held` says).\n2361\t- `gamepad?: boolean`: Whether a gamepad is connected (`gamepadConnected`), when that changes.\n2362\t- `touch?: boolean`: Whether the pointer is a touch (`pointer.touch`), when that changes.\n2363\t- `clicks?: [string, 0 | 1, number, number][]`: Pointer buttons pressed (1) and let go (0) this frame, in order, each where the pointer was (`buttonEvents`).\n2364\t- `commands?: unknown[]`: Data from outside the canvas delivered this frame (`send()`): a DOM widget's value, a save read from storage.\n2365\t- `sync?: true`: Ran in the same task as the frame before (frames of one `step(n)` call): no promise callbacks ran between them. A replay waits for them before every other frame.\n2366\t\n2367\t### interface InputState\n2368\t\n2369\tWhat input a later frame still reads, as data (`engine.snapshot()`): keys held, presses not yet used and how old they are, the pointer.\n2370\t\n2371\t- `readonly held: readonly string[]`\n2372\t- `readonly queued: readonly (readonly [string, number])[]`: Queued presses: [key, seconds of game time since it was pressed].\n2373\t- `readonly pointer: readonly [number, number, number, number]`\n2374\t- `readonly gamepad: boolean`\n2375\t- `readonly outbox: readonly unknown[]`: Data sent (`send`) and not yet delivered: the next frame that runs delivers it.\n2376\t\n2377\t### MOUSE_BUTTONS\n2378\t\n2379\t```ts\n2380\tconst MOUSE_BUTTONS: readonly (readonly [code: string, bit: number])[]\n2381\t```"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"66624378-a7bb-42a2-8c1d-fc63666b5654","uuid":"79138a4f-9ee4-4f76-a2bb-e13ed1d69fec","timestamp":"2026-11-05T23:21:54.373Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261105-162057__x4c6ec5/work/_research/engine/pixel-engine/API.md","content":"```ts\nconst DEFAULT_TOUCH_BUTTONS: readonly TouchButton[]\n```\n\nDefault buttons for PlatformerCharacter (see character/controls.ts KEYMAP).\n\n### GAMEPAD_BUTTONS\n\n```ts\nconst GAMEPAD_BUTTONS: Readonly<Record<number, string>>\n```\n\nStandard-mapping gamepad buttons → the key codes they press (see character/controls.ts\nKEYMAP). Change `input.gamepadButtons` to remap. Indices follow the W3C \"standard\" layout:\n0 A · 1 B · 2 X · 3 Y · 4 LB · 5 RB · 6 LT · 7 RT · 8 Back · 9 Start · 12–15 d-pad.\n\n### interface GamepadLike\n\nThe parts of the Gamepad API that Input reads (tests can pass plain objects).\n\n- `readonly connected?: boolean`\n- `readonly mapping?: string`\n- `readonly axes: readonly number[]`\n- `readonly buttons: readonly { readonly pressed: boolean; readonly value?: number }[]`\n\n### class Input\n\nKeyboard + pointer + gamepad input.\n- Keys: held state, per-frame presses (`wasPressed`) and queued presses for fixed-step\n  code (`consumePress`). Queued presses expire after `pressWindow` s of game time.\n- Gamepads (standard mapping): the left stick feeds `moveAxis()` (radial deadzone), the\n  right stick feeds the pointer delta (camera look), buttons press key codes\n  (`gamepadButtons`). Polled once per frame by the engine (`beginFrame`).\n- Pointer: accumulated movement per frame (while a button is held or the pointer is\n  locked), wheel delta per frame, optional pointer lock (first-person). Mouse buttons on\n  the canvas are keys too (`MOUSE_BUTTONS`: `MouseLeft`, `MouseRight`, `MouseMiddle`), and a\n  finger is `Touch` (`TOUCH_KEY`), held and pressed like any other, so a click shorter than\n  a frame is not lost.\n- Commands (`send`): data from outside the canvas, delivered to the game…\n\n- `new Input(target: Window | null = window)`\n- `readonly mouseDelta: { x: number; y: number; }`: Pointer movement this frame, in CSS pixels (drag or pointer lock).\n- `readonly pointer: { x: number; y: number; over: boolean; touch: boolean; }`: Where the pointer last was over the canvas, in normalised device coordinates (−1..1, y up: `camera.rayAt(pointer.x, pointer.y, …)` turns it into a ray), and whether it is over it now. Under pointer lock it is the screen's centre (the crosshair).\n- `wheel: number`: Wheel ticks this frame (+1 = scroll down / zoom out).\n- `wantsPointerLock: boolean`: When true, clicking the canvas requests pointer lock (first-person / free camera).\n- `readonly analog: { x: number; y: number; }`: Touch joystick, -1..1 per axis; overrides keys when active.\n- `readonly padAxis: { x: number; y: number; }`: Gamepad left stick after the deadzone, -1..1 (y = forward).\n- `gamepadConnected: boolean`: True while any gamepad is connected.\n- `readonly preventKeys: Set<string>`: Extra key codes whose browser default is blocked (arrows and Space always are): a game's own F1 / F2 / Tab panels, so the browser's help page or focus change doesn't fire.\n- `gamepadButtons: Record<number, string>`: Gamepad button index → key code (defaults: GAMEPAD_BUTTONS).\n- `gamepadDeadzone: number`: Stick deadzone (radial, 0..1).\n- `gamepadLookSpeed: number`: Right stick look speed: pointer pixels per second at full tilt.\n- `now: number`: Game time (s) of the current frame; set by the engine through `beginFrame`.\n- `get playing: boolean`: True while a replay drives input (`engine.replay()`): keyboard, pointer and wheel events, `setKey` / `setPointer` / `setButtons` / `addPointer` (touch buttons, scripts) and `send` are ignored, and `playBefore` / `playAfter` set everything.\n- `pressWindow: number`: Queued presses older than this many seconds of game time are dropped.\n- `attachPointer(el: HTMLElement): void`: Listen for pointer input on the game canvas. Only pointers that went down on the canvas drive the camera (so a touch joystick doesn't orbit it). Two canvas touches pinch to zoom (emitted as wheel ticks).\n- `dispose(): void`: Remove every listener this Input added (window and canvas).\n- `release(): void`: Let go of everything held (keys, gamepad buttons, sticks, pointer buttons); queued presses stay.\n- `reset(): void`: Forget held keys, presses, sticks and pointer movement (e.g. when a level loads).\n- `beginFrame(now: number, dt: number, pads?: readonly (GamepadLike | null)[]): void`: Engine: begin a frame at game time `now` (seconds). Polls gamepads and drops expired queued presses. `pads` defaults to `navigator.getGamepads()`; tests can pass fakes.\n- `get pointerLocked: boolean`\n- `isDown(...codes: string[]): boolean`\n- `anyDown(codes: readonly string[]): boolean`: `isDown` for a list of codes, without building a rest-argument array (fixed-step code).\n- `wasPressed(...codes: string[]): boolean`: True once per physical key press (valid for the current render frame).\n- `consumePress(...codes: string[]): boolean`: True once per physical key press, kept until consumed or until it is older than `pressWindow` (150 ms of game time). Use from fixed-step code (which may run zero or several times per frame) so no press is dropped, while a stale press (made during a pause, a l…\n- `consumeAny(codes: readonly string[]): boolean`: `consumePress` for a list of codes, without building a rest-argument array.\n- `state(): InputState`: Held keys, queued presses and the pointer as data (`engine.snapshot()`), between frames.\n- `setState(s: InputState, now: number): void`: Back to `state()` at game time `now` (a restore): held keys are those the snapshot held, live ones let go.\n- `get queuedPresses: string[]`: Presses waiting to be consumed (tooling / tests).\n- `clearQueued(...codes: string[]): void`: Drop queued presses (e.g. when a menu or mode swallows input).\n- `moveAxis(): { x: number; y: number }`: -1..1 on each axis (x = right, y = forward): the touch joystick or a gamepad's left stick (whichever is tilted further), else WASD / arrow keys / d-pad.\n- `endFrame(): void`: Call at the end of each rendered frame.\n- `setKey(code: string, down: boolean): void`: Test hook: simulate a key being held (true) or released (false).\n- `setPointer(x: number, y: number, down?: boolean): void`: Test hook: put the pointer at (x, y) in normalised device coordinates, pressed or not.\n- `setButtons(bits: number): void`: Test hook: hold mouse buttons (`PointerEvent.buttons` bits: 1 left, 2 right, 4 middle) as the canvas would.\n- `get mouseButtons: number`: Buttons held, as `PointerEvent.buttons` bits (1 left, 2 right, 4 middle; a finger is left): the same keys as `isDown('MouseLeft')`.\n- `get buttonEvents: readonly ButtonEvent[]`: This frame's pointer button presses and releases, in order, each with where the pointer was: a drag shorter than a frame (a script's) still starts where it started and ends where it ended. Recorded with the frame. UI that acts on clicks reads these; play read…\n- `send(command: unknown): void`: Hand the game data from outside the canvas: a DOM widget's value, a file the player picked, a save read from storage, anything a game would otherwise take straight from the page. It arrives in `commands` at the next frame the game runs, and the recording keep…\n- `get commands: readonly unknown[]`: This frame's commands (`send`), oldest first; empty on most frames. They last one rendered frame: read them in `update` (`fixedUpdate` runs zero or more times a frame).\n- `deliver(): void`: Engine: a live frame begins to run: what was sent since the last one becomes its `commands`.\n- `addPointer(dx: number, dy: number, wheel = 0): void`: Test hook: inject pointer movement / wheel for the next frame.\n- `snapshot(dt: number, last?: SnapshotMemory): InputFrame`: This frame's input as data (after `beginFrame`), for a recording. `last` is the frame before: unchanged held keys, pointer and gamepad connection are left out.\n- `playBefore(f: InputFrame): void`: Replay, before the engine's `beginFrame`: the frame's held keys and presses (presses queue at the time they would have arrived, the frame before's game time).\n- `playAfter(f: InputFrame): void`: Replay, after `beginFrame` (which polled no gamepads): sticks, pointer, wheel, gamepad connection.\n\n### interface InputFrame\n\nWhat the game could read from input in one frame (a recorded frame, see src/engine/replay.ts).\nOptional fields are left out when empty or zero; `held` and `pointer` also when unchanged\nfrom the frame before.\n\n- `dt: number`: Real seconds this frame lasted (game time follows from it).\n- `held?: string[]`: Keys held, keyboard and gamepad buttons together, sorted.\n- `pressed?: string[]`: Keys pressed since the frame before (a tap can be pressed and not held).\n- `analog?: [number, number]`: The touch joystick and the gamepad's left stick (after its deadzone).\n- `pad?: [number, number]`\n- `delta?: [number, number]`: Pointer movement (CSS pixels) and wheel ticks this frame.\n- `wheel?: number`\n- `pointer?: [number, number, number, number]`: Pointer position (normalised device coordinates), over the canvas (1/0), buttons (`mouseButtons`: what `held` says).\n- `gamepad?: boolean`: Whether a gamepad is connected (`gamepadConnected`), when that changes.\n- `touch?: boolean`: Whether the pointer is a touch (`pointer.touch`), when that changes.\n- `clicks?: [string, 0 | 1, number, number][]`: Pointer buttons pressed (1) and let go (0) this frame, in order, each where the pointer was (`buttonEvents`).\n- `commands?: unknown[]`: Data from outside the canvas delivered this frame (`send()`): a DOM widget's value, a save read from storage.\n- `sync?: true`: Ran in the same task as the frame before (frames of one `step(n)` call): no promise callbacks ran between them. A replay waits for them before every other frame.\n\n### interface InputState\n\nWhat input a later frame still reads, as data (`engine.snapshot()`): keys held, presses not yet used and how old they are, the pointer.\n\n- `readonly held: readonly string[]`\n- `readonly queued: readonly (readonly [string, number])[]`: Queued presses: [key, seconds of game time since it was pressed].\n- `readonly pointer: readonly [number, number, number, number]`\n- `readonly gamepad: boolean`\n- `readonly outbox: readonly unknown[]`: Data sent (`send`) and not yet delivered: the next frame that runs delivers it.\n\n### MOUSE_BUTTONS\n\n```ts\nconst MOUSE_BUTTONS: readonly (readonly [code: string, bit: number])[]\n```","numLines":120,"startLine":2262,"totalLines":5144}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01WooGpTHY6VpQJUnKDcgPgU","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgke6HSAkMg7YsQSMohXb","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01PRwD7VPuyKvGYnC8Gk4HaU","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261105-162057__x4c6ec5/work/_research/engine/pixel-engine/API.md","offset":3020,"limit":30},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":13039,"cache_read_input_tokens":106108,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":13039},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"66624378-a7bb-42a2-8c1d-fc63666b5654","uuid":"e7ec4f54-8931-4891-962b-f41db46f552c","timestamp":"2026-11-05T23:21:56.365Z","request_id":"req_011Cgke6HCHNhcqDRLmSZmXm","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01PRwD7VPuyKvGYnC8Gk4HaU":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261105-162057__x4c6ec5/work/_research/engine/pixel-engine/API.md","offset":3020,"limit":30}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PRwD7VPuyKvGYnC8Gk4HaU","type":"tool_result","content":"3020\tclone whose materials are converted to the engine's toon materials.\n3021\t\n3022\t### loadPack()\n3023\t\n3024\t```ts\n3025\tfunction loadPack(url: string, opts: { readonly into?: ModelCatalog; readonly textures?: TextureCatalog } = {}): Promise<ModelCatalog>\n3026\t```\n3027\t\n3028\tLoad a pack of imported models and textures (`public/packs/<pack>.json`, docs/assets/importing.md\n3029\t*Packs*) once, the way `loadModel` loads a GLB: the URL resolved the same way (under the\n3030\tbase URL; a page may put a data URL for it in `__PIXEL_ASSETS__`, as single-file builds do for\n3031\tevery `packs/<id>.json` path the built code names, cut to the models it names), counted by\n3032\t`assetProgress`. Resolves to a catalog of the\n3033\tpack's ModelDefs, to build from with `buildModel(id, { catalog })`; `into` also adds them to a\n3034\tcatalog of yours (e.g. `MODEL_LIBRARY`, so `use` finds them), and `textures` gets the pack's\n3035\tTextureDefs (e.g. `TEXTURE_LIBRARY`, so `textureMap(id)` and `hudSprite(id)` find them).\n3036\t\n3037\t### loadPackIndex()\n3038\t\n3039\t```ts\n3040\tfunction loadPackIndex(url = 'packs/index.json'): Promise<PackIndex>\n3041\t```\n3042\t\n3043\tThe packs a site carries (`public/packs/index.json`, written by the importers): each pack's\n3044\tid, title, file (relative to the index), how many models and textures it holds, its licence\n3045\tand author. Load one with `loadPack(new URL(entry.file, indexUrl))`, or `packs/<file>`.\n3046\t\n3047\t### meanColour()\n3048\t\n3049\t```ts"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"66624378-a7bb-42a2-8c1d-fc63666b5654","uuid":"91f66675-a2c6-4285-a955-de0e59b65312","timestamp":"2026-11-05T23:21:56.370Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261105-162057__x4c6ec5/work/_research/engine/pixel-engine/API.md","content":"clone whose materials are converted to the engine's toon materials.\n\n### loadPack()\n\n```ts\nfunction loadPack(url: string, opts: { readonly into?: ModelCatalog; readonly textures?: TextureCatalog } = {}): Promise<ModelCatalog>\n```\n\nLoad a pack of imported models and textures (`public/packs/<pack>.json`, docs/assets/importing.md\n*Packs*) once, the way `loadModel` loads a GLB: the URL resolved the same way (under the\nbase URL; a page may put a data URL for it in `__PIXEL_ASSETS__`, as single-file builds do for\nevery `packs/<id>.json` path the built code names, cut to the models it names), counted by\n`assetProgress`. Resolves to a catalog of the\npack's ModelDefs, to build from with `buildModel(id, { catalog })`; `into` also adds them to a\ncatalog of yours (e.g. `MODEL_LIBRARY`, so `use` finds them), and `textures` gets the pack's\nTextureDefs (e.g. `TEXTURE_LIBRARY`, so `textureMap(id)` and `hudSprite(id)` find them).\n\n### loadPackIndex()\n\n```ts\nfunction loadPackIndex(url = 'packs/index.json'): Promise<PackIndex>\n```\n\nThe packs a site carries (`public/packs/index.json`, written by the importers): each pack's\nid, title, file (relative to the index), how many models and textures it holds, its licence\nand author. Load one with `loadPack(new URL(entry.file, indexUrl))`, or `packs/<file>`.\n\n### meanColour()\n\n```ts","numLines":30,"startLine":3020,"totalLines":5144}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01PRwD7VPuyKvGYnC8Gk4HaU","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgke6HSAkMg7YsQSMohXb","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01QrioqZ4YG9K5Nag25sazne","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"sed -n 1,30p CHANGELOG.md; echo ...; grep -n \"^## \\|^### \" API.md | sed -n 1,60p; echo ...; grep -n -E \"^### (class |interface )?(Song|AudioSystem|Audio|Sound|SfxDef|SoundDef|Voice|AudioBus|Engine$|class Engine|ToneOptions|Music)\" API.md | head -30; grep -n -i \"voice\\|mix\\|volume\\|musicVolume\\|bus\" API.md | head -50","description":"Skim changelog, API sections and audio features"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":13039,"cache_read_input_tokens":106108,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":13039},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"66624378-a7bb-42a2-8c1d-fc63666b5654","uuid":"eae68411-44d3-4133-8f65-835ca78fa865","timestamp":"2026-11-05T23:21:59.719Z","request_id":"req_011Cgke6HCHNhcqDRLmSZmXm","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01QrioqZ4YG9K5Nag25sazne":{"command":"cd /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261105-162057__x4c6ec5/work/_research/engine/pixel-engine && sed -n 1,30p CHANGELOG.md; echo ...; grep -n \"^## \\|^### \" API.md | sed -n 1,60p; echo ...; grep -n -E \"^### (class |interface )?(Song|AudioSystem|Audio|Sound|SfxDef|SoundDef|Voice|AudioBus|Engine$|class Engine|ToneOptions|Music)\" API.md | head -30; grep -n -i \"voice\\|mix\\|volume\\|musicVolume\\|bus\" API.md | head -50","description":"Skim changelog, API sections and audio features"}},"wire_ingest_context":{"toolu_01QrioqZ4YG9K5Nag25sazne":{"cwd":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261105-162057__x4c6ec5/work/_research/engine/pixel-engine"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QrioqZ4YG9K5Nag25sazne","type":"tool_result","content":"# Changelog\n\nWhat changed in Pixel Engine for a game built on it, newest first. The version is\n`package.json`'s; a game reads it as `ENGINE_VERSION`, `Engine.version` or\n`engine.state().version`. The engine kit (`npm run bundle`, docs/GUIDE.md) ships this file.\n\nWhen a change alters the public API (`src/bundle.ts` exports) or something a game can see,\nbump the version (minor while it is 0.x) and add its section here; `npm test` fails when\n`package.json`'s version has no section.\n\n## 0.46.0\n\n- **No more handoff page**: the engine's site is gone, so `manifest.json` has no `handoff` and the\n  kit's `README.md` no longer names a page for agents. Newer kits are where they were:\n  `latest` (the newest release's zip), `releases` and `source`.\n\n## 0.45.0\n\n- **The kit runs in Node**: `Engine.headless(game, { assets })` from `pixel-engine.js` alone, with\n  no `node_modules`. In Node, Rapier's wasm comes from the data URL the kit inlines, and a reader\n  (`assets`, `setAssetReader`) no longer sees the files the kit embeds (`__PIXEL_ASSETS__`: the\n  built-in models load as they do in a page).\n- **`node check.mjs --headless game.js`**: plays a game module in Node, no browser. It makes the game\n  with the module's `createGame()` export, plays the `--keys` frames, replays the recording in a\n  fresh game and writes `report.json` (`state()`, errors, the fingerprint). Exit 1 on an error or a\n  replay that ends elsewhere, 2 for a page or a module without `createGame()`.\n- **The starter `game.js` exports `createGame()`** and starts the engine only where there is a\n  `document`, so it imports in Node. GUIDE.md section 8, *Play it in Node*, shows a test of your own.\n\n## 0.44.0\n...\n5:## Sections\n23:## 1. Engine and games\n25:### ADAPTIVE_ASPECT\n33:### type AspectMode\n39:### BUILTIN_MODELS\n47:### chunkyResolution()\n55:### clearScene()\n63:### computeFraming()\n69:### countObjects()\n77:### type DebugAction\n87:### type DebugKeyChanges\n95:### type DebugKeyMap\n101:### type DebugKeysOption\n114:### DEFAULT_DEBUG_KEYS\n122:### disposeObject()\n132:### class Engine\n197:### ENGINE_VERSION\n203:### interface EngineOptions\n226:### FPS_OPTIONS\n232:### class FrameLimiter\n242:### frameView()\n253:### interface Framing\n266:### interface Game\n288:### interface GameContext\n305:### interface HeadlessOptions\n312:### class LoadingScreen\n323:### loadSettings()\n331:### normalizeSettings()\n339:### optionsFromUrl()\n352:### PIXEL_SIZES\n358:### interface PlayerSettings\n372:### QUALITY\n381:### QUALITY_LEVELS\n387:### QUALITY_OPTIONS\n393:### type QualityLevel\n407:### type QualityOption\n413:### interface QualitySettings\n418:### rebindDebugKeys()\n428:### interface Resolution\n446:### RESOLUTIONS\n452:### resolveDebugKeys()\n460:### class Settings\n479:### interface SettingsHost\n492:### class SettingsPanel\n507:### storeSettings()\n513:### urlSettings()\n523:### interface Viewport\n531:### withUrlOptions()\n542:## 2. Camera\n544:### CAMERA_PRESETS\n550:### interface CameraConfig\n566:### type CameraPreset\n577:### class CameraRig\n604:### interface CameraRigState\n617:### createCameraRig()\n623:### class FirstPersonRig\n637:### class FreeRig\n655:### class OrthoRig\n666:### class ThirdPersonRig\n677:## 3. Rendering, looks and filters\n...\n132:### class Engine\n2462:### class AudioManager\n2500:### interface AudioSettings\n2582:### interface Song\n2596:### interface SoundDef\n2634:### interface Voice\n2645:### interface VoiceSettings\n14:8. Audio: `attenuation`, `AudioManager`, `AudioSettings`, `Instrument`, `Listener`, `muffleCutoff`, `noteFrequency`, `parseSong`, `PLAYGROUND_SONG`, `PlayOptions`, `renderSound`, `SFX`, `SfxName`, `Song`, `SoundDef`, `Spatial`, `spatialize`, `SpatialOptions`, `Voice`, `VoiceSettings`, `VolumeChannel`, `Wave`\n299:- `readonly audio: AudioManager`: Sound effects, music, volumes (src/engine/audio).\n361:(sound is the AudioManager's, which keeps its own volumes). A field left out is the game's own\n496:settings.ts; the AudioManager's volumes). The engine pauses the game while it is open. Keys\n685:Every filter's strength: the filtered colour mixed over the input (0 = off, 1 = full).\n694:result is mixed over the input by its `amount`. Unknown ids return the input.\n897:(`FILTER_PRESETS`) as a whole-scene look, then looks that mix layers and stacks.\n1498:- `updateVisual(model: Object3D, dt: number): void`: Per render frame: pick animation state and advance the mixer.\n1637:- `add(body: RAPIER_TYPE.RigidBody): void`: Float `body`: its first collider's shape sets the octants (its bounding box) and the volume it can push aside (a box, ball, capsule, cylinder or cone; any other shape counts as a box).\n1813:- `trigger(shape: TriggerShape, at: readonly [number, number, number] | Vector3, options: TriggerOptions = {}): Trigger`: A trigger volume with enter/exit callbacks (pickups, checkpoints, kill zones, doors). Checked after every fixed step against dynamic and kinematic bodies (the character included); set `includeStatic` for level geometry. Never blocks movement or rays.\n1968:along and across, that keeps its volume. `pressure` 0..1 sets how firm it is.\n1989:A sensor volume with enter/exit callbacks. It is not a collider in the world: after\n1996:- `readonly inside: Map<number, Collider>`: Colliders inside the volume right now, by handle.\n2008:- `onEnter?(other: RAPIER.Collider, trigger: Trigger): void`: Called once when a collider starts overlapping the volume.\n2022:Trigger volume shape (half extents / radius in world units).\n2069:- `tris: Uint32Array<ArrayBuffer>`: Triangles (cloth and blobs): wind drag, volume and drawing.\n2081:- `restVolume: number`: Pressure blobs: the volume it tries to keep (0: off), and how hard.\n2089:- `volume(): number`: Signed volume enclosed by the triangles (blobs; needs a closed, outward-wound surface).\n2219:- `animationMix(): { name: string; weight: number; time: number; rate: number }[]`: Tooling: every clip currently contributing to the pose, with its blend weight and time (s).\n2460:Volume at `distance` (m): 1 inside `ref`, then inverse distance, fading to 0 at `max`.\n2465:sequencer (`Song`, data), optional audio files, and master / sfx / music volume + mute\n2480:- `get volume: Readonly<AudioSettings>`: Current volumes and mute (persisted).\n2486:- `loop(sound: string | SoundDef, settings: VoiceSettings = {}): Voice`: Start a sound looping, as a voice you steer while it plays (volume, pan, muffle, pitch: with `spatialize` it sits at a place in the world). Before the first user input, or with no Web Audio, the voice keeps its settings silently and starts once audio unlocks.…\n2487:- `get loops: readonly Voice[]`: Voices playing or waiting to (tooling and tests).\n2488:- `stopLoops(): void`: Stop every looping voice (the engine does this when a level unloads).\n2492:- `persist: boolean`: Volume changes are written to storage. The engine turns this off while a replay plays: the recording sets the volumes it set, nothing is written, and the player's come back after.\n2493:- `setVolume(channel: VolumeChannel, value: number): void`\n2496:- `resetVolumes(): void`: The default volumes, sound on (the settings panel's Reset).\n2554:- `volume?: number`: 0..1 multiplier on the sound's own volume.\n2569:const SFX: { jump: { wave: \"square\"; duty: number; freq: number; freqEnd: number; attack: number; sustain: number; decay: number; volume: number; }; doubleJump: { wave: \"square\"; duty: number; freq: number; freqEnd: number; ... 5 more ...; volume: number; }; ... 8 more ...; fanfare: { ...; }; }\n2603:- `attack?: number`: Envelope (seconds): ramp up, hold at full volume, then fade out.\n2606:- `volume?: number`: Peak level 0..1 (default 0.5).\n2612:- `layers?: readonly SoundDef[]`: Extra sounds mixed in (e.g. a noise burst under a thump).\n2616:- `gain: number`: 0..1 volume.\n2630:- `ref?: number`: Within this distance (m) the sound is at full volume (default 1).\n2634:### interface Voice\n2639:- `readonly settings: Readonly<Required<VoiceSettings>>`: The values last set (kept headless too, so games and tests can read them).\n2642:- `set(s: VoiceSettings): void`: Change any of its settings; they glide over a few hundredths of a second (no clicks).\n2645:### interface VoiceSettings\n2647:- `volume?: number`: 0..1 (default 1).\n2652:### type VolumeChannel\n2655:type VolumeChannel = 'master' | 'sfx' | 'music'\n3685:- `pose?: Pose`: Extra joints/overrides mixed into every frame (hands, hat...).\n3820:`scale()` turns it into a volume-preserving pair: `const [h, v] = squash.scale();\n3831:- `scale(out: [number, number] = [1, 1]): [number, number]`: [horizontal, vertical] scale: y = 1 + s, xz = 1 / √(1 + s), so the volume stays put.\n3907:`three/tsl` (three.js shading language: `uniform`, `vec3`, `mix`, `Fn`, ...) for node materials and custom filters. All shader code is TSL; there is no GLSL.\n4575:const PLANTS: { bush: { system: { axiom: string; rules: { A: string; F: string; S: string; }; }; iterations: number; turtle: { angle: number; length: number; lengthScale: number; radius: number; radiusScale: number; }; }; fern: { ...; }; weed: { ...; }; tree: { ...; }; }","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"66624378-a7bb-42a2-8c1d-fc63666b5654","uuid":"879a4283-734b-4e8d-b724-e38c84cc8fda","timestamp":"2026-11-05T23:21:59.742Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"# Changelog\n\nWhat changed in Pixel Engine for a game built on it, newest first. The version is\n`package.json`'s; a game reads it as `ENGINE_VERSION`, `Engine.version` or\n`engine.state().version`. The engine kit (`npm run bundle`, docs/GUIDE.md) ships this file.\n\nWhen a change alters the public API (`src/bundle.ts` exports) or something a game can see,\nbump the version (minor while it is 0.x) and add its section here; `npm test` fails when\n`pac