{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgke71chWQiSGL9BhcthZ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_018kSrL84yBrMFCwevTRuVJV","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261105-162057__x4c6ec5/work/_research/engine/pixel-engine/API.md","offset":132,"limit":175},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11647,"cache_read_input_tokens":119147,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11647},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"66624378-a7bb-42a2-8c1d-fc63666b5654","uuid":"b91a3c1f-c413-40f5-9215-5d6037672618","timestamp":"2026-11-05T23:23:26.160Z","request_id":"req_011Cgke71P4H5ev6jrSqR8ke","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_018kSrL84yBrMFCwevTRuVJV":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261105-162057__x4c6ec5/work/_research/engine/pixel-engine/API.md","offset":132,"limit":175}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018kSrL84yBrMFCwevTRuVJV","type":"tool_result","content":"132\t### class Engine\n133\t\n134\t- `static readonly version: string`: The engine's version (package.json).\n135\t- `static start(game: Game, options: EngineOptions = {}): Promise<Engine>`\n136\t- `static headless(game: Game, options: HeadlessOptions = {}): Promise<Engine>`: The engine without a page (docs/engine/headless.md): the same boot, game code and frames as `start`, with a `HeadlessView` that draws nothing, in Node. No DOM, no GPU, no listeners, no AudioContext (`audio.counts` still counts), no panels; errors go to `engin…\n137\t- `readonly errors: string[]`: Errors thrown by the game's hooks while the render loop ran, each message once, oldest first (at most 20). They are logged to the console and shown in a box on screen; the frame keeps rendering so the picture stays up. Fix the first one first. (`step()` throw…\n138\t- `readonly input: Input`\n139\t- `readonly sun: DirectionalLight`\n140\t- `readonly ambient: AmbientLight`\n141\t- `readonly context: GameContext`\n142\t- `readonly audio: AudioManager`\n143\t- `readonly particles: Particles`\n144\t- `readonly hud: Hud`\n145\t- `readonly tweens: Tweens`: Tweens on game time (`ctx.tweens`).\n146\t- `readonly shake: CameraShake`: Screen shake (trauma 0..1): `engine.shake.add(0.4)`. Added to the camera after the rig placed it, on real time; ortho cameras move by whole art pixels. Reset on level unload.\n147\t- `debug: DebugUI | null`\n148\t- `touch: TouchControls | null`\n149\t- `readonly settings: Settings`: The player's settings: look, pixel art, pixel size, quality, frame rate and debug info (plain data, saved per game), applied over the game's own choices; `open()` shows them in the settings panel (the ⚙ button, `EngineOptions.settings`). Sound is `audio`'s.\n150\t- `debugKeys: DebugKeyMap`: Active engine hotkeys (from EngineOptions.debugKeys; none until the engine starts).\n151\t- `manual: boolean`: Tooling: the render loop stops driving the game; advance it yourself with `step()`. Frame-exact and independent of how fast the browser renders (filmstrips, replays).\n152\t- `frame: number`\n153\t- `fps: number`: Rendered frames per second (measured over ~0.5 s).\n154\t- `readonly qualityOption: QualityOption`: Requested quality (`auto` adapts); `quality` is the level in use.\n155\t- `time: number`\n156\t- `readonly renderer: View`\n157\t- `readonly physics: Physics`\n158\t- `camera: CameraRig`\n159\t- `readonly scene: Scene`\n160\t- `get gpu: PixelRenderer | null`: The page's renderer, for direct GPU work (readbacks, the canvas, compute): null headless (`Engine.headless`), where nothing is drawn. Gameplay reads `renderer`, which both have.\n161\t- `get game: Game`: The running game. Replace it with `loadGame()`.\n162\t- `get paused: boolean`: Freeze simulation, animation, particles and game time (rendering continues). For tooling, capture and pause menus. Queued key presses are dropped while paused.\n163\t- `get lights: LightPool`: The dynamic light pool (render/lights.ts), engine-owned: its lights stay in the scene across levels, so loading another level never changes the light count (no recompiles). Created on first use; sized by quality.\n164\t- `get timeScale: number`: Game speed: 1 normal, 0.25 slow motion, 2 double, 0 frozen (a menu: key presses made meanwhile are dropped, like a pause). Scales game time, physics, animation, particles, tweens and the camera follow; not transitions, shake or the render loop. Back to 1 when…\n165\t- `hitstop(seconds: number): void`: Freeze the game for `seconds` of real time: impact frames that sell a hit. Rendering, screen shake and transitions keep going; overlapping hitstops keep the longest. Key presses made during a hitstop still count afterwards (a buffered jump).\n166\t- `get screen: ScreenFx`: Transitions, flashes and shockwaves (render/screenFx.ts): `await engine.screen.cover('iris')`.\n167\t- `get sunDir: Vector3`: The direction sunlight comes from (unit, pointing at the sun).\n168\t- `setSunDirection(dir: Vector3 | readonly [number, number, number]): void`: Point the sun (and its shadows): a direction toward the sun, normalised here. A level's time of day or a sun dial; reset to the engine default when the level unloads. Below the horizon the sun is kept just above it (lower `sun.intensity` for night instead).\n169\t- `get quality: QualityLevel`: The quality level in use (see `EngineOptions.quality`).\n170\t- `useQuality(option: QualityOption): void`: Quality by option: a level, or `auto`: the device's level (low on phones and tablets, else medium), lowered once if a few seconds of frames are slow (measured once, then remembered).\n171\t- `get debugShown: boolean`: Whether the debug panel is showing.\n172\t- `showDebug(on: boolean): void`: Show or hide the debug panel (created the first time it shows, filled at once: the loop fills it only while the game runs).\n173\t- `setQuality(level: QualityLevel): void`\n174\t- `get maxFps: number`: Frame cap of the render loop (0 = display rate).\n175\t- `setCamera(config: CameraConfig, options: { syncUrl?: boolean } = {}): CameraRig`: Swap the camera preset mid-game (review tool: presets are a per-game choice). The player, physics and the rest of the world are left exactly as they are. `free` and `fixed` without an explicit position start from the view currently on screen. Keeps `?camera=`…\n176\t- `toggleMode(): RenderMode`\n177\t- `toggleResolution(): Resolution`\n178\t- `get availableFilters: readonly string[]`: Every filter id the engine ships (see render/filters.ts).\n179\t- `get filters: readonly string[]`\n180\t- `setFilters(ids: readonly string[]): void`\n181\t- `get look: Look`: The current look (a copy): per-layer pixel art and filters, whole-scene filters.\n182\t- `get lookPresets: Readonly<Record<string, Look>>`: Every named look (LOOK_PRESETS): `engine.setLook(engine.lookPresets.pixel_heroes)`.\n183\t- `setLook(look: Look): void`: Apply a look (render/look.ts); `LOOK_PRESETS` has named ones.\n184\t- `cycleLook(step: 1 | -1): string`: Cycle through LOOK_PRESETS ([ and ] keys).\n185\t- `state(): { version: string; game: string; seed: number; ready: boolean; paused: boolean; time: number; timeScale: number; hitstop: number; screen: ScreenFxState; backend: BackendName; ... 19 more ...; status: string; }`: Snapshot for tests, tooling and agents.\n186\t- `step(n = 1, dt = 1 / 60): void`: Tooling: switch to manual time and advance `n` frames of `dt` seconds (simulation, animation, camera). Nothing is drawn; call `renderer.capture()` to see the result.\n187\t- `loadGame(game: Game, options: { camera?: CameraConfig } = {}): Promise<void>`: Unload the running game and start `game` in the same engine (renderer, canvas, camera rig, input, audio context and debug UI stay).\n188\t- `dispose(): void`: Stop the loop and free everything: game, scene, physics, audio, input listeners, DOM, GPU. With a `loadGame()` in flight, the world and GPU resources are freed once its `setup()` has finished (it never runs against a freed physics world or renderer).\n189\t- `get seed: number`: The seed every level's `ctx.random` comes from (`EngineOptions.seed`, `?seed=`; a replay sets it).\n190\t- `get random: Rng`: This level's seeded randomness (`ctx.random`).\n191\t- `recording(): Recording | null`: The running level's recording: its seed, when it began and every frame's input since (src/engine/replay.ts). Plain JSON: save it, attach it to a bug, replay it with `replay()`.\n192\t- `replay(rec: Recording, start: () => unknown, o: { frames?: number } = {}): Promise<void>`: Play a recording back: its seed and start time are restored, `start` loads the level it was made in (e.g. `() => engine.loadGame(new MyGame())`; it must not wait on frames, so load without a transition), then every frame runs with the recorded input, in manua…\n193\t- `snapshot(): Promise<Snapshot>`: The running level as data, between frames (docs/engine/determinism.md *Save-states*): game time, the clock, `ctx.random`, the physics world, the camera, the shake, input still waiting, and the game's own state (`Game.snapshot`). Plain JSON. Taken once the las…\n194\t- `restore(saved: Snapshot): Promise<void>`: Put the running level back to a snapshot of it: the same game (by name), already loaded; nothing in between is replayed. The level's recording starts again from it (`Recording.from`), so what is played next replays with `replay()`. Live input held now is let…\n195\t- `fingerprint(): string`: A hash of the simulation: game time, every physics body (exact), `ctx.random`'s state and the game's own (`Game.fingerprint`). Two runs that should be the same (a replay) compare equal. Not the `status()` line: a debug line may carry measurements (a step's mi…\n196\t\n197\t### ENGINE_VERSION\n198\t\n199\t```ts\n200\tconst ENGINE_VERSION: string\n201\t```\n202\t\n203\t### interface EngineOptions\n204\t\n205\t- `container?: HTMLElement`\n206\t- `resolution?: Resolution`\n207\t- `aspect?: AspectMode`: `adaptive` (default): the art height stays `resolution.height` and the art width follows the screen's aspect (portrait phones fill the screen). `fixed`: always exactly `resolution`, letterboxed. `fill`: adjusts both art dimensions to cover the viewport at an…\n208\t- `maxFps?: number`: Cap the render loop (simulation keeps its fixed 60 Hz physics step). Default 60, so 120 Hz phones don't run the whole pipeline twice per game frame. 0 = display rate.\n209\t- `quality?: QualityOption`: Shadow map quality, or `auto` (default: low on touch devices, else medium; lowered once on a slow start).\n210\t- `loadingScreen?: boolean`: Pixel-styled loading indicator while the renderer, physics and assets load. Default true.\n211\t- `mode?: RenderMode`\n212\t- `edges?: EdgeSettings`\n213\t- `camera?: CameraConfig`: Camera preset + parameters. Pick one per game; not meant to change during play.\n214\t- `filters?: readonly string[]`: Post filters (ids from FILTERS) applied in Pixel mode, in order.\n215\t- `look?: Look`: A full look: pixel art or clean rendering and filters per layer (characters & objects, environment) plus whole-scene filters (render/look.ts). Wins over `filters` and `edges`.\n216\t- `seed?: number`: Seed for `ctx.random`: each level gets its own stream from this and the game's name, so the same seed replays the same game. Default 1. `?seed=` in the URL.\n217\t- `record?: number`: Frames of input the engine keeps for each level (`recording()`). Default `MAX_FRAMES`, an hour at 60 fps (up to some 20 MB of JSON with the mouse moving all the time); 0 records nothing.\n218\t- `debugUI?: boolean`: Debug panel. Default: on in dev (`vite`) or with ?debug=1, off in production builds.\n219\t- `debugKeys?: DebugKeysOption`: Engine hotkeys (P Pixel/Raw, R resolution, ` debug panel, [ ] looks, M mute): developer tools. Default: on with the debug panel (dev, ?debug=1), off in a shipped game, whose players have the same options in the settings. `true` / `false`: always on / off; an…\n220\t- `settings?: { button?: TouchCorner | boolean; key?: string | false }`: The player's settings (`engine.settings`): look, pixel art, pixel size, quality, frame rate, sound and debug info, in a panel the ⚙ button opens. `button`: its corner (default, or true: 'top-right'; on a mouse it shows while the pointer moves), or false for n…\n221\t- `touch?: boolean`: On-screen joystick + buttons. Default: auto (coarse pointer), or ?touch=1 / ?touch=0.\n222\t- `touchButtons?: readonly TouchButton[]`: On-screen action buttons. Default: the platformer's (DEFAULT_TOUCH_BUTTONS).\n223\t- `touchBar?: boolean`: The touch top bar of the engine hotkeys (ⓘ debug panel, P, R, ◐ looks, ♪ mute), on a touch screen while they are on (see `debugKeys`). Default true; false hides it even then, unless the debug panel is on (`?debug=1`, dev builds).\n224\t- `background?: number`\n225\t\n226\t### FPS_OPTIONS\n227\t\n228\t```ts\n229\tconst FPS_OPTIONS: readonly number[]\n230\t```\n231\t\n232\t### class FrameLimiter\n233\t\n234\tFrame cap for `requestAnimationFrame` loops. Returns whether the frame at `now` (ms)\n235\tshould run, and advances the schedule so the long-run rate is `maxFps` even when the\n236\tdisplay rate isn't a multiple of it (90 Hz, 144 Hz) and timestamps jitter.\n237\t\n238\t- `new FrameLimiter(maxFps: number)`\n239\t- `maxFps: number`\n240\t- `shouldRun(now: number): boolean`\n241\t\n242\t### frameView()\n243\t\n244\t```ts\n245\tfunction frameView(viewport: Viewport, base: Resolution, aspect: AspectMode, current: Resolution): { framing: Framing; resolution: Resolution }\n246\t```\n247\t\n248\tThe framing and art resolution of a view (PixelRenderer's canvas, or a HeadlessView's viewport):\n249\t`computeFraming`, and the art size as a resolution. Identity is kept where nothing changed: the\n250\t`current` object while the art size stays, else `base` itself when the art size equals it (a\n251\tfixed or 16:9 view reports the preset it was given), else a new one.\n252\t\n253\t### interface Framing\n254\t\n255\t- `readonly artWidth: number`: Art (internal) resolution actually rendered. Equals `internal` in fixed mode.\n256\t- `readonly artHeight: number`\n257\t- `readonly scale: number`: Device pixels per art pixel. Integer whenever `integer` is true.\n258\t- `readonly integer: boolean`: True when every art pixel maps to an integer block of device pixels.\n259\t- `readonly canvasWidth: number`: Canvas backing-store size in device pixels.\n260\t- `readonly canvasHeight: number`\n261\t- `readonly cssWidth: number`: Canvas CSS size and offset inside the viewport, in CSS pixels.\n262\t- `readonly cssHeight: number`\n263\t- `readonly offsetX: number`\n264\t- `readonly offsetY: number`\n265\t\n266\t### interface Game\n267\t\n268\tA game is a plain object. The engine owns rendering, physics stepping, camera,\n269\tlighting and debug UI; the game only builds its world and reacts to input.\n270\t\n271\t- `readonly name: string`\n272\t- `readonly args?: unknown`: The plain JSON the game was made with, when its name alone can't rebuild it (a lab's depth and seed, the door a room was entered by): kept in its recordings (`Recording.args`), so a tool can make the same game again to replay one (`npm run replay`, src/games.…\n273\t- `readonly assets?: readonly string[]`: Models `setup` will load (`ctx.loadModel` URLs). Optional: listing them lets the engine start downloading while the renderer and physics initialise.\n274\t- `setup(ctx: GameContext): Promise<void> | void`: Build the level: load models, add colliders, spawn entities.\n275\t- `fixedUpdate?(ctx: GameContext, dt: number): void`: Runs before every fixed 60 Hz physics step. Move characters/apply forces here.\n276\t- `ready?(ctx: GameContext): void`: Runs when the engine starts running this game: after `Engine.start` (just before `__PIXEL_READY__` resolves) and after each `loadGame`. Install page handles for tools here (`window.__MYGAME__`), so they never exist before the engine can step. An exception is…\n277\t- `update?(ctx: GameContext, dt: number): void`: Runs once per rendered frame after physics (animation, pickups, UI state).\n278\t- `cameraTarget(ctx: GameContext): Vector3`: World position the camera follows.\n279\t- `eyePosition?(ctx: GameContext): Vector3`: First-person eye position (defaults to cameraTarget + 0.7 up).\n280\t- `status?(ctx: GameContext): string`: One-line status for the debug UI (score, lives...).\n281\t- `fingerprint?(ctx: GameContext): string`: The game's own state as a short string or hash, for `engine.fingerprint()`: what lives outside physics and `ctx.random` (positions, life, counters, the game's own random streams), so two runs that should match (a replay) can be compared. Only what a replay mu…\n282\t- `snapshot?(ctx: GameContext): unknown`: The game's own state as plain data, between frames (`engine.snapshot()`): everything a later frame reads that the engine does not keep (its entities, timers, random streams; the handles of its physics bodies). With `restore`, the game has save-states.\n283\t- `restore?(ctx: GameContext, state: unknown): void | Promise<void>`: Put the game back to what `snapshot` returned (`engine.restore()`): the same game, already set up. Find physics bodies again by handle (`ctx.physics.world.getRigidBody(h)`).\n284\t- `beforeRestore?(ctx: GameContext): void | Promise<void>`: Optional: leave whatever mode the game is in before a restore changes anything (a mode with a camera of its own, a dialog, a level): `engine.restore()` calls it first, then checks the snapshot fits (the camera preset) and puts the engine's part back, then `re…\n285\t- `onCameraChange?(ctx: GameContext): void`: Called after `engine.setCamera()` swapped the camera preset mid-game. The world, physics and player are untouched; adjust anything that depends on the preset (e.g. side-scroller lane lock, hiding the player model in first person).\n286\t- `dispose?(ctx: GameContext): void`: Called when the game is unloaded (`engine.loadGame(other)` or `engine.dispose()`), before the engine removes its scene objects, physics bodies, triggers, particles, HUD and music. Free anything else the game owns: DOM, timers, listeners, HMR hooks.\n287\t\n288\t### interface GameContext\n289\t\n290\t- `readonly engine: Engine`\n291\t- `readonly scene: Scene`\n292\t- `readonly physics: Physics`\n293\t- `readonly input: Input`\n294\t- `readonly camera: CameraRig`\n295\t- `readonly loadModel: typeof loadModel`\n296\t- `readonly palette: typeof PALETTE`\n297\t- `readonly time: number`: Game time in seconds since start. Frozen while paused or loading.\n298\t- `readonly random: Rng`: This level's seeded randomness (src/engine/random.ts): the same engine seed and game give the same numbers. Use it (or forks of it) for everything random; never Math.random.\n299\t- `readonly audio: AudioManager`: Sound effects, music, volumes (src/engine/audio).\n300\t- `readonly particles: Particles`: Pixel particles: `particles.burst('dust', at)` (src/engine/particles).\n301\t- `readonly hud: Hud`: Pixel HUD text and icons in art pixels (src/engine/hud).\n302\t- `readonly lights: LightPool`: Dynamic point lights: `lights.request({ position, color, intensity, radius, flicker })` (src/engine/render/lights.ts). A fixed pool sized by quality, created on first use.\n303\t- `readonly tweens: Tweens`: Tweens on game time: `tweens.to(door.position, { y: 3 }, { duration: 0.8, ease: 'outBack' })` (src/engine/tween.ts). Cleared when the level unloads.\n304\t\n305\t### interface HeadlessOptions\n306\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"66624378-a7bb-42a2-8c1d-fc63666b5654","uuid":"3154b649-326e-4a3d-a071-ca6a945356c8","timestamp":"2026-11-05T23:23:26.167Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261105-162057__x4c6ec5/work/_research/engine/pixel-engine/API.md","content":"### class Engine\n\n- `static readonly version: string`: The engine's version (package.json).\n- `static start(game: Game, options: EngineOptions = {}): Promise<Engine>`\n- `static headless(game: Game, options: HeadlessOptions = {}): Promise<Engine>`: The engine without a page (docs/engine/headless.md): the same boot, game code and frames as `start`, with a `HeadlessView` that draws nothing, in Node. No DOM, no GPU, no listeners, no AudioContext (`audio.counts` still counts), no panels; errors go to `engin…\n- `readonly errors: string[]`: Errors thrown by the game's hooks while the render loop ran, each message once, oldest first (at most 20). They are logged to the console and shown in a box on screen; the frame keeps rendering so the picture stays up. Fix the first one first. (`step()` throw…\n- `readonly input: Input`\n- `readonly sun: DirectionalLight`\n- `readonly ambient: AmbientLight`\n- `readonly context: GameContext`\n- `readonly audio: AudioManager`\n- `readonly particles: Particles`\n- `readonly hud: Hud`\n- `readonly tweens: Tweens`: Tweens on game time (`ctx.tweens`).\n- `readonly shake: CameraShake`: Screen shake (trauma 0..1): `engine.shake.add(0.4)`. Added to the camera after the rig placed it, on real time; ortho cameras move by whole art pixels. Reset on level unload.\n- `debug: DebugUI | null`\n- `touch: TouchControls | null`\n- `readonly settings: Settings`: The player's settings: look, pixel art, pixel size, quality, frame rate and debug info (plain data, saved per game), applied over the game's own choices; `open()` shows them in the settings panel (the ⚙ button, `EngineOptions.settings`). Sound is `audio`'s.\n- `debugKeys: DebugKeyMap`: Active engine hotkeys (from EngineOptions.debugKeys; none until the engine starts).\n- `manual: boolean`: Tooling: the render loop stops driving the game; advance it yourself with `step()`. Frame-exact and independent of how fast the browser renders (filmstrips, replays).\n- `frame: number`\n- `fps: number`: Rendered frames per second (measured over ~0.5 s).\n- `readonly qualityOption: QualityOption`: Requested quality (`auto` adapts); `quality` is the level in use.\n- `time: number`\n- `readonly renderer: View`\n- `readonly physics: Physics`\n- `camera: CameraRig`\n- `readonly scene: Scene`\n- `get gpu: PixelRenderer | null`: The page's renderer, for direct GPU work (readbacks, the canvas, compute): null headless (`Engine.headless`), where nothing is drawn. Gameplay reads `renderer`, which both have.\n- `get game: Game`: The running game. Replace it with `loadGame()`.\n- `get paused: boolean`: Freeze simulation, animation, particles and game time (rendering continues). For tooling, capture and pause menus. Queued key presses are dropped while paused.\n- `get lights: LightPool`: The dynamic light pool (render/lights.ts), engine-owned: its lights stay in the scene across levels, so loading another level never changes the light count (no recompiles). Created on first use; sized by quality.\n- `get timeScale: number`: Game speed: 1 normal, 0.25 slow motion, 2 double, 0 frozen (a menu: key presses made meanwhile are dropped, like a pause). Scales game time, physics, animation, particles, tweens and the camera follow; not transitions, shake or the render loop. Back to 1 when…\n- `hitstop(seconds: number): void`: Freeze the game for `seconds` of real time: impact frames that sell a hit. Rendering, screen shake and transitions keep going; overlapping hitstops keep the longest. Key presses made during a hitstop still count afterwards (a buffered jump).\n- `get screen: ScreenFx`: Transitions, flashes and shockwaves (render/screenFx.ts): `await engine.screen.cover('iris')`.\n- `get sunDir: Vector3`: The direction sunlight comes from (unit, pointing at the sun).\n- `setSunDirection(dir: Vector3 | readonly [number, number, number]): void`: Point the sun (and its shadows): a direction toward the sun, normalised here. A level's time of day or a sun dial; reset to the engine default when the level unloads. Below the horizon the sun is kept just above it (lower `sun.intensity` for night instead).\n- `get quality: QualityLevel`: The quality level in use (see `EngineOptions.quality`).\n- `useQuality(option: QualityOption): void`: Quality by option: a level, or `auto`: the device's level (low on phones and tablets, else medium), lowered once if a few seconds of frames are slow (measured once, then remembered).\n- `get debugShown: boolean`: Whether the debug panel is showing.\n- `showDebug(on: boolean): void`: Show or hide the debug panel (created the first time it shows, filled at once: the loop fills it only while the game runs).\n- `setQuality(level: QualityLevel): void`\n- `get maxFps: number`: Frame cap of the render loop (0 = display rate).\n- `setCamera(config: CameraConfig, options: { syncUrl?: boolean } = {}): CameraRig`: Swap the camera preset mid-game (review tool: presets are a per-game choice). The player, physics and the rest of the world are left exactly as they are. `free` and `fixed` without an explicit position start from the view currently on screen. Keeps `?camera=`…\n- `toggleMode(): RenderMode`\n- `toggleResolution(): Resolution`\n- `get availableFilters: readonly string[]`: Every filter id the engine ships (see render/filters.ts).\n- `get filters: readonly string[]`\n- `setFilters(ids: readonly string[]): void`\n- `get look: Look`: The current look (a copy): per-layer pixel art and filters, whole-scene filters.\n- `get lookPresets: Readonly<Record<string, Look>>`: Every named look (LOOK_PRESETS): `engine.setLook(engine.lookPresets.pixel_heroes)`.\n- `setLook(look: Look): void`: Apply a look (render/look.ts); `LOOK_PRESETS` has named ones.\n- `cycleLook(step: 1 | -1): string`: Cycle through LOOK_PRESETS ([ and ] keys).\n- `state(): { version: string; game: string; seed: number; ready: boolean; paused: boolean; time: number; timeScale: number; hitstop: number; screen: ScreenFxState; backend: BackendName; ... 19 more ...; status: string; }`: Snapshot for tests, tooling and agents.\n- `step(n = 1, dt = 1 / 60): void`: Tooling: switch to manual time and advance `n` frames of `dt` seconds (simulation, animation, camera). Nothing is drawn; call `renderer.capture()` to see the result.\n- `loadGame(game: Game, options: { camera?: CameraConfig } = {}): Promise<void>`: Unload the running game and start `game` in the same engine (renderer, canvas, camera rig, input, audio context and debug UI stay).\n- `dispose(): void`: Stop the loop and free everything: game, scene, physics, audio, input listeners, DOM, GPU. With a `loadGame()` in flight, the world and GPU resources are freed once its `setup()` has finished (it never runs against a freed physics world or renderer).\n- `get seed: number`: The seed every level's `ctx.random` comes from (`EngineOptions.seed`, `?seed=`; a replay sets it).\n- `get random: Rng`: This level's seeded randomness (`ctx.random`).\n- `recording(): Recording | null`: The running level's recording: its seed, when it began and every frame's input since (src/engine/replay.ts). Plain JSON: save it, attach it to a bug, replay it with `replay()`.\n- `replay(rec: Recording, start: () => unknown, o: { frames?: number } = {}): Promise<void>`: Play a recording back: its seed and start time are restored, `start` loads the level it was made in (e.g. `() => engine.loadGame(new MyGame())`; it must not wait on frames, so load without a transition), then every frame runs with the recorded input, in manua…\n- `snapshot(): Promise<Snapshot>`: The running level as data, between frames (docs/engine/determinism.md *Save-states*): game time, the clock, `ctx.random`, the physics world, the camera, the shake, input still waiting, and the game's own state (`Game.snapshot`). Plain JSON. Taken once the las…\n- `restore(saved: Snapshot): Promise<void>`: Put the running level back to a snapshot of it: the same game (by name), already loaded; nothing in between is replayed. The level's recording starts again from it (`Recording.from`), so what is played next replays with `replay()`. Live input held now is let…\n- `fingerprint(): string`: A hash of the simulation: game time, every physics body (exact), `ctx.random`'s state and the game's own (`Game.fingerprint`). Two runs that should be the same (a replay) compare equal. Not the `status()` line: a debug line may carry measurements (a step's mi…\n\n### ENGINE_VERSION\n\n```ts\nconst ENGINE_VERSION: string\n```\n\n### interface EngineOptions\n\n- `container?: HTMLElement`\n- `resolution?: Resolution`\n- `aspect?: AspectMode`: `adaptive` (default): the art height stays `resolution.height` and the art width follows the screen's aspect (portrait phones fill the screen). `fixed`: always exactly `resolution`, letterboxed. `fill`: adjusts both art dimensions to cover the viewport at an…\n- `maxFps?: number`: Cap the render loop (simulation keeps its fixed 60 Hz physics step). Default 60, so 120 Hz phones don't run the whole pipeline twice per game frame. 0 = display rate.\n- `quality?: QualityOption`: Shadow map quality, or `auto` (default: low on touch devices, else medium; lowered once on a slow start).\n- `loadingScreen?: boolean`: Pixel-styled loading indicator while the renderer, physics and assets load. Default true.\n- `mode?: RenderMode`\n- `edges?: EdgeSettings`\n- `camera?: CameraConfig`: Camera preset + parameters. Pick one per game; not meant to change during play.\n- `filters?: readonly string[]`: Post filters (ids from FILTERS) applied in Pixel mode, in order.\n- `look?: Look`: A full look: pixel art or clean rendering and filters per layer (characters & objects, environment) plus whole-scene filters (render/look.ts). Wins over `filters` and `edges`.\n- `seed?: number`: Seed for `ctx.random`: each level gets its own stream from this and the game's name, so the same seed replays the same game. Default 1. `?seed=` in the URL.\n- `record?: number`: Frames of input the engine keeps for each level (`recording()`). Default `MAX_FRAMES`, an hour at 60 fps (up to some 20 MB of JSON with the mouse moving all the time); 0 records nothing.\n- `debugUI?: boolean`: Debug panel. Default: on in dev (`vite`) or with ?debug=1, off in production builds.\n- `debugKeys?: DebugKeysOption`: Engine hotkeys (P Pixel/Raw, R resolution, ` debug panel, [ ] looks, M mute): developer tools. Default: on with the debug panel (dev, ?debug=1), off in a shipped game, whose players have the same options in the settings. `true` / `false`: always on / off; an…\n- `settings?: { button?: TouchCorner | boolean; key?: string | false }`: The player's settings (`engine.settings`): look, pixel art, pixel size, quality, frame rate, sound and debug info, in a panel the ⚙ button opens. `button`: its corner (default, or true: 'top-right'; on a mouse it shows while the pointer moves), or false for n…\n- `touch?: boolean`: On-screen joystick + buttons. Default: auto (coarse pointer), or ?touch=1 / ?touch=0.\n- `touchButtons?: readonly TouchButton[]`: On-screen action buttons. Default: the platformer's (DEFAULT_TOUCH_BUTTONS).\n- `touchBar?: boolean`: The touch top bar of the engine hotkeys (ⓘ debug panel, P, R, ◐ looks, ♪ mute), on a touch screen while they are on (see `debugKeys`). Default true; false hides it even then, unless the debug panel is on (`?debug=1`, dev builds).\n- `background?: number`\n\n### FPS_OPTIONS\n\n```ts\nconst FPS_OPTIONS: readonly number[]\n```\n\n### class FrameLimiter\n\nFrame cap for `requestAnimationFrame` loops. Returns whether the frame at `now` (ms)\nshould run, and advances the schedule so the long-run rate is `maxFps` even when the\ndisplay rate isn't a multiple of it (90 Hz, 144 Hz) and timestamps jitter.\n\n- `new FrameLimiter(maxFps: number)`\n- `maxFps: number`\n- `shouldRun(now: number): boolean`\n\n### frameView()\n\n```ts\nfunction frameView(viewport: Viewport, base: Resolution, aspect: AspectMode, current: Resolution): { framing: Framing; resolution: Resolution }\n```\n\nThe framing and art resolution of a view (PixelRenderer's canvas, or a HeadlessView's viewport):\n`computeFraming`, and the art size as a resolution. Identity is kept where nothing changed: the\n`current` object while the art size stays, else `base` itself when the art size equals it (a\nfixed or 16:9 view reports the preset it was given), else a new one.\n\n### interface Framing\n\n- `readonly artWidth: number`: Art (internal) resolution actually rendered. Equals `internal` in fixed mode.\n- `readonly artHeight: number`\n- `readonly scale: number`: Device pixels per art pixel. Integer whenever `integer` is true.\n- `readonly integer: boolean`: True when every art pixel maps to an integer block of device pixels.\n- `readonly canvasWidth: number`: Canvas backing-store size in device pixels.\n- `readonly canvasHeight: number`\n- `readonly cssWidth: number`: Canvas CSS size and offset inside the viewport, in CSS pixels.\n- `readonly cssHeight: number`\n- `readonly offsetX: number`\n- `readonly offsetY: number`\n\n### interface Game\n\nA game is a plain object. The engine owns rendering, physics stepping, camera,\nlighting and debug UI; the game only builds its world and reacts to input.\n\n- `readonly name: string`\n- `readonly args?: unknown`: The plain JSON the game was made with, when its name alone can't rebuild it (a lab's depth and seed, the door a room was entered by): kept in its recordings (`Recording.args`), so a tool can make the same game again to replay one (`npm run replay`, src/games.…\n- `readonly assets?: readonly string[]`: Models `setup` will load (`ctx.loadModel` URLs). Optional: listing them lets the engine start downloading while the renderer and physics initialise.\n- `setup(ctx: GameContext): Promise<void> | void`: Build the level: load models, add colliders, spawn entities.\n- `fixedUpdate?(ctx: GameContext, dt: number): void`: Runs before every fixed 60 Hz physics step. Move characters/apply forces here.\n- `ready?(ctx: GameContext): void`: Runs when the engine starts running this game: after `Engine.start` (just before `__PIXEL_READY__` resolves) and after each `loadGame`. Install page handles for tools here (`window.__MYGAME__`), so they never exist before the engine can step. An exception is…\n- `update?(ctx: GameContext, dt: number): void`: Runs once per rendered frame after physics (animation, pickups, UI state).\n- `cameraTarget(ctx: GameContext): Vector3`: World position the camera follows.\n- `eyePosition?(ctx: GameContext): Vector3`: First-person eye position (defaults to cameraTarget + 0.7 up).\n- `status?(ctx: GameContext): string`: One-line status for the debug UI (score, lives...).\n- `fingerprint?(ctx: GameContext): string`: The game's own state as a short string or hash, for `engine.fingerprint()`: what lives outside physics and `ctx.random` (positions, life, counters, the game's own random streams), so two runs that should match (a replay) can be compared. Only what a replay mu…\n- `snapshot?(ctx: GameContext): unknown`: The game's own state as plain data, between frames (`engine.snapshot()`): everything a later frame reads that the engine does not keep (its entities, timers, random streams; the handles of its physics bodies). With `restore`, the game has save-states.\n- `restore?(ctx: GameContext, state: unknown): void | Promise<void>`: Put the game back to what `snapshot` returned (`engine.restore()`): the same game, already set up. Find physics bodies again by handle (`ctx.physics.world.getRigidBody(h)`).\n- `beforeRestore?(ctx: GameContext): void | Promise<void>`: Optional: leave whatever mode the game is in before a restore changes anything (a mode with a camera of its own, a dialog, a level): `engine.restore()` calls it first, then checks the snapshot fits (the camera preset) and puts the engine's part back, then `re…\n- `onCameraChange?(ctx: GameContext): void`: Called after `engine.setCamera()` swapped the camera preset mid-game. The world, physics and player are untouched; adjust anything that depends on the preset (e.g. side-scroller lane lock, hiding the player model in first person).\n- `dispose?(ctx: GameContext): void`: Called when the game is unloaded (`engine.loadGame(other)` or `engine.dispose()`), before the engine removes its scene objects, physics bodies, triggers, particles, HUD and music. Free anything else the game owns: DOM, timers, listeners, HMR hooks.\n\n### interface GameContext\n\n- `readonly engine: Engine`\n- `readonly scene: Scene`\n- `readonly physics: Physics`\n- `readonly input: Input`\n- `readonly camera: CameraRig`\n- `readonly loadModel: typeof loadModel`\n- `readonly palette: typeof PALETTE`\n- `readonly time: number`: Game time in seconds since start. Frozen while paused or loading.\n- `readonly random: Rng`: This level's seeded randomness (src/engine/random.ts): the same engine seed and game give the same numbers. Use it (or forks of it) for everything random; never Math.random.\n- `readonly audio: AudioManager`: Sound effects, music, volumes (src/engine/audio).\n- `readonly particles: Particles`: Pixel particles: `particles.burst('dust', at)` (src/engine/particles).\n- `readonly hud: Hud`: Pixel HUD text and icons in art pixels (src/engine/hud).\n- `readonly lights: LightPool`: Dynamic point lights: `lights.request({ position, color, intensity, radius, flicker })` (src/engine/render/lights.ts). A fixed pool sized by quality, created on first use.\n- `readonly tweens: Tweens`: Tweens on game time: `tweens.to(door.position, { y: 3 }, { duration: 0.8, ease: 'outBack' })` (src/engine/tween.ts). Cleared when the level unloads.\n\n### interface HeadlessOptions\n","numLines":175,"startLine":132,"totalLines":5144}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_018kSrL84yBrMFCwevTRuVJV","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgke71chWQiSGL9BhcthZ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01F3bLXS7hbEqqXaA7Rwfn4a","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261105-162057__x4c6ec5/work/_research/engine/pixel-engine/API.md","offset":355,"limit":25},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11647,"cache_read_input_tokens":119147,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11647},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"66624378-a7bb-42a2-8c1d-fc63666b5654","uuid":"d414efb5-23bb-4d56-bf5a-d52ab5c5bfe1","timestamp":"2026-11-05T23:23:27.961Z","request_id":"req_011Cgke71P4H5ev6jrSqR8ke","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01F3bLXS7hbEqqXaA7Rwfn4a":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261105-162057__x4c6ec5/work/_research/engine/pixel-engine/API.md","offset":355,"limit":25}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01F3bLXS7hbEqqXaA7Rwfn4a","type":"tool_result","content":"355\tconst PIXEL_SIZES: readonly [\"fine\", \"chunky\"]\n356\t```\n357\t\n358\t### interface PlayerSettings\n359\t\n360\tThe player's settings: the standard picture and engine options every game gets, as plain data\n361\t(sound is the AudioManager's, which keeps its own volumes). A field left out is the game's own\n362\tchoice; a field set is the player's and wins over it. `engine.settings` holds them, applies\n363\tthem, saves them per game and shows them in the settings panel (the ⚙ button).\n364\t\n365\t- `look?: string`: A LOOK_PRESETS name. Left out: the look the game gives each level.\n366\t- `pixelArt?: boolean`: Pixel art (the pixel pass), or false for smooth 3D (render mode `raw`).\n367\t- `pixelSize?: 'fine' | 'chunky'`: The art resolution: `fine` (the game's, 480×270 by default) or `chunky` (two thirds of it: bigger pixels).\n368\t- `quality?: QualityOption`: Shadow and light quality: a level, or `auto` (the device's).\n369\t- `fps?: number`: Frame cap: 60, 30, or 0 for the display's own rate.\n370\t- `debugInfo?: boolean`: The debug panel: frame rate, camera, GPU errors and the game's status.\n371\t\n372\t### QUALITY\n373\t\n374\t```ts\n375\tconst QUALITY: Readonly<Record<QualityLevel, QualitySettings>>\n376\t```\n377\t\n378\tAt the iso preset's default zoom the shadow box spans ~30 world units, i.e. ~600 art\n379\tpixels: 512² is about one shadow texel per art pixel, 1024² is finer than the art can show."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"66624378-a7bb-42a2-8c1d-fc63666b5654","uuid":"67b615ec-60bb-4eb7-9d2f-34a29e1e040b","timestamp":"2026-11-05T23:23:27.972Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261105-162057__x4c6ec5/work/_research/engine/pixel-engine/API.md","content":"const PIXEL_SIZES: readonly [\"fine\", \"chunky\"]\n```\n\n### interface PlayerSettings\n\nThe player's settings: the standard picture and engine options every game gets, as plain data\n(sound is the AudioManager's, which keeps its own volumes). A field left out is the game's own\nchoice; a field set is the player's and wins over it. `engine.settings` holds them, applies\nthem, saves them per game and shows them in the settings panel (the ⚙ button).\n\n- `look?: string`: A LOOK_PRESETS name. Left out: the look the game gives each level.\n- `pixelArt?: boolean`: Pixel art (the pixel pass), or false for smooth 3D (render mode `raw`).\n- `pixelSize?: 'fine' | 'chunky'`: The art resolution: `fine` (the game's, 480×270 by default) or `chunky` (two thirds of it: bigger pixels).\n- `quality?: QualityOption`: Shadow and light quality: a level, or `auto` (the device's).\n- `fps?: number`: Frame cap: 60, 30, or 0 for the display's own rate.\n- `debugInfo?: boolean`: The debug panel: frame rate, camera, GPU errors and the game's status.\n\n### QUALITY\n\n```ts\nconst QUALITY: Readonly<Record<QualityLevel, QualitySettings>>\n```\n\nAt the iso preset's default zoom the shadow box spans ~30 world units, i.e. ~600 art\npixels: 512² is about one shadow texel per art pixel, 1024² is finer than the art can show.","numLines":25,"startLine":355,"totalLines":5144}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01F3bLXS7hbEqqXaA7Rwfn4a","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgke71chWQiSGL9BhcthZ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_012sL9VJ3Zybkv4vk96GqGmc","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261105-162057__x4c6ec5/work/_research/engine/pixel-engine/API.md","offset":460,"limit":225},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11647,"cache_read_input_tokens":119147,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11647},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"66624378-a7bb-42a2-8c1d-fc63666b5654","uuid":"aa495ccc-9285-4e97-9982-6e0bbbf7df71","timestamp":"2026-11-05T23:23:29.711Z","request_id":"req_011Cgke71P4H5ev6jrSqR8ke","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_012sL9VJ3Zybkv4vk96GqGmc":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261105-162057__x4c6ec5/work/_research/engine/pixel-engine/API.md","offset":460,"limit":225}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012sL9VJ3Zybkv4vk96GqGmc","type":"tool_result","content":"460\t### class Settings\n461\t\n462\t`engine.settings`: the player's settings, applied to the engine and saved. A field the page URL\n463\tsets (`?look=`, `?mode=`, `?res=`, `?quality=`, `?fps=`, `?debug=`: review flags) wins over a\n464\tsaved one at start (which stays saved). `open`, `close` and `toggle` show and hide the\n465\tsettings panel.\n466\t\n467\t- `new Settings(host: SettingsHost, key: string | null)`\n468\t- `readonly key: string | null`\n469\t- `get values: Readonly<PlayerSettings>`: The player's choices in effect: fields left out are the game's (or a review flag's).\n470\t- `get isOpen: boolean`\n471\t- `set(patch: PlayerSettings): void`: Change some settings: applied at once and saved. A field set to `undefined` goes back to the game's.\n472\t- `reset(): void`: Forget every choice, the held ones too: the game's own picture again.\n473\t- `replace(values: PlayerSettings): void`: Make `values` the settings, changing only the fields that differ: the ones it leaves out go back to the game's.\n474\t- `onChange(fn: (values: Readonly<PlayerSettings>) => void): () => void`: Called with the values after every change; returns the unsubscribe.\n475\t- `open(): void`\n476\t- `close(): void`\n477\t- `toggle(): void`\n478\t\n479\t### interface SettingsHost\n480\t\n481\tWhat the settings change in the engine (Engine implements it; tests pass a fake).\n482\t\n483\t- `readonly look: Look`\n484\t- `setLook(look: Look): void`\n485\t- `readonly renderer: { readonly mode: RenderMode; readonly baseResolution: Resolution; readonly lookVersion: number; setMode(mode: RenderMode): void; setResolution(resolution: Reso…`\n486\t- `maxFps: number`\n487\t- `readonly qualityOption: QualityOption`\n488\t- `useQuality(option: QualityOption): void`\n489\t- `readonly debugShown: boolean`\n490\t- `showDebug(on: boolean): void`\n491\t\n492\t### class SettingsPanel\n493\t\n494\tThe settings panel (DOM, like the debug panel: it stays legible at any art resolution) and the\n495\t⚙ button that opens it: the player's picture, sound and engine options (`engine.settings`,\n496\tsettings.ts; the AudioManager's volumes). The engine pauses the game while it is open. Keys\n497\tpressed in it never reach the game; Escape or Done closes it.\n498\t\n499\t- `new SettingsPanel(engine: Engine, container: HTMLElement, corner: TouchCorner | false)`\n500\t- `readonly root: HTMLDivElement`\n501\t- `readonly button: HTMLButtonElement | null`\n502\t- `show(): void`: Show the panel with the engine's current values. The panel takes the focus, not a control: a game key pressed out of habit (D, Space) must not pick an option; Tab reaches the controls.\n503\t- `hide(): void`\n504\t- `sync(): void`: Put the engine's current values into the controls, and into the debug panel (the loop fills it only while the game runs).\n505\t- `dispose(): void`\n506\t\n507\t### storeSettings()\n508\t\n509\t```ts\n510\tfunction storeSettings(key: string, settings: PlayerSettings): void\n511\t```\n512\t\n513\t### urlSettings()\n514\t\n515\t```ts\n516\tfunction urlSettings(url: UrlFlags, options: UrlFlags = url): Set<keyof PlayerSettings>\n517\t```\n518\t\n519\tThe settings a page URL's review flags set (`?look=`, `?filters=`, `?mode=`, `?res=`,\n520\t`?quality=`, `?fps=`, `?debug=`): the fields `url` (optionsFromUrl) holds that the game's\n521\t`options` took (`withUrlOptions`). They win over the saved ones at start.\n522\t\n523\t### interface Viewport\n524\t\n525\tA page's viewport: its size in CSS pixels and the device pixel ratio (a recording keeps it: `Recording.view`).\n526\t\n527\t- `readonly width: number`\n528\t- `readonly height: number`\n529\t- `readonly dpr: number`\n530\t\n531\t### withUrlOptions()\n532\t\n533\t```ts\n534\tfunction withUrlOptions(options: EngineOptions, search = location.search): EngineOptions\n535\t```\n536\t\n537\tA game's own options with the review flags from the page URL (optionsFromUrl) on top:\n538\t`Engine.start(game, withUrlOptions({ container, camera: { preset: 'side', zoom: 1.2 } }))`.\n539\tThe camera is merged key by key (`?zoom=1.5` keeps the game's `side` preset), and\n540\t`?filters=` replaces the game's look (a look would win over filters otherwise).\n541\t\n542\t## 2. Camera\n543\t\n544\t### CAMERA_PRESETS\n545\t\n546\t```ts\n547\tconst CAMERA_PRESETS: readonly CameraPreset[]\n548\t```\n549\t\n550\t### interface CameraConfig\n551\t\n552\t- `preset?: CameraPreset`\n553\t- `zoom?: number`: Zoom factor, 1 = preset default, >1 = closer. Ignored by `first`.\n554\t- `minZoom?: number`\n555\t- `maxZoom?: number`\n556\t- `viewHeight?: number`: Ortho presets: visible world height at zoom 1.\n557\t- `pitch?: number`: iso: degrees below horizon / around Y.\n558\t- `yaw?: number`\n559\t- `fov?: number`: Perspective presets: vertical field of view in degrees.\n560\t- `distance?: number`: third: orbit distance at zoom 1.\n561\t- `position?: [number, number, number]`: fixed: camera position and look-at target.\n562\t- `target?: [number, number, number]`\n563\t- `projection?: 'perspective' | 'ortho'`: fixed/free: projection.\n564\t- `stiffness?: number`: Follow smoothing; higher is snappier.\n565\t\n566\t### type CameraPreset\n567\t\n568\t```ts\n569\ttype CameraPreset = 'iso' | 'topdown' | 'side' | 'third' | 'first' | 'free' | 'fixed'\n570\t```\n571\t\n572\tCamera presets. A game picks ONE at startup (EngineOptions.camera / `?camera=`). For\n573\treviewing, `engine.setCamera()` swaps presets live without touching the world. `free`\n574\tis an authoring tool: fly around, press Enter to fix the view, and paste the printed\n575\t`fixed` config into your game.\n576\t\n577\t### class CameraRig\n578\t\n579\tBase rig: owns the three.js camera, zoom and the ground-plane movement basis.\n580\t\n581\t- `new CameraRig(config: CameraConfig)`\n582\t- `readonly preset: CameraPreset`\n583\t- `readonly camera: OrthographicCamera | PerspectiveCamera`\n584\t- `readonly focus: Vector3`: World point the rig is centred on (used for shadow framing).\n585\t- `zoom: number`\n586\t- `readonly minZoom: number`\n587\t- `readonly maxZoom: number`\n588\t- `readonly zoomable: boolean`: Whether zoom applies (all presets except first person).\n589\t- `readonly hidesTarget: boolean`: First person hides the player model.\n590\t- `readonly lockDepth: boolean`: Side-scroller: characters should stay on their depth lane.\n591\t- `controlsCharacter: boolean`: False while the free camera is flying (input drives the camera, not the player).\n592\t- `snap: boolean`: Pixel-snap the camera (ortho only).\n593\t- `aspect: number`: Viewport aspect (width / height), following the art resolution (see `setAspect`).\n594\t- `rayAt(x: number, y: number, origin: Vector3, dir: Vector3): void`: The ray through screen point (x, y) in normalised device coordinates (−1..1, y up, as `input.pointer` gives them): where it starts (on the near plane) and its unit direction. For picking things with the mouse, or aiming at it. An orthographic rig stands far b…\n595\t- `setZoom(zoom: number): void`\n596\t- `setAspect(aspect: number): void`: Match the art resolution's aspect (adaptive framing changes it with the screen). `update()` calls this from `CameraUpdate.resolution`; the engine also calls it on swap.\n597\t- `teleport(target: Vector3): void`: Place the camera without smoothing.\n598\t- `groundBasis(): { right: Vector3; forward: Vector3 }`: Screen-relative movement basis on the ground plane.\n599\t- `update(u: CameraUpdate): void`\n600\t- `describe(): Record<string, unknown>`: Serializable description (debug UI, `state()`).\n601\t- `state(): CameraRigState`: The rig as data, exact (`engine.snapshot()`).\n602\t- `setState(s: CameraRigState): void`: Put the rig back where `state()` found it (the same preset).\n603\t\n604\t### interface CameraRigState\n605\t\n606\tWhere a rig is and looks, as data (`engine.snapshot()`): the frame after a restore sees the camera the frame after the snapshot did.\n607\t\n608\t- `readonly preset: CameraPreset`\n609\t- `readonly focus: readonly number[]`\n610\t- `readonly zoom: number`\n611\t- `readonly aspect: number`\n612\t- `readonly position: readonly number[]`\n613\t- `readonly quaternion: readonly number[]`\n614\t- `readonly shaken: readonly number[]`\n615\t- `readonly own: Readonly<Record<string, number>>`: What a preset keeps of its own (an orbit's yaw, pitch and distance).\n616\t\n617\t### createCameraRig()\n618\t\n619\t```ts\n620\tfunction createCameraRig(config: CameraConfig = {}): CameraRig\n621\t```\n622\t\n623\t### class FirstPersonRig\n624\t\n625\tEyes of the character: mouse look (click to lock the pointer) or Q/E to turn. No zoom.\n626\t\n627\t- `new FirstPersonRig(config: CameraConfig = {})`\n628\t- `readonly preset: \"first\"`\n629\t- `readonly camera: PerspectiveCamera`\n630\t- `readonly zoomable: false`: Whether zoom applies (all presets except first person).\n631\t- `readonly hidesTarget: true`: First person hides the player model.\n632\t- `yaw: number`\n633\t- `pitch: number`\n634\t- `groundBasis(): { right: Vector3; forward: Vector3; }`: Screen-relative movement basis on the ground plane.\n635\t- `describe(): { yaw: number; pitch: number; }`: Serializable description (debug UI, `state()`).\n636\t\n637\t### class FreeRig\n638\t\n639\tAuthoring camera. While flying: WASD move, Q/E down/up, drag (or click to lock) to look,\n640\tShift = fast, wheel = field of view. Press Enter to fix it: the view freezes, the player\n641\tgets control, and `fixedConfig()` returns the config to hard-code (preset 'fixed').\n642\tEnter again unfixes.\n643\t\n644\t- `new FreeRig(preset: 'free' | 'fixed', config: CameraConfig = {})`\n645\t- `readonly preset: 'free' | 'fixed'`\n646\t- `readonly camera: PerspectiveCamera | OrthographicCamera`\n647\t- `fixed: boolean`\n648\t- `yaw: number`\n649\t- `pitch: number`\n650\t- `onFix: ((config: CameraConfig) => void) | null`\n651\t- `setFixed(fixed: boolean): void`: Freeze (or unfreeze) the view.\n652\t- `fixedConfig(): CameraConfig`\n653\t- `describe(): { fixed: boolean; config: CameraConfig; }`: Serializable description (debug UI, `state()`).\n654\t\n655\t### class OrthoRig\n656\t\n657\tOrthographic presets: classic 3/4 isometric, straight top-down, straight side-on.\n658\t\n659\t- `new OrthoRig(preset: 'iso' | 'topdown' | 'side', config: CameraConfig = {})`\n660\t- `readonly camera: OrthographicCamera`\n661\t- `readonly preset: 'iso' | 'topdown' | 'side'`\n662\t- `readonly lockDepth: boolean`: Side-scroller: characters should stay on their depth lane.\n663\t- `readonly baseViewHeight: number`\n664\t- `get viewHeight: number`\n665\t\n666\t### class ThirdPersonRig\n667\t\n668\tOver-the-shoulder orbit camera: mouse-drag or Q/E to orbit, wheel to zoom, avoids walls.\n669\t\n670\t- `new ThirdPersonRig(config: CameraConfig = {})`\n671\t- `readonly preset: \"third\"`\n672\t- `readonly camera: PerspectiveCamera`\n673\t- `yaw: number`\n674\t- `pitch: number`\n675\t- `describe(): { yaw: number; pitch: number; }`: Serializable description (debug UI, `state()`).\n676\t\n677\t## 3. Rendering, looks and filters\n678\t\n679\t### AMOUNT\n680\t\n681\t```ts\n682\tconst AMOUNT: FilterParam\n683\t```\n684\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"66624378-a7bb-42a2-8c1d-fc63666b5654","uuid":"e26194a6-ad3b-49a4-81bb-6725b4ca9da8","timestamp":"2026-11-05T23:23:29.721Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261105-162057__x4c6ec5/work/_research/engine/pixel-engine/API.md","content":"### class Settings\n\n`engine.settings`: the player's settings, applied to the engine and saved. A field the page URL\nsets (`?look=`, `?mode=`, `?res=`, `?quality=`, `?fps=`, `?debug=`: review flags) wins over a\nsaved one at start (which stays saved). `open`, `close` and `toggle` show and hide the\nsettings panel.\n\n- `new Settings(host: SettingsHost, key: string | null)`\n- `readonly key: string | null`\n- `get values: Readonly<PlayerSettings>`: The player's choices in effect: fields left out are the game's (or a review flag's).\n- `get isOpen: boolean`\n- `set(patch: PlayerSettings): void`: Change some settings: applied at once and saved. A field set to `undefined` goes back to the game's.\n- `reset(): void`: Forget every choice, the held ones too: the game's own picture again.\n- `replace(values: PlayerSettings): void`: Make `values` the settings, changing only the fields that differ: the ones it leaves out go back to the game's.\n- `onChange(fn: (values: Readonly<PlayerSettings>) => void): () => void`: Called with the values after every change; returns the unsubscribe.\n- `open(): void`\n- `close(): void`\n- `toggle(): void`\n\n### interface SettingsHost\n\nWhat the settings change in the engine (Engine implements it; tests pass a fake).\n\n- `readonly look: Look`\n- `setLook(look: Look): void`\n- `readonly renderer: { readonly mode: RenderMode; readonly baseResolution: Resolution; readonly lookVersion: number; setMode(mode: RenderMode): void; setResolution(resolution: Reso…`\n- `maxFps: number`\n- `readonly qualityOption: QualityOption`\n- `useQuality(option: QualityOption): void`\n- `readonly debugShown: boolean`\n- `showDebug(on: boolean): void`\n\n### class SettingsPanel\n\nThe settings panel (DOM, like the debug panel: it stays legible at any art resolution) and the\n⚙ button that opens it: the player's picture, sound and engine options (`engine.settings`,\nsettings.ts; the AudioManager's volumes). The engine pauses the game while it is open. Keys\npressed in it never reach the game; Escape or Done closes it.\n\n- `new SettingsPanel(engine: Engine, container: HTMLElement, corner: TouchCorner | false)`\n- `readonly root: HTMLDivElement`\n- `readonly button: HTMLButtonElement | null`\n- `show(): void`: Show the panel with the engine's current values. The panel takes the focus, not a control: a game key pressed out of habit (D, Space) must not pick an option; Tab reaches the controls.\n- `hide(): void`\n- `sync(): void`: Put the engine's current values into the controls, and into the debug panel (the loop fills it only while the game runs).\n- `dispose(): void`\n\n### storeSettings()\n\n```ts\nfunction storeSettings(key: string, settings: PlayerSettings): void\n```\n\n### urlSettings()\n\n```ts\nfunction urlSettings(url: UrlFlags, options: UrlFlags = url): Set<keyof PlayerSettings>\n```\n\nThe settings a page URL's review flags set (`?look=`, `?filters=`, `?mode=`, `?res=`,\n`?quality=`, `?fps=`, `?debug=`): the fields `url` (optionsFromUrl) holds that the game's\n`options` took (`withUrlOptions`). They win over the saved ones at start.\n\n### interface Viewport\n\nA page's viewport: its size in CSS pixels and the device pixel ratio (a recording keeps it: `Recording.view`).\n\n- `readonly width: number`\n- `readonly height: number`\n- `readonly dpr: number`\n\n### withUrlOptions()\n\n```ts\nfunction withUrlOptions(options: EngineOptions, search = location.search): EngineOptions\n```\n\nA game's own options with the review flags from the page URL (optionsFromUrl) on top:\n`Engine.start(game, withUrlOptions({ container, camera: { preset: 'side', zoom: 1.2 } }))`.\nThe camera is merged key by key (`?zoom=1.5` keeps the game's `side` preset), and\n`?filters=` replaces the game's look (a look would win over filters otherwise).\n\n## 2. Camera\n\n### CAMERA_PRESETS\n\n```ts\nconst CAMERA_PRESETS: readonly CameraPreset[]\n```\n\n### interface CameraConfig\n\n- `preset?: CameraPreset`\n- `zoom?: number`: Zoom factor, 1 = preset default, >1 = closer. Ignored by `first`.\n- `minZoom?: number`\n- `maxZoom?: number`\n- `viewHeight?: number`: Ortho presets: visible world height at zoom 1.\n- `pitch?: number`: iso: degrees below horizon / around Y.\n- `yaw?: number`\n- `fov?: number`: Perspective presets: vertical field of view in degrees.\n- `distance?: number`: third: orbit distance at zoom 1.\n- `position?: [number, number, number]`: fixed: camera position and look-at target.\n- `target?: [number, number, number]`\n- `projection?: 'perspective' | 'ortho'`: fixed/free: projection.\n- `stiffness?: number`: Follow smoothing; higher is snappier.\n\n### type CameraPreset\n\n```ts\ntype CameraPreset = 'iso' | 'topdown' | 'side' | 'third' | 'first' | 'free' | 'fixed'\n```\n\nCamera presets. A game picks ONE at startup (EngineOptions.camera / `?camera=`). For\nreviewing, `engine.setCamera()` swaps presets live without touching the world. `free`\nis an authoring tool: fly around, press Enter to fix the view, and paste the printed\n`fixed` config into your game.\n\n### class CameraRig\n\nBase rig: owns the three.js camera, zoom and the ground-plane movement basis.\n\n- `new CameraRig(config: CameraConfig)`\n- `readonly preset: CameraPreset`\n- `readonly camera: OrthographicCamera | PerspectiveCamera`\n- `readonly focus: Vector3`: World point the rig is centred on (used for shadow framing).\n- `zoom: number`\n- `readonly minZoom: number`\n- `readonly maxZoom: number`\n- `readonly zoomable: boolean`: Whether zoom applies (all presets except first person).\n- `readonly hidesTarget: boolean`: First person hides the player model.\n- `readonly lockDepth: boolean`: Side-scroller: characters should stay on their depth lane.\n- `controlsCharacter: boolean`: False while the free camera is flying (input drives the camera, not the player).\n- `snap: boolean`: Pixel-snap the camera (ortho only).\n- `aspect: number`: Viewport aspect (width / height), following the art resolution (see `setAspect`).\n- `rayAt(x: number, y: number, origin: Vector3, dir: Vector3): void`: The ray through screen point (x, y) in normalised device coordinates (−1..1, y up, as `input.pointer` gives them): where it starts (on the near plane) and its unit direction. For picking things with the mouse, or aiming at it. An orthographic rig stands far b…\n- `setZoom(zoom: number): void`\n- `setAspect(aspect: number): void`: Match the art resolution's aspect (adaptive framing changes it with the screen). `update()` calls this from `CameraUpdate.resolution`; the engine also calls it on swap.\n- `teleport(target: Vector3): void`: Place the camera without smoothing.\n- `groundBasis(): { right: Vector3; forward: Vector3 }`: Screen-relative movement basis on the ground plane.\n- `update(u: CameraUpdate): void`\n- `describe(): Record<string, unknown>`: Serializable description (debug UI, `state()`).\n- `state(): CameraRigState`: The rig as data, exact (`engine.snapshot()`).\n- `setState(s: CameraRigState): void`: Put the rig back where `state()` found it (the same preset).\n\n### interface CameraRigState\n\nWhere a rig is and looks, as data (`engine.snapshot()`): the frame after a restore sees the camera the frame after the snapshot did.\n\n- `readonly preset: CameraPreset`\n- `readonly focus: readonly number[]`\n- `readonly zoom: number`\n- `readonly aspect: number`\n- `readonly position: readonly number[]`\n- `readonly quaternion: readonly number[]`\n- `readonly shaken: readonly number[]`\n- `readonly own: Readonly<Record<string, number>>`: What a preset keeps of its own (an orbit's yaw, pitch and distance).\n\n### createCameraRig()\n\n```ts\nfunction createCameraRig(config: CameraConfig = {}): CameraRig\n```\n\n### class FirstPersonRig\n\nEyes of the character: mouse look (click to lock the pointer) or Q/E to turn. No zoom.\n\n- `new FirstPersonRig(config: CameraConfig = {})`\n- `readonly preset: \"first\"`\n- `readonly camera: PerspectiveCamera`\n- `readonly zoomable: false`: Whether zoom applies (all presets except first person).\n- `readonly hidesTarget: true`: First person hides the player model.\n- `yaw: number`\n- `pitch: number`\n- `groundBasis(): { right: Vector3; forward: Vector3; }`: Screen-relative movement basis on the ground plane.\n- `describe(): { yaw: number; pitch: number; }`: Serializable description (debug UI, `state()`).\n\n### class FreeRig\n\nAuthoring camera. While flying: WASD move, Q/E down/up, drag (or click to lock) to look,\nShift = fast, wheel = field of view. Press Enter to fix it: the view freezes, the player\ngets control, and `fixedConfig()` returns the config to hard-code (preset 'fixed').\nEnter again unfixes.\n\n- `new FreeRig(preset: 'free' | 'fixed', config: CameraConfig = {})`\n- `readonly preset: 'free' | 'fixed'`\n- `readonly camera: PerspectiveCamera | OrthographicCamera`\n- `fixed: boolean`\n- `yaw: number`\n- `pitch: number`\n- `onFix: ((config: CameraConfig) => void) | null`\n- `setFixed(fixed: boolean): void`: Freeze (or unfreeze) the view.\n- `fixedConfig(): CameraConfig`\n- `describe(): { fixed: boolean; config: CameraConfig; }`: Serializable description (debug UI, `state()`).\n\n### class OrthoRig\n\nOrthographic presets: classic 3/4 isometric, straight top-down, straight side-on.\n\n- `new OrthoRig(preset: 'iso' | 'topdown' | 'side', config: CameraConfig = {})`\n- `readonly camera: OrthographicCamera`\n- `readonly preset: 'iso' | 'topdown' | 'side'`\n- `readonly lockDepth: boolean`: Side-scroller: characters should stay on their depth lane.\n- `readonly baseViewHeight: number`\n- `get viewHeight: number`\n\n### class ThirdPersonRig\n\nOver-the-shoulder orbit camera: mouse-drag or Q/E to orbit, wheel to zoom, avoids walls.\n\n- `new ThirdPersonRig(config: CameraConfig = {})`\n- `readonly preset: \"third\"`\n- `readonly camera: PerspectiveCamera`\n- `yaw: number`\n- `pitch: number`\n- `describe(): { yaw: number; pitch: number; }`: Serializable description (debug UI, `state()`).\n\n## 3. Rendering, looks and filters\n\n### AMOUNT\n\n```ts\nconst AMOUNT: FilterParam\n```\n","numLines":225,"startLine":460,"totalLines":5144}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_012sL9VJ3Zybkv4vk96GqGmc","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgke71chWQiSGL9BhcthZ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Hj8nRUWrwHz62QCSim33dd","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261105-162057__x4c6ec5/work/_research/engine/pixel-engine/API.md","offset":2460,"limit":205},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11647,"cache_read_input_tokens":119147,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11647},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"66624378-a7bb-42a2-8c1d-fc63666b5654","uuid":"7bc630cd-0dd3-405c-bff8-8be924486388","timestamp":"2026-11-05T23:23:31.422Z","request_id":"req_011Cgke71P4H5ev6jrSqR8ke","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01Hj8nRUWrwHz62QCSim33dd":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261105-162057__x4c6ec5/work/_research/engine/pixel-engine/API.md","offset":2460,"limit":205}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Hj8nRUWrwHz62QCSim33dd","type":"tool_result","content":"2460\tVolume at `distance` (m): 1 inside `ref`, then inverse distance, fading to 0 at `max`.\n2461\t\n2462\t### class AudioManager\n2463\t\n2464\tWeb Audio for games: procedural retro sound effects (`SFX`, data), a small chiptune\n2465\tsequencer (`Song`, data), optional audio files, and master / sfx / music volume + mute\n2466\tpersisted in localStorage. Available as `ctx.audio`.\n2467\t\n2468\tBrowsers only allow sound after a user gesture, so the AudioContext is created on the\n2469\tfirst key / pointer / touch input. Before that, and wherever Web Audio does not exist\n2470\t(headless tests, old browsers), every call is a silent no-op that still records what\n2471\twas played in `log`, so games behave identically and tests can assert on sounds.\n2472\t\n2473\t- `new AudioManager(target: EventTarget | null = typeof window !== 'undefined' ? window : null)`\n2474\t- `static get supported: boolean`: Web Audio exists in this environment.\n2475\t- `readonly sounds: Map<string, SoundDef>`: Sound registry: the built-in SFX plus anything registered or loaded.\n2476\t- `readonly log: { name: string; at: number }[]`: The most recent plays (name and game-independent wall time), newest last; tooling/tests.\n2477\t- `readonly counts: Record<string, number>`: How many times each sound was requested.\n2478\t- `context: AudioContext | null`\n2479\t- `get unlocked: boolean`: True once the AudioContext is running (after the first user input).\n2480\t- `get volume: Readonly<AudioSettings>`: Current volumes and mute (persisted).\n2481\t- `get muted: boolean`\n2482\t- `get playing: string`: Name of the playing song ('' when none).\n2483\t- `unlock(): void`: Create / resume the AudioContext. Called automatically on the first user input; call it yourself from a click handler if you start sound from your own UI.\n2484\t- `register(name: string, def: SoundDef): void`: Add or replace a sound effect (data, see SoundDef).\n2485\t- `play(sound: string | SoundDef, options: PlayOptions = {}): boolean`: Play a sound effect by name (built-in SFX, `register`ed or `load`ed) or directly from a SoundDef. Returns false when nothing could be heard (locked, muted, no Web Audio).\n2486\t- `loop(sound: string | SoundDef, settings: VoiceSettings = {}): Voice`: Start a sound looping, as a voice you steer while it plays (volume, pan, muffle, pitch: with `spatialize` it sits at a place in the world). Before the first user input, or with no Web Audio, the voice keeps its settings silently and starts once audio unlocks.…\n2487\t- `get loops: readonly Voice[]`: Voices playing or waiting to (tooling and tests).\n2488\t- `stopLoops(): void`: Stop every looping voice (the engine does this when a level unloads).\n2489\t- `load(name: string, url: string): Promise<void>`: Load an audio file (wav/ogg/mp3…) under `name`; `play(name)` then plays it. Works before the first user input too: the file is decoded once audio unlocks.\n2490\t- `playMusic(song: Song | null, name = 'song'): void`: Start a song (looping by default), replacing the current one. `null` stops music.\n2491\t- `stopMusic(): void`\n2492\t- `persist: boolean`: Volume changes are written to storage. The engine turns this off while a replay plays: the recording sets the volumes it set, nothing is written, and the player's come back after.\n2493\t- `setVolume(channel: VolumeChannel, value: number): void`\n2494\t- `setMuted(muted: boolean): void`\n2495\t- `toggleMute(): boolean`\n2496\t- `resetVolumes(): void`: The default volumes, sound on (the settings panel's Reset).\n2497\t- `update(): void`: Engine: once per rendered frame. Schedules the next notes of the song.\n2498\t- `dispose(): void`: Stop everything and close the AudioContext.\n2499\t\n2500\t### interface AudioSettings\n2501\t\n2502\t- `master: number`\n2503\t- `sfx: number`\n2504\t- `music: number`\n2505\t- `muted: boolean`\n2506\t\n2507\t### type Instrument\n2508\t\n2509\t```ts\n2510\ttype Instrument = Omit<SoundDef, 'freq' | 'freqEnd' | 'sustain' | 'delay'>\n2511\t```\n2512\t\n2513\tA track's instrument: a SoundDef without pitch or length (each note sets those).\n2514\t\n2515\t### interface Listener\n2516\t\n2517\t- `position: V3Like`\n2518\t- `right: V3Like`: The listener's right, in world space (unit): the camera's right for a third-person game.\n2519\t\n2520\t### muffleCutoff\n2521\t\n2522\t```ts\n2523\tconst muffleCutoff: (muffle: number) => number\n2524\t```\n2525\t\n2526\tLow-pass cutoff (Hz) for a muffle of 0..1: wide open to a dull thud.\n2527\t\n2528\t### noteFrequency()\n2529\t\n2530\t```ts\n2531\tfunction noteFrequency(note: string): number | null\n2532\t```\n2533\t\n2534\t'A4' → 440, 'C#5', 'Eb3'… Returns null for anything that is not a note.\n2535\t\n2536\t### parseSong()\n2537\t\n2538\t```ts\n2539\tfunction parseSong(song: Song): ParsedSong\n2540\t```\n2541\t\n2542\tFlatten a song into timed notes. Throws on malformed patterns, so tests catch typos.\n2543\t\n2544\t### PLAYGROUND_SONG\n2545\t\n2546\t```ts\n2547\tconst PLAYGROUND_SONG: Song\n2548\t```\n2549\t\n2550\tThe playground's short looping track: C major, bouncy, 2 × 16 steps.\n2551\t\n2552\t### interface PlayOptions\n2553\t\n2554\t- `volume?: number`: 0..1 multiplier on the sound's own volume.\n2555\t- `pitch?: number`: Pitch shift in semitones (playback rate), e.g. a rising combo.\n2556\t- `pan?: number`: Stereo position -1 (left) .. 1 (right).\n2557\t\n2558\t### renderSound()\n2559\t\n2560\t```ts\n2561\tfunction renderSound(def: SoundDef, sampleRate = 44100, seed = 1): Float32Array<ArrayBuffer>\n2562\t```\n2563\t\n2564\tRender a sound to mono samples in -1..1.\n2565\t\n2566\t### SFX\n2567\t\n2568\t```ts\n2569\tconst SFX: { jump: { wave: \"square\"; duty: number; freq: number; freqEnd: number; attack: number; sustain: number; decay: number; volume: number; }; doubleJump: { wave: \"square\"; duty: number; freq: number; freqEnd: number; ... 5 more ...; volume: number; }; ... 8 more ...; fanfare: { ...; }; }\n2570\t```\n2571\t\n2572\tBuilt-in retro sound effects, as data. Tweak numbers here (or register your own with\n2573\t`ctx.audio.register(name, def)`) and every `ctx.audio.play(name)` picks them up.\n2574\tSee docs/engine/audio.md for the SoundDef fields.\n2575\t\n2576\t### type SfxName\n2577\t\n2578\t```ts\n2579\ttype SfxName = keyof typeof SFX\n2580\t```\n2581\t\n2582\t### interface Song\n2583\t\n2584\tA chiptune song as data. Each pattern holds one string per track: space-separated steps,\n2585\twhere a step is a note ('C4', 'F#5', 'Bb2'), '.' (rest) or '-' (hold the previous note).\n2586\tEvery track of a pattern must have the same number of steps. For `noise` tracks the\n2587\tnote's pitch is the noise rate: C6 is a snare-ish hiss, C8 a hi-hat tick.\n2588\t\n2589\t- `bpm: number`\n2590\t- `stepsPerBeat?: number`: Steps per beat (4 = sixteenth notes). Default 4.\n2591\t- `tracks: Record<string, Instrument>`\n2592\t- `patterns: Record<string, Record<string, string>>`\n2593\t- `order: readonly string[]`: Pattern names in play order.\n2594\t- `loop?: boolean`: Loop forever (default true).\n2595\t\n2596\t### interface SoundDef\n2597\t\n2598\t- `wave: Wave`\n2599\t- `freq: number`: Start pitch in Hz. For `noise` it is the sample-and-hold rate (higher = hissier).\n2600\t- `freqEnd?: number`: End pitch for a sweep (exponential glide from `freq`).\n2601\t- `sweepTime?: number`: Seconds the sweep takes (default: the whole sound).\n2602\t- `duty?: number`: Square duty cycle: 0.125, 0.25 or 0.5 are the classic NES widths. Default 0.5.\n2603\t- `attack?: number`: Envelope (seconds): ramp up, hold at full volume, then fade out.\n2604\t- `sustain?: number`\n2605\t- `decay?: number`\n2606\t- `volume?: number`: Peak level 0..1 (default 0.5).\n2607\t- `arp?: readonly number[]`: Arpeggio: semitone offsets stepped every `arpRate` seconds (the coin \"ba-ding\").\n2608\t- `arpRate?: number`\n2609\t- `vibrato?: { depth: number; rate: number }`: Vibrato depth in semitones and rate in Hz.\n2610\t- `crush?: number`: Quantize to this many levels (4-bit style crunch). 0 = off.\n2611\t- `delay?: number`: Seconds of silence before the sound starts (for layers).\n2612\t- `layers?: readonly SoundDef[]`: Extra sounds mixed in (e.g. a noise burst under a thump).\n2613\t\n2614\t### interface Spatial\n2615\t\n2616\t- `gain: number`: 0..1 volume.\n2617\t- `pan: number`: -1 (left) .. 1 (right).\n2618\t- `distance: number`\n2619\t\n2620\t### spatialize()\n2621\t\n2622\t```ts\n2623\tfunction spatialize(listener: Listener, at: V3Like, o: SpatialOptions = {}): Spatial\n2624\t```\n2625\t\n2626\tGain and pan of a sound at `at` for `listener`.\n2627\t\n2628\t### interface SpatialOptions\n2629\t\n2630\t- `ref?: number`: Within this distance (m) the sound is at full volume (default 1).\n2631\t- `max?: number`: Silent from this distance on (default 30); it fades out over the last fifth.\n2632\t- `rolloff?: number`: How fast it falls off past `ref` (default 1: inverse distance, the way sound really does).\n2633\t\n2634\t### interface Voice\n2635\t\n2636\tA looping sound steered while it plays (`AudioManager.loop`): a hum, a fire, a waterfall.\n2637\t\n2638\t- `readonly name: string`\n2639\t- `readonly settings: Readonly<Required<VoiceSettings>>`: The values last set (kept headless too, so games and tests can read them).\n2640\t- `readonly sounding: boolean`: Really sounding: Web Audio running and the loop started.\n2641\t- `readonly stopped: boolean`\n2642\t- `set(s: VoiceSettings): void`: Change any of its settings; they glide over a few hundredths of a second (no clicks).\n2643\t- `stop(): void`\n2644\t\n2645\t### interface VoiceSettings\n2646\t\n2647\t- `volume?: number`: 0..1 (default 1).\n2648\t- `pan?: number`: Stereo position -1 (left) .. 1 (right).\n2649\t- `muffle?: number`: 0 (clear) .. 1 (behind a thick wall, under water): a low-pass filter closing down.\n2650\t- `pitch?: number`: Pitch shift in semitones.\n2651\t\n2652\t### type VolumeChannel\n2653\t\n2654\t```ts\n2655\ttype VolumeChannel = 'master' | 'sfx' | 'music'\n2656\t```\n2657\t\n2658\t### type Wave\n2659\t\n2660\t```ts\n2661\ttype Wave = 'square' | 'triangle' | 'saw' | 'sine' | 'noise'\n2662\t```\n2663\t\n2664\tRetro sound synthesis as pure functions (no Web Audio): a sound is data (`SoundDef`)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"66624378-a7bb-42a2-8c1d-fc63666b5654","uuid":"7328e2e2-60f1-48ea-b4d4-f127a6da43b1","timestamp":"2026-11-05T23:23:31.432Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261105-162057__x4c6ec5/work/_research/engine/pixel-engine/API.md","content":"Volume at `distance` (m): 1 inside `ref`, then inverse distance, fading to 0 at `max`.\n\n### class AudioManager\n\nWeb Audio for games: procedural retro sound effects (`SFX`, data), a small chiptune\nsequencer (`Song`, data), optional audio files, and master / sfx / music volume + mute\npersisted in localStorage. Available as `ctx.audio`.\n\nBrowsers only allow sound after a user gesture, so the AudioContext is created on the\nfirst key / pointer / touch input. Before that, and wherever Web Audio does not exist\n(headless tests, old browsers), every call is a silent no-op that still records what\nwas played in `log`, so games behave identically and tests can assert on sounds.\n\n- `new AudioManager(target: EventTarget | null = typeof window !== 'undefined' ? window : null)`\n- `static get supported: boolean`: Web Audio exists in this environment.\n- `readonly sounds: Map<string, SoundDef>`: Sound registry: the built-in SFX plus anything registered or loaded.\n- `readonly log: { name: string; at: number }[]`: The most recent plays (name and game-independent wall time), newest last; tooling/tests.\n- `readonly counts: Record<string, number>`: How many times each sound was requested.\n- `context: AudioContext | null`\n- `get unlocked: boolean`: True once the AudioContext is running (after the first user input).\n- `get volume: Readonly<AudioSettings>`: Current volumes and mute (persisted).\n- `get muted: boolean`\n- `get playing: string`: Name of the playing song ('' when none).\n- `unlock(): void`: Create / resume the AudioContext. Called automatically on the first user input; call it yourself from a click handler if you start sound from your own UI.\n- `register(name: string, def: SoundDef): void`: Add or replace a sound effect (data, see SoundDef).\n- `play(sound: string | SoundDef, options: PlayOptions = {}): boolean`: Play a sound effect by name (built-in SFX, `register`ed or `load`ed) or directly from a SoundDef. Returns false when nothing could be heard (locked, muted, no Web Audio).\n- `loop(sound: string | SoundDef, settings: VoiceSettings = {}): Voice`: Start a sound looping, as a voice you steer while it plays (volume, pan, muffle, pitch: with `spatialize` it sits at a place in the world). Before the first user input, or with no Web Audio, the voice keeps its settings silently and starts once audio unlocks.…\n- `get loops: readonly Voice[]`: Voices playing or waiting to (tooling and tests).\n- `stopLoops(): void`: Stop every looping voice (the engine does this when a level unloads).\n- `load(name: string, url: string): Promise<void>`: Load an audio file (wav/ogg/mp3…) under `name`; `play(name)` then plays it. Works before the first user input too: the file is decoded once audio unlocks.\n- `playMusic(song: Song | null, name = 'song'): void`: Start a song (looping by default), replacing the current one. `null` stops music.\n- `stopMusic(): void`\n- `persist: boolean`: Volume changes are written to storage. The engine turns this off while a replay plays: the recording sets the volumes it set, nothing is written, and the player's come back after.\n- `setVolume(channel: VolumeChannel, value: number): void`\n- `setMuted(muted: boolean): void`\n- `toggleMute(): boolean`\n- `resetVolumes(): void`: The default volumes, sound on (the settings panel's Reset).\n- `update(): void`: Engine: once per rendered frame. Schedules the next notes of the song.\n- `dispose(): void`: Stop everything and close the AudioContext.\n\n### interface AudioSettings\n\n- `master: number`\n- `sfx: number`\n- `music: number`\n- `muted: boolean`\n\n### type Instrument\n\n```ts\ntype Instrument = Omit<SoundDef, 'freq' | 'freqEnd' | 'sustain' | 'delay'>\n```\n\nA track's instrument: a SoundDef without pitch or length (each note sets those).\n\n### interface Listener\n\n- `position: V3Like`\n- `right: V3Like`: The listener's right, in world space (unit): the camera's right for a third-person game.\n\n### muffleCutoff\n\n```ts\nconst muffleCutoff: (muffle: number) => number\n```\n\nLow-pass cutoff (Hz) for a muffle of 0..1: wide open to a dull thud.\n\n### noteFrequency()\n\n```ts\nfunction noteFrequency(note: string): number | null\n```\n\n'A4' → 440, 'C#5', 'Eb3'… Returns null for anything that is not a note.\n\n### parseSong()\n\n```ts\nfunction parseSong(song: Song): ParsedSong\n```\n\nFlatten a song into timed notes. Throws on malformed patterns, so tests catch typos.\n\n### PLAYGROUND_SONG\n\n```ts\nconst PLAYGROUND_SONG: Song\n```\n\nThe playground's short looping track: C major, bouncy, 2 × 16 steps.\n\n### interface PlayOptions\n\n- `volume?: number`: 0..1 multiplier on the sound's own volume.\n- `pitch?: number`: Pitch shift in semitones (playback rate), e.g. a rising combo.\n- `pan?: number`: Stereo position -1 (left) .. 1 (right).\n\n### renderSound()\n\n```ts\nfunction renderSound(def: SoundDef, sampleRate = 44100, seed = 1): Float32Array<ArrayBuffer>\n```\n\nRender a sound to mono samples in -1..1.\n\n### SFX\n\n```ts\nconst SFX: { jump: { wave: \"square\"; duty: number; freq: number; freqEnd: number; attack: number; sustain: number; decay: number; volume: number; }; doubleJump: { wave: \"square\"; duty: number; freq: number; freqEnd: number; ... 5 more ...; volume: number; }; ... 8 more ...; fanfare: { ...; }; }\n```\n\nBuilt-in retro sound effects, as data. Tweak numbers here (or register your own with\n`ctx.audio.register(name, def)`) and every `ctx.audio.play(name)` picks them up.\nSee docs/engine/audio.md for the SoundDef fields.\n\n### type SfxName\n\n```ts\ntype SfxName = keyof typeof SFX\n```\n\n### interface Song\n\nA chiptune song as data. Each pattern holds one string per track: space-separated steps,\nwhere a step is a note ('C4', 'F#5', 'Bb2'), '.' (rest) or '-' (hold the previous note).\nEvery track of a pattern must have the same number of steps. For `noise` tracks the\nnote's pitch is the noise rate: C6 is a snare-ish hiss, C8 a hi-hat tick.\n\n- `bpm: number`\n- `stepsPerBeat?: number`: Steps per beat (4 = sixteenth notes). Default 4.\n- `tracks: Record<string, Instrument>`\n- `patterns: Record<string, Record<string, string>>`\n- `order: readonly string[]`: Pattern names in play order.\n- `loop?: boolean`: Loop forever (default true).\n\n### interface SoundDef\n\n- `wave: Wave`\n- `freq: number`: Start pitch in Hz. For `noise` it is the sample-and-hold rate (higher = hissier).\n- `freqEnd?: number`: End pitch for a sweep (exponential glide from `freq`).\n- `sweepTime?: number`: Seconds the sweep takes (default: the whole sound).\n- `duty?: number`: Square duty cycle: 0.125, 0.25 or 0.5 are the classic NES widths. Default 0.5.\n- `attack?: number`: Envelope (seconds): ramp up, hold at full volume, then fade out.\n- `sustain?: number`\n- `decay?: number`\n- `volume?: number`: Peak level 0..1 (default 0.5).\n- `arp?: readonly number[]`: Arpeggio: semitone offsets stepped every `arpRate` seconds (the coin \"ba-ding\").\n- `arpRate?: number`\n- `vibrato?: { depth: number; rate: number }`: Vibrato depth in semitones and rate in Hz.\n- `crush?: number`: Quantize to this many levels (4-bit style crunch). 0 = off.\n- `delay?: number`: Seconds of silence before the sound starts (for layers).\n- `layers?: readonly SoundDef[]`: Extra sounds mixed in (e.g. a noise burst under a thump).\n\n### interface Spatial\n\n- `gain: number`: 0..1 volume.\n- `pan: number`: -1 (left) .. 1 (right).\n- `distance: number`\n\n### spatialize()\n\n```ts\nfunction spatialize(listener: Listener, at: V3Like, o: SpatialOptions = {}): Spatial\n```\n\nGain and pan of a sound at `at` for `listener`.\n\n### interface SpatialOptions\n\n- `ref?: number`: Within this distance (m) the sound is at full volume (default 1).\n- `max?: number`: Silent from this distance on (default 30); it fades out over the last fifth.\n- `rolloff?: number`: How fast it falls off past `ref` (default 1: inverse distance, the way sound really does).\n\n### interface Voice\n\nA looping sound steered while it plays (`AudioManager.loop`): a hum, a fire, a waterfall.\n\n- `readonly name: string`\n- `readonly settings: Readonly<Required<VoiceSettings>>`: The values last set (kept headless too, so games and tests can read them).\n- `readonly sounding: boolean`: Really sounding: Web Audio running and the loop started.\n- `readonly stopped: boolean`\n- `set(s: VoiceSettings): void`: Change any of its settings; they glide over a few hundredths of a second (no clicks).\n- `stop(): void`\n\n### interface VoiceSettings\n\n- `volume?: number`: 0..1 (default 1).\n- `pan?: number`: Stereo position -1 (left) .. 1 (right).\n- `muffle?: number`: 0 (clear) .. 1 (behind a thick wall, under water): a low-pass filter closing down.\n- `pitch?: number`: Pitch shift in semitones.\n\n### type VolumeChannel\n\n```ts\ntype VolumeChannel = 'master' | 'sfx' | 'music'\n```\n\n### type Wave\n\n```ts\ntype Wave = 'square' | 'triangle' | 'saw' | 'sine' | 'noise'\n```\n\nRetro sound synthesis as pure functions (no Web Audio): a sound is data (`SoundDef`)","numLines":205,"startLine":2460,"totalLines":5144}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01Hj8nRUWrwHz62QCSim33dd","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
