{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgkiGwzuKpCL5heBuUvyb","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01UobFMawBQ6FGXzHphNaGCY","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261105-162057__x4c6ec5/work/PLAN.md","offset":1,"limit":100},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2634,"cache_read_input_tokens":567816,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2634},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"66624378-a7bb-42a2-8c1d-fc63666b5654","uuid":"39d442ba-4186-4147-8331-2b61a5f4ee7f","timestamp":"2026-11-06T00:16:48.128Z","request_id":"req_011CgkiGwZMi77NbA31MATxK","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01UobFMawBQ6FGXzHphNaGCY":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261105-162057__x4c6ec5/work/PLAN.md","offset":1,"limit":100}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01UobFMawBQ6FGXzHphNaGCY","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# PLAN: a racing life in chunky pixels\n2\t\n3\tYou are building this game from this plan and your brief, without the director. Where they disagree on a file or folder name, the brief wins. Where the plan makes a design call, follow it; where it leaves room, use your judgment and keep it consistent.\n4\t\n5\t**Words used here.** \"The brief\" is your TASK.md. \"The game\" is the game: you pick its title as the brief says, and every on-screen use reads one constant, `TITLE`, in `src/game/data/meta.js`. All paths are relative to your dev folder. The finished single HTML goes where the brief says, under the name the brief gives.\n6\t\n7\tSections: 1 The game · 2 Originality · 3 What is already proven · 4 Architecture · 5 Driving and racing · 6 Content · 7 Screens and controls · 8 Art · 9 Animation · 10 Audio · 11 Writing and cast · 12 Attract mode · 13 Dev mode · 14 Multiplayer · 15 About, credits and the exit interview · 16 Testing · 17 Build order · 18 Done checklist. Appendices: A simulation code · B data · C code recipes.\n8\t\n9\t---\n10\t\n11\t## 1. The game\n12\t\n13\t**Pitch.** You arrive on the Halcyon Peninsula with 15,000 credits, no licence and an appetite. You buy a tired second-hand hatchback, pass your first test, win a little money, spend it on brake pads, and go back for more. Thirty hours of that life, compressed into a three-hour demo: a calm, reverent, slightly obsessive driving game about the difference between cars, drawn in 480×270 pixels.\n14\t\n15\t**Genre.** Racing simulation with a career, tuning and car collecting. For the hub catalogue: `Racing`.\n16\t\n17\t**Perspective.** Third-person chase camera (near and far), a bonnet view, and television cameras in replays. Menus are a 3D diorama of the peninsula, seen from a slow three-quarter camera. Why: the chase view is what makes a car's silhouette and body roll readable at 480×270, and television replays are the source's quiet signature.\n18\t\n19\t**What makes the source distinctive, and what it becomes here:**\n20\t\n21\t| Source trait | In this game |\n22\t| --- | --- |\n23\t| Cars treated as the characters, each with its own feel | 14 cars across FF, FR, MR, RR and 4WD. A four-wheel tyre model makes layout, weight and power felt. Each has its own sound, silhouette, tail-light signature and brochure text |\n24\t| You must earn the right to race | Three licences, 12 tests, bronze, silver and gold, with an examiner and a demonstration |\n25\t| Money, parts and patience | A dealer row, used cars, a parts street, real settings. Every part changes numbers you can feel, hear or see |\n26\t| Long, calm menus between races | A diorama map, a turntable showroom, lounge music at 10%, slow camera moves, one idea per screen |\n27\t| Television replays | After every race: a replay director with trackside cameras, slow motion and a free camera |\n28\t| Oil changes, car wash, prize cars, trophies | Service Bay, mud that washes off, prize-car reveals, a trophy shelf, a Museum |\n29\t| Rally and endurance | One-on-one gap racing on dirt, and a ten-lap night race with tyre wear, fuel and an animated pit crew |\n30\t| Catalogue voice | Sincere, precise, gently dry writing (section 11) |\n31\t\n32\t**Pillars.** When a decision is unclear, pick what serves these, in this order:\n33\t\n34\t1. **The corner is the game.** Braking, line and throttle decide everything. The licences teach it; medals reward it.\n35\t2. **Every car is a character.** If two cars feel the same, fix the data.\n36\t3. **The garage is the story.** Progress is a car that is yours and that you made better.\n37\t4. **Calm between storms.** Menus breathe. Nothing shouts. Then the green light.\n38\t\n39\t**What the player can and cannot do** (the Handbook, section 7, says this in-game): cars cannot be damaged or flip over, by design. You can hit walls and other cars and it costs you time. You cannot leave the circuit past the barriers. There is no cockpit view. Assists can be turned off one by one. A stopped or lost car can be put back on the road at any time.\n40\t\n41\t**The first ten minutes**, on a new save: title, a short greeting from the examiner, Dealer Row with exactly three used cars you can afford, Licence Centre with test C-1 (15 seconds of driving and you have a medal), then the rest of Licence C, then the first race. Every step is a one-line nudge on the map; none of it is forced after the first car is bought.\n42\t\n43\t---\n44\t\n45\t## 2. Originality\n46\t\n47\tBuild an original game in the genre, inspired by the source. **Do not recreate the source.** That means:\n48\t\n49\t- No real car makers or models. Use only the fictional marques in section 6.\n50\t- No real or source circuits. Use the layouts in Appendix B; they are original.\n51\t- Nothing from the source's logo, menu layout, fonts, tagline, event names, licence names, music or sound. Mechanics such as licence tests, tuning and oil changes are fair genre mechanics; their names and look here are our own.\n52\t- The title is a playful riff on the source's name, as the brief asks. It must read as its own name and a joke, must not contain the source's name unchanged, and the title screen must not imitate the source's logo.\n53\t- About and credits say \"inspired by\" and name the source, as the brief asks.\n54\t\n55\t---\n56\t\n57\t## 3. What is already proven\n58\t\n59\tThe director tested the riskiest parts against the real kit (Pixel Engine 0.46.0) and the real hub harness (client 1.4.0) on this machine, on Chromium with software WebGPU. These results are facts, not suggestions:\n60\t\n61\t| Question | Result |\n62\t| --- | --- |\n63\t| One HTML from `file://`? | Yes. Bundle with esbuild, inline after escaping `</script`. 5.2 MB plus the game. The test scene boots in under a second. Recipe in C1 |\n64\t| Chase camera? | Use preset `fixed`: its `step()` leaves the camera alone, so set position, look-at and FOV yourself in `update()`. Far plane 6000 works. Recipe in C3 |\n65\t| Does a racing game read at 480×270? | Very well. Cars 8.5 m ahead are about 70 art pixels wide and instantly readable |\n66\t| Car models as data? | Yes. Side-profile polygons (`slab`) extruded across the width. Recipe in C2 |\n67\t| Split screen on one TV? | Yes. Two `Engine.start` calls in two side-by-side containers, each 240×270 art, each with its own camera, input and audio. A second engine can start and be disposed during play while the first keeps running, and the first re-frames to full screen by itself |\n68\t| Hub conformance with WebGPU in iframes? | Passes: 2 players; 4 players with `latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5`; `scenario=host-leave`; and the game's own card |\n69\t| The engine's `Vehicle`? | An arcade vehicle (default top speed 16 m/s, no gearbox, no load transfer), so tuning couldn't be felt. **Don't use it.** The tested car model in Appendix A replaces it |\n70\t| Is a real car model feasible and stable? | Yes. Appendix A has run every car on every track, plus an 8-car race with contact, airborne crests, reverse, manual gears, wear and fuel. A driver limited to keyboard-style digital inputs laps all ten sampled car and track pairings with no recoveries |\n71\t| `Engine.headless` and `check.mjs --headless`? | Work for a game whose `createGame()` builds models and HUD. The replay check passes |\n72\t| Pixel ghost cars? | `toonMaterial(color, { dissolve: true })` cloned per ghost with `material.dissolve = 0.35` gives a stippled ghost. At 0.55 it is too faint to read |\n73\t| Rain and night? | `Precipitation`, `applySky(engine, skyAt(hour))` and `ctx.lights` following a car all work and look right. Use fewer, thinner drops than the default near the camera |\n74\t\n75\t### Engine facts that will otherwise cost you hours\n76\t\n77\t1. **Menus and `loadGame`.** `loadGame` frees music, sets and lights. Keep all menus in **one** engine Game (`MenuScene`) so music never restarts. Use `loadGame` only to go between Menu, Race and Replay.\n78\t2. **Pausing.** `engine.paused` and `timeScale = 0` make `dt` 0 and drop queued presses. Pause a race by not stepping the simulation, and keep the engine running so the menu animates and reads input.\n79\t3. **Volumes.** Engine defaults are master 0.8, sfx 1, music 0.6. On first launch call `audio.setVolume('music', 0.1)`. `audio.resetVolumes()` restores 0.6, so your own reset must set 0.1 again.\n80\t4. **The engine's settings panel** is a DOM panel that isn't built for a pad. Start with `settings: { button: false }`, build your own Settings screen, and drive `engine.settings.set({...})` and `audio.setVolume` from it. (`settings.key` is where the engine saves them: leave it for the first engine, and pass `key: false` on a second engine.)\n81\t5. **Touch.** The engine adds on-screen controls on touch devices by default. The brief forbids them: pass `touch: false`.\n82\t6. **Pads.** The engine merges all pads into one and reads buttons as digital keys. Analog triggers and a second pad aren't exposed. Set `input.gamepadButtons = {}` and poll `navigator.getGamepads()` yourself (section 4, Input). The standard mapping is 0 Cross, 1 Circle, 2 Square, 3 Triangle, 4 L1, 5 R1, 6 L2, 7 R2, 8 Create, 9 Options, 10 L3, 11 R3, 12–15 D-pad.\n83\t7. **`wasPressed` works inside engine hooks only.** It didn't fire when polled from an outside `requestAnimationFrame` loop. Anything outside a hook (the hub card's Enter) uses a plain `keydown` listener on `window`.\n84\t8. **`input.commands` last one rendered frame** and `fixedUpdate` can run zero or more times. Latch them once per frame at the top of whichever hook runs first (compare `ctx.engine.frame`).\n85\t9. **Lights.** The pool holds 4 to 16 lights and only serves requests within about 24 m plus their radius of `cameraTarget`. Give the player's headlights high priority. Fake everything else at night: `glow` parts, plus vertex-coloured light pools baked into the road.\n86\t10. **Shadows on cars.** Set `receiveShadow = false` on car meshes (keep `castShadow = true`). Otherwise the rear faces, which you stare at all race, show saw-tooth shadow acne.\n87\t11. **Vertex colours blend across shared vertices.** For hard-edged kerbs and lines, give each colour run its own quad with unshared vertices.\n88\t12. **Far plane.** The `fixed` rig defaults to near 0.2, far 200. Set `camera.near = 0.5; camera.far = 6000; camera.updateProjectionMatrix()`. Pass `minZoom: 1, maxZoom: 1` or the wheel changes your FOV.\n89\t13. **The HUD font is uppercase 5×7.** Draw all UI text through `ctx.hud`. Check characters the font lacks (hub names can contain anything: fall back to `P1`, `P2`).\n90\t14. **Two engines.** `__PIXEL_ENGINE__` holds the last one started, so keep your own handles. Each has its own `AudioManager`. Set `audio.persist = false` on the second before muting it, or the mute is saved for the first. Mute the second one's music and let one mix own all sound.\n91\t15. **Sound unlock.** The AudioContext opens on a key, click or tap. A pad press may not count as a user gesture. Call `audio.unlock()` on any pad press, check `audio.unlocked`, and if sound is still locked show a small speaker-off icon and the line `PRESS A KEY OR CLICK FOR SOUND` on the title and Settings.\n92\t16. **`engine.step(n)` draws nothing.** For a screenshot, step, then set `e.manual = false` and wait for a frame.\n93\t17. **On software WebGPU** the engine logs `quality medium → low` at start. That is an info line, not an error. Expect 15 to 50 fps here. The brief makes FPS out of scope: never treat FPS as a failure and don't simplify the game for it.\n94\t18. **Port printing.** `python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` prints its port late when piped, because stdout is buffered. A script waiting on that line hangs forever. Run it with `python3 -u -m http.server ...`. Stop it by the PID you started.\n95\t19. **No Math.random in anything a replay or the hub reads.** Use `ctx.random` and its forks.\n96\t20. **`file://`.** The kit's note that modules don't load from `file://` is true of `src/index.html`. The bundled HTML does load from `file://`, so test that (the brief prefers it). `check.mjs` serves `src/` by itself on a port it picks, for model sheets and quick checks.\n97\t\n98\t---\n99\t\n100\t## 4. Architecture"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"66624378-a7bb-42a2-8c1d-fc63666b5654","uuid":"cbff06cc-39c7-47c8-be6a-0dac34f03405","timestamp":"2026-11-06T00:16:48.137Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261105-162057__x4c6ec5/work/PLAN.md","content":"# PLAN: a racing life in chunky pixels\n\nYou are building this game from this plan and your brief, without the director. Where they disagree on a file or folder name, the brief wins. Where the plan makes a design call, follow it; where it leaves room, use your judgment and keep it consistent.\n\n**Words used here.** \"The brief\" is your TASK.md. \"The game\" is the game: you pick its title as the brief says, and every on-screen use reads one constant, `TITLE`, in `src/game/data/meta.js`. All paths are relative to your dev folder. The finished single HTML goes where the brief says, under the name the brief gives.\n\nSections: 1 The game · 2 Originality · 3 What is already proven · 4 Architecture · 5 Driving and racing · 6 Content · 7 Screens and controls · 8 Art · 9 Animation · 10 Audio · 11 Writing and cast · 12 Attract mode · 13 Dev mode · 14 Multiplayer · 15 About, credits and the exit interview · 16 Testing · 17 Build order · 18 Done checklist. Appendices: A simulation code · B data · C code recipes.\n\n---\n\n## 1. The game\n\n**Pitch.** You arrive on the Halcyon Peninsula with 15,000 credits, no licence and an appetite. You buy a tired second-hand hatchback, pass your first test, win a little money, spend it on brake pads, and go back for more. Thirty hours of that life, compressed into a three-hour demo: a calm, reverent, slightly obsessive driving game about the difference between cars, drawn in 480×270 pixels.\n\n**Genre.** Racing simulation with a career, tuning and car collecting. For the hub catalogue: `Racing`.\n\n**Perspective.** Third-person chase camera (near and far), a bonnet view, and television cameras in replays. Menus are a 3D diorama of the peninsula, seen from a slow three-quarter camera. Why: the chase view is what makes a car's silhouette and body roll readable at 480×270, and television replays are the source's quiet signature.\n\n**What makes the source distinctive, and what it becomes here:**\n\n| Source trait | In this game |\n| --- | --- |\n| Cars treated as the characters, each with its own feel | 14 cars across FF, FR, MR, RR and 4WD. A four-wheel tyre model makes layout, weight and power felt. Each has its own sound, silhouette, tail-light signature and brochure text |\n| You must earn the right to race | Three licences, 12 tests, bronze, silver and gold, with an examiner and a demonstration |\n| Money, parts and patience | A dealer row, used cars, a parts street, real settings. Every part changes numbers you can feel, hear or see |\n| Long, calm menus between races | A diorama map, a turntable showroom, lounge music at 10%, slow camera moves, one idea per screen |\n| Television replays | After every race: a replay director with trackside cameras, slow motion and a free camera |\n| Oil changes, car wash, prize cars, trophies | Service Bay, mud that washes off, prize-car reveals, a trophy shelf, a Museum |\n| Rally and endurance | One-on-one gap racing on dirt, and a ten-lap night race with tyre wear, fuel and an animated pit crew |\n| Catalogue voice | Sincere, precise, gently dry writing (section 11) |\n\n**Pillars.** When a decision is unclear, pick what serves these, in this order:\n\n1. **The corner is the game.** Braking, line and throttle decide everything. The licences teach it; medals reward it.\n2. **Every car is a character.** If two cars feel the same, fix the data.\n3. **The garage is the story.** Progress is a car that is yours and that you made better.\n4. **Calm between storms.** Menus breathe. Nothing shouts. Then the green light.\n\n**What the player can and cannot do** (the Handbook, section 7, says this in-game): cars cannot be damaged or flip over, by design. You can hit walls and other cars and it costs you time. You cannot leave the circuit past the barriers. There is no cockpit view. Assists can be turned off one by one. A stopped or lost car can be put back on the road at any time.\n\n**The first ten minutes**, on a new save: title, a short greeting from the examiner, Dealer Row with exactly three used cars you can afford, Licence Centre with test C-1 (15 seconds of driving and you have a medal), then the rest of Licence C, then the first race. Every step is a one-line nudge on the map; none of it is forced after the first car is bought.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Originality\n\nBuild an original game in the genre, inspired by the source. **Do not recreate the source.** That means:\n\n- No real car makers or models. Use only the fictional marques in section 6.\n- No real or source circuits. Use the layouts in Appendix B; they are original.\n- Nothing from the source's logo, menu layout, fonts, tagline, event names, licence names, music or sound. Mechanics such as licence tests, tuning and oil changes are fair genre mechanics; their names and look here are our own.\n- The title is a playful riff on the source's name, as the brief asks. It must read as its own name and a joke, must not contain the source's name unchanged, and the title screen must not imitate the source's logo.\n- About and credits say \"inspired by\" and name the source, as the brief asks.\n\n---\n\n## 3. What is already proven\n\nThe director tested the riskiest parts against the real kit (Pixel Engine 0.46.0) and the real hub harness (client 1.4.0) on this machine, on Chromium with software WebGPU. These results are facts, not suggestions:\n\n| Question | Result |\n| --- | --- |\n| One HTML from `file://`? | Yes. Bundle with esbuild, inline after escaping `</script`. 5.2 MB plus the game. The test scene boots in under a second. Recipe in C1 |\n| Chase camera? | Use preset `fixed`: its `step()` leaves the camera alone, so set position, look-at and FOV yourself in `update()`. Far plane 6000 works. Recipe in C3 |\n| Does a racing game read at 480×270? | Very well. Cars 8.5 m ahead are about 70 art pixels wide and instantly readable |\n| Car models as data? | Yes. Side-profile polygons (`slab`) extruded across the width. Recipe in C2 |\n| Split screen on one TV? | Yes. Two `Engine.start` calls in two side-by-side containers, each 240×270 art, each with its own camera, input and audio. A second engine can start and be disposed during play while the first keeps running, and the first re-frames to full screen by itself |\n| Hub conformance with WebGPU in iframes? | Passes: 2 players; 4 players with `latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5`; `scenario=host-leave`; and the game's own card |\n| The engine's `Vehicle`? | An arcade vehicle (default top speed 16 m/s, no gearbox, no load transfer), so tuning couldn't be felt. **Don't use it.** The tested car model in Appendix A replaces it |\n| Is a real car model feasible and stable? | Yes. Appendix A has run every car on every track, plus an 8-car race with contact, airborne crests, reverse, manual gears, wear and fuel. A driver limited to keyboard-style digital inputs laps all ten sampled car and track pairings with no recoveries |\n| `Engine.headless` and `check.mjs --headless`? | Work for a game whose `createGame()` builds models and HUD. The replay check passes |\n| Pixel ghost cars? | `toonMaterial(color, { dissolve: true })` cloned per ghost with `material.dissolve = 0.35` gives a stippled ghost. At 0.55 it is too faint to read |\n| Rain and night? | `Precipitation`, `applySky(engine, skyAt(hour))` and `ctx.lights` following a car all work and look right. Use fewer, thinner drops than the default near the camera |\n\n### Engine facts that will otherwise cost you hours\n\n1. **Menus and `loadGame`.** `loadGame` frees music, sets and lights. Keep all menus in **one** engine Game (`MenuScene`) so music never restarts. Use `loadGame` only to go between Menu, Race and Replay.\n2. **Pausing.** `engine.paused` and `timeScale = 0` make `dt` 0 and drop queued presses. Pause a race by not stepping the simulation, and keep the engine running so the menu animates and reads input.\n3. **Volumes.** Engine defaults are master 0.8, sfx 1, music 0.6. On first launch call `audio.setVolume('music', 0.1)`. `audio.resetVolumes()` restores 0.6, so your own reset must set 0.1 again.\n4. **The engine's settings panel** is a DOM panel that isn't built for a pad. Start with `settings: { button: false }`, build your own Settings screen, and drive `engine.settings.set({...})` and `audio.setVolume` from it. (`settings.key` is where the engine saves them: leave it for the first engine, and pass `key: false` on a second engine.)\n5. **Touch.** The engine adds on-screen controls on touch devices by default. The brief forbids them: pass `touch: false`.\n6. **Pads.** The engine merges all pads into one and reads buttons as digital keys. Analog triggers and a second pad aren't exposed. Set `input.gamepadButtons = {}` and poll `navigator.getGamepads()` yourself (section 4, Input). The standard mapping is 0 Cross, 1 Circle, 2 Square, 3 Triangle, 4 L1, 5 R1, 6 L2, 7 R2, 8 Create, 9 Options, 10 L3, 11 R3, 12–15 D-pad.\n7. **`wasPressed` works inside engine hooks only.** It didn't fire when polled from an outside `requestAnimationFrame` loop. Anything outside a hook (the hub card's Enter) uses a plain `keydown` listener on `window`.\n8. **`input.commands` last one rendered frame** and `fixedUpdate` can run zero or more times. Latch them once per frame at the top of whichever hook runs first (compare `ctx.engine.frame`).\n9. **Lights.** The pool holds 4 to 16 lights and only serves requests within about 24 m plus their radius of `cameraTarget`. Give the player's headlights high priority. Fake everything else at night: `glow` parts, plus vertex-coloured light pools baked into the road.\n10. **Shadows on cars.** Set `receiveShadow = false` on car meshes (keep `castShadow = true`). Otherwise the rear faces, which you stare at all race, show saw-tooth shadow acne.\n11. **Vertex colours blend across shared vertices.** For hard-edged kerbs and lines, give each colour run its own quad with unshared vertices.\n12. **Far plane.** The `fixed` rig defaults to near 0.2, far 200. Set `camera.near = 0.5; camera.far = 6000; camera.updateProjectionMatrix()`. Pass `minZoom: 1, maxZoom: 1` or the wheel changes your FOV.\n13. **The HUD font is uppercase 5×7.** Draw all UI text through `ctx.hud`. Check characters the font lacks (hub names can contain anything: fall back to `P1`, `P2`).\n14. **Two engines.** `__PIXEL_ENGINE__` holds the last one started, so keep your own handles. Each has its own `AudioManager`. Set `audio.persist = false` on the second before muting it, or the mute is saved for the first. Mute the second one's music and let one mix own all sound.\n15. **Sound unlock.** The AudioContext opens on a key, click or tap. A pad press may not count as a user gesture. Call `audio.unlock()` on any pad press, check `audio.unlocked`, and if sound is still locked show a small speaker-off icon and the line `PRESS A KEY OR CLICK FOR SOUND` on the title and Settings.\n16. **`engine.step(n)` draws nothing.** For a screenshot, step, then set `e.manual = false` and wait for a frame.\n17. **On software WebGPU** the engine logs `quality medium → low` at start. That is an info line, not an error. Expect 15 to 50 fps here. The brief makes FPS out of scope: never treat FPS as a failure and don't simplify the game for it.\n18. **Port printing.** `python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` prints its port late when piped, because stdout is buffered. A script waiting on that line hangs forever. Run it with `python3 -u -m http.server ...`. Stop it by the PID you started.\n19. **No Math.random in anything a replay or the hub reads.** Use `ctx.random` and its forks.\n20. **`file://`.** The kit's note that modules don't load from `file://` is true of `src/index.html`. The bundled HTML does load from `file://`, so test that (the brief prefers it). `check.mjs` serves `src/` by itself on a port it picks, for model sheets and quick checks.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Architecture","numLines":100,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1331}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01UobFMawBQ6FGXzHphNaGCY","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
