{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgkiJTmq1K34HKSvzZM8g","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01NkQchQgzCjEaXjzvf1CgVx","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261105-162057__x4c6ec5/work/PLAN.md","offset":642,"limit":218},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11613,"cache_read_input_tokens":588529,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11613},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"66624378-a7bb-42a2-8c1d-fc63666b5654","uuid":"3ef94e7e-5572-48df-bca3-d0211bfd1eaf","timestamp":"2026-11-06T00:17:07.409Z","request_id":"req_011CgkiJTVTreQcjPiZ8r8ZT","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01NkQchQgzCjEaXjzvf1CgVx":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261105-162057__x4c6ec5/work/PLAN.md","offset":642,"limit":218}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NkQchQgzCjEaXjzvf1CgVx","type":"tool_result","content":"642\t| 3 | Tuning: dyno pull with before and after curve, new wing appears | Tuning | 8 |\n643\t| 4 | Licence B-1 demonstration, medal awarded | Licences | 10 |\n644\t| 5 | Kestrel Park: AI race, starts in chase, cuts to TV cameras, a pass | Racing, replay cameras | 20 |\n645\t| 6 | Red Clay: the Ryu over a crest, dust, a handbrake hairpin | Rally | 12 |\n646\t| 7 | Harbour Lights at night, then a pit stop with the crew | Night, endurance | 20 |\n647\t| 8 | Saltline: slipstream at 300 km/h past the blimp | Speed | 10 |\n648\t| 9 | Podium, prize car reveal | Rewards | 8 |\n649\t| 10 | Credits card: `PRESS ANY BUTTON` | Call to action | 4 |\n650\t\n651\tEach race clip is a real `RaceScene` with AI on every car, `env` god settings, and hard cuts to skip dull parts. Since the player's profile isn't touched, nothing here can change the save. On a touch-only device it runs the same way.\n652\t\n653\t---\n654\t\n655\t## 13. Dev mode\n656\t\n657\t`DEV MODE` is an ordinary item in the pause menu (hidden only in a hub session), also opened from the Map's pause and with `?dev=1`. It's a pad-friendly panel over the running game, 10-foot like the rest, in tabs.\n658\t\n659\t**Live sliders** (left/right changes it at once, ✕ resets one, △ resets all, mouse drags). They write to the sim's `env` or the rules:\n660\t\n661\t| Slider | Default | Range | Sim hook |\n662\t| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n663\t| Grip | 1 | 0.5–1.5 | `env.gripScale` |\n664\t| Power | 1 | 0.5–2 | `env.powerScale` |\n665\t| Brake strength | 1 | 0.5–1.5 | `env.brakeScale` |\n666\t| Downforce | 1 | 0–2 | scales `clA` |\n667\t| Tyre wear | 1 | 0–5 | `env.wearRate` |\n668\t| Fuel use | 1 | 0–5 | `env.fuelRate` |\n669\t| Steering rate | 3 | 1–8 | `inp.steerRate` |\n670\t| Stability strength | 1 | 0–2 | scales `asm` |\n671\t| Traction strength | 1 | 0–2 | scales `tcs` |\n672\t| AI pace | event | 0.7–1.05 | `skill` |\n673\t| AI rubber band | 0 | 0–0.03 | adds to `skill` by gap |\n674\t| Wall speed loss | 1 | 0–2 | scales the barrier's `keep` |\n675\t| Slipstream | 0.65 | 0.4–1 | `dragScale` |\n676\t| Prize money | 1 | 0–5 | career |\n677\t| Chase distance, FOV | 8.5, 58 | 4–14, 40–90 | camera |\n678\t\n679\t**Tools:**\n680\t\n681\t- Teleport along the track (scrub `s`), finish now in position N, end race, restart, reset car.\n682\t- Time scale 0.25, 0.5, 1, 2, 4. Pause and frame step. Save state and load state (sim JSON).\n683\t- God mode (no wall loss, no wear, no fuel use).\n684\t- Free camera; engine debug panel (`engine.showDebug`).\n685\t- Overlays: racing line and speed profile, wheel load and slip bars, surface tint, collision circles, AI targets and lanes, telemetry strip (speed, throttle, brake, ax, ay).\n686\t- Give credits; unlock all cars; pass all tests with gold; win the current event; jump to any event, test or menu set; reset the save.\n687\t- Hour of day, rain toggle, field size, change car on the spot, start attract now.\n688\t\n689\tSliders persist for the session and are cleared by Reset. Medals and records earned with sliders off default aren't saved, and the HUD shows a small `DEV` tag so a tester knows.\n690\t\n691\t---\n692\t\n693\t## 14. Multiplayer\n694\t\n695\tThe brief points to the AI Hub session protocol, in the hub repository's `lib/hub/README.md`. Read it whole. Two things to build:\n696\t\n697\t### In a session: ghost mode\n698\t\n699\tWhy ghost and not shared world: every copy runs its own physics at 240 Hz with no input lag. A shared world through a host, at up to 20 messages a second with 150 ms of latency each way, would make a precise driving game feel wrong. Ghost also needs no host, so it survives anyone leaving.\n700\t\n701\t**Tags,** in `<head>`, after the client:\n702\t\n703\t```html\n704\t<meta name=\"hub:session\" content=\"1\">\n705\t<meta name=\"hub:session-players\" content=\"1-4\">\n706\t<meta name=\"hub:session-mode\" content=\"ghost\">\n707\t<meta name=\"hub:session-minutes\" content=\"4\">\n708\t<meta name=\"hub:session-goal\" content=\"Lowest time wins: same car, same track\">\n709\t<meta name=\"hub:session-howto\" content=\"Steer stick or A D. R2 or W go. L2 or S brake.\">\n710\t<meta name=\"hub:webgpu\" content=\"required\">\n711\t```\n712\t\n713\tThe hub's examples also carry `hub:type` and `hub:players`: use `Game`, and `1-2` for players on one device. Your exit interview's players field is `1–4`, counting sessions.\n714\t\n715\t**A round:**\n716\t\n717\t- When `AIHub.active`: no title, no menus, no attract, no Dev mode, no saves. Await `connect` before anything else, then `Engine.start` a `RaceScene` with `seed: info.seed`. If `connect` returns `null`, play as on its own.\n718\t- `AIHub.rng('round')` picks the same round on every copy: track and car from a fixed list, tuned to about 4 minutes: Kestrel 2 laps, Cedar 2, Harbour 2, Saltline 4; cars Ginga, Ryu, Brandt 3.2, Kite. All get the same stock setup and default assists. Hour and rain also come from the same rng.\n719\t- **Own card** (`card: true`), so the round opens in the game's world: the cars lined up at the start, each seat's colour shown, the track map, how to win, the controls for the last-used device, the countdown from the `card` event. Enter or ✕ answers Ready; △ or M answers More time (also on-screen buttons for mouse and tap). It has no other choices. Use `AIHub.rng('card')` for anything random in it. Read Enter from a `window` `keydown` listener (engine fact 7; tested).\n720\t- Call `AIHub.ready()` as soon as the scene is built, with no key press. The harness waits 20 s for this, so build only this round's track. Keep the build fast and don't load extras in a session.\n721\t- **Start:** on `start`, three red lights with `AIHub.time()` at −3, −2, −1, green at 0. Controls lock until then. Standing start, no AI cars.\n722\t- **While racing:** at 10 Hz, `AIHub.send({ t: 'g', a: trackId, x, y, z, yaw, v, s, lap, steer, br }, { reliable: false })`, values rounded to keep it small. Draw others as ghost cars, interpolated through a 150 ms buffer, in seat colours with name tags. They never collide or affect anything. `AIHub.progress({ score: totalMetres, label: 'LAP 1/2 P2' })` on the same tick.\n723\t- **Finish:** `AIHub.result({ time: Math.round(AIHub.time()), stats: { 'Best lap': seconds, 'Top km/h': n, 'Walls': n }, awards: [...] })` once. `AIHub.send({ t: 'done', time })` reliable. Then an AI drives your car while you wait, and your camera can follow another ghost.\n724\t- **`time-up`:** if not finished, `AIHub.result({ score: totalMetres, stats })` at once.\n725\t- **`leave`:** remove that ghost. **`end`:** stop input and all sound including `enginesynth`; the hub shows results over the game.\n726\t- **Menu:** Options or Esc opens settings over the running race (Sound, Button glyphs, Handbook). It never pauses and offers no quit or restart. Ignore Options while Create is held.\n727\t- **Rules:** no `alert`, `confirm`, `prompt`, `window.open`, no navigation, no other network, no chat. Names only in HUD text, with the `P1` fallback.\n728\t- If the device is touch-only: show the required-input message, still call `ready()`, and on `time-up` report `score: 0`. A connected pad or key lifts it, as usual.\n729\t\n730\t### On its own\n731\t\n732\tYour brief's phrase \"as well as for the game on its own\" is read two ways, and this plan satisfies both. **First**, the game runs fully outside a session: `AIHub.active` false means none of the session rules apply and no network is used. **Second**, multiplayer also exists without the hub, on one screen:\n733\t\n734\t**Couch Duel** (Arcade Gate): two players, one TV, split screen, any track and car, with optional AI cars and real contact. Implementation (tested): `src/game/net/couch.js` makes the page two side-by-side containers and starts a second engine in the second. One `RaceSim` is shared: P1's `RaceScene.fixedUpdate` steps it, P2's only draws it. P2's engine has `settings: { button: false, key: false }`, `audio.persist = false`, muted; one mix plays both cars' engines, panned left and right. Pads are read raw, so P1 is pad 0, P2 is pad 1; with one pad, the pad is P1 and the keyboard is P2; with none, P1 is WASD and P2 is arrows, with Enter and Space remapped. Each side has its own compact HUD. On leaving, `engine2.dispose()`, hide the second container, restore the first to full width.\n735\t\n736\t**Also:** Time Trial's ghost can be raced in turn by whoever holds the pad (\"Pass the pad\", best time wins), using the same ghost view.\n737\t\n738\t---\n739\t\n740\t## 15. About, credits and the exit interview\n741\t\n742\tThe brief says exactly what this must contain. Build it once, as data, then show it two ways:\n743\t\n744\t1. Fill in the exit-interview file the brief names, in the exact shape the brief gives, and check it parses.\n745\t2. `tools/build.mjs` reads that file and embeds it, so the in-game **About/Credits** screen and your final chat reply can't drift apart.\n746\t\n747\t**The About screen** is paged for the TV, reached from Title, Map and Settings:\n748\t\n749\t- **Source Information:** date, source, plan (this plan's file name), genre and perspective, tech, art direction, animation, audio, online, testing, research, environment, model, usage (`added on import`, exactly as the brief says). \"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm.\n750\t- **Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback, Prompt Feedback:** each question from the brief, with your answer. Answer honestly from what really happened, at the end of the work.\n751\t- **Credits:** the game's title and maker line; \"Inspired by\" the source; Pixel Engine with version; three.js r186 (MIT) and Rapier (Apache-2.0) and the Sweetie 16 palette by GrafxKid, from the kit's `CREDITS.md`, which also says what it asks you to ship: embed its text on a scrollable page.\n752\t\n753\tPaginate long answers. Never use a dense wall: it's still a TV.\n754\t\n755\tUse the same answers in your final chat reply, as the brief requires. Don't estimate usage; write what the brief says to write.\n756\t\n757\t**The `aiHub` block:** type `Game`, genre `Racing`, source and series for the source named in your brief, players `1–4`, controls in one line (`Stick or WASD to steer, R2 or W to go, L2 or S to brake`), and tags such as `3d`, `pixel art`, `vehicles`, `retro`, `third-person`, `versus`. The name is your title. Write the tagline and description yourself, in the catalogue voice.\n758\t\n759\t**Research:** the director's research on the kit, the hub protocol and the source is folded into this plan. You don't need web research unless you want it. In Source Information, report what you yourself did.\n760\t\n761\t---\n762\t\n763\t## 16. Testing\n764\t\n765\tThe brief's rules: one command that prints only failures and a one-line count; rerun only after changing what it covers; screenshots only for what a script can't check; no subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\n766\t\n767\tWrite `node tools/test.mjs [suite...]`. Suites cache by file hash, so an unchanged suite is skipped. Output: failures, then `tests: 212 passed, 0 failed`.\n768\t\n769\t| Suite | Runs in | Covers |\n770\t| --- | --- | --- |\n771\t| `sim` | Node, seconds | The regression table in Appendix B ±3 s; 0–100; each car laps each track; determinism (same inputs, same hash); reverse, manual gears, airborne; contact doesn't explode; wear and fuel over ten laps |\n772\t| `data` | Node | Every id resolves; every event winnable with a car that's obtainable by then; every test has medals; all text fits the 60-character line and 4-line card |\n773\t| `career` | Node | Money, licences, unlocks, prizes, oil, days; a save survives a version migration |\n774\t| `headless` | Node, `Engine.headless` | Menu flows by actions: new game, buy car, test, tune, race, results, save. `engine.replay` ends on the same `fingerprint`. Use `check.mjs --headless` too. Music at 0.1 on a fresh profile; Reset sets it back |\n775\t| `play` | Node, `Engine.headless` | **The whole game by script:** a bot presses real actions through real screens: buys the Kite, passes licences, wins events (driving through the input layer, using `aiDrive` as its brain with `skill` 1.0), tunes, buys, pits, reaches the Grand Prix, sees the ending. Dev tools off. It reports money at each step, and fails if the game can't be finished without cheating |\n776\t| `browser` | Playwright, the built HTML from `file://` | Boots with no console errors. Pad stub (`add_init_script` replacing `navigator.getGamepads`, standard mapping) drives every top-level screen. Prompts swap on input change; Xbox glyphs setting. Mouse hover moves focus, click confirms. Attract starts at 10 s, any input exits. Dev mode sliders change `env`, Reset restores. Touch-only profile (Chromium with `has_touch`, `is_mobile`) shows the gate, the settings tap works, and attract runs. Couch Duel with two pad stubs: two engines up, then disposed, no errors. About shows every field |\n777\t| `audio` | Playwright | `enginesynth` rendered in an `OfflineAudioContext`: non-silent, and spectral centroid rises with rpm. Analyser on the live mix is non-silent in a race. `audio.counts` shows shifts, kerbs, tyre, voice. Music at 10% produces a level well under effects |\n778\t| `hub` | Playwright, harness served on an OS-picked port from a folder holding the HTML and `tools/hub/` | `?game=<html>&players=2&auto=1`, `&players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1`, and `&scenario=host-leave`. Pass means `window.__hubCheck.pass`. Also opens the game outside a session and checks it still reaches the title |\n779\t\n780\t**Machine notes.**\n781\t\n782\t- Chromium flags that worked for WebGPU: `--enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-vulkan=swiftshader --use-angle=swiftshader --use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader`.\n783\t- System Python has Playwright: `from playwright.sync_api import sync_playwright` (don't add `-I`). Node needs `npm install playwright@1.63.0` in your own folder.\n784\t- The kit's `check.mjs` picks its own port. For the harness, run `python3 -u -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` (the `-u` matters) in a folder with the game, and confirm you got your own page.\n785\t- One or two browsers at a time. Close them. Stop only your own PIDs. Keep all files in your folder, not `/tmp`. WebKit here has no WebGPU, so it can't run this engine: note that in testing feedback rather than chasing it.\n786\t- In tests, drive time with `engine.step(n)` rather than waiting. Treat \"Target closed\" as memory, not a bug: run fewer browsers.\n787\t\n788\t**Look at these at 1920×1080**, when their code changes, because no script can judge them: each car in the showroom (`check.mjs --sheets` first), each track at the start and at a corner, Map at day and night, a race HUD, a results screen, Settings, Handbook page, Tuning, the Harbour at night, the rally crest, the pit stop, the podium, Couch Duel, the attract mode's first clips. At each ask: can this be read from ten feet? Is one idea on screen?\n789\t\n790\t---\n791\t\n792\t## 17. Build order\n793\t\n794\tRisky and central first. After each step, the game should run. Run the matching suite before moving on.\n795\t\n796\t1. **Pipeline.** Kit in `src/`, `tools/build.mjs` (C1), a blank scene from `file://`, `tools/test.mjs` skeleton.\n797\t2. **Sim in Node.** Port Appendix A and B. The `sim` suite must match the numbers. Add `RaceSim`.\n798\t3. **One car on one track.** Track mesh, car kit, chase camera, race HUD, pad and keyboard. Spend real time here until driving feels good on the stick, the keyboard and at speed. Body motion, sound and smoke are part of feel, so do the engine sound now.\n799\t4. **A race.** AI field, rolling start, results, replay recording and director.\n800\t5. **All tracks, dressed.** Hour, night, rain, dirt, crests, slipstream.\n801\t6. **Menu world.** UI widgets, glyphs and prompts, Map, Dealer Row, Garage, saves.\n802\t7. **Career.** Licences and medals, events, tuning, setup sliders, service, prizes, podium, Museum, Handbook.\n803\t8. **Pit stops and rally,** then the Harbour Lights 200.\n804\t9. **Attract mode, Dev mode, Settings, touch gate, About.**\n805\t10. **Multiplayer.** Hub round first (harness), then Couch Duel.\n806\t11. **Whole-game playthrough script,** balance, audio mix, voices, music, polish, screenshots.\n807\t12. **Finish.** Exit interview, final build, every suite green, chat reply.\n808\t\n809\t**If budget runs short,** cut content counts and never features. Floors: 10 cars covering all five layouts, 5 tracks including one dirt and one night, 9 licence tests across three licences, 5 events including rally and endurance. Every feature in this plan still works with fewer items. Never leave a stub, a placeholder or a menu item that goes nowhere: the brief forbids it.\n810\t\n811\t---\n812\t\n813\t## 18. Done checklist\n814\t\n815\t**Requirements, with where each is covered:**\n816\t\n817\t| The brief asks for | Here |\n818\t| --- | --- |\n819\t| Genre and perspective picked | §1 |\n820\t| Title: playful riff, brainstormed, picked by real RNG | Yours, as the brief says; `TITLE` constant, §2 guardrails |\n821\t| Whether to research | §15 |\n822\t| One HTML, named as the brief says, dev files in the named subfolder | §4, C1 |\n823\t| Build on the kit, engine unchanged | §3, §4 |\n824\t| Art: engine's style as base, new assets for the source's feel | §8, C2 |\n825\t| Animation: engine's system as base, new animations | §9 |\n826\t| Audio in code; gibberish voices; music at 10% by default | §10 |\n827\t| Online: hub protocol, in a session and on its own | §14, C4 |\n828\t| Share the machine; `file://` first; OS-picked ports; own PIDs only | §16 |\n829\t| Performance out of scope; add detail freely | §3 fact 17, §8 |\n830\t| Headless tests; a scripted whole-game run; one command; one-line count | §16 |\n831\t| No subagents to review or debate | §16 |\n832\t| Original game, feature-complete demo, long enough to show everything | §1, §2, §6 |\n833\t| Infrastructure for designers to continue | §4 Designer tools |\n834\t| Dev mode from the pause menu, live sliders, reset | §13 |\n835\t| Attract mode at 10 s, any input exits, may cheat | §12 |\n836\t| Narrative tone like the source's | §11 |\n837\t| 10-foot, controller-first, native keyboard and mouse, not dense | §7 |\n838\t| Prompts follow last input; PlayStation glyphs, Xbox option | §7 |\n839\t| Touch-only gate, settings button, attract runs | §7 |\n840\t| No TODOs, placeholders or stubs | §17, below |\n841\t| Source Information, Testing, Engine and Prompt Feedback in chat and on an About/Credits screen | §15 |\n842\t| Exit interview file in the brief's exact shape | §15 |\n843\t\n844\t**Before you stop:**\n845\t\n846\t- [ ] `node tools/test.mjs` is all green, with the hub suite passing all three runs.\n847\t- [ ] The scripted playthrough finishes the career with no Dev tools.\n848\t- [ ] The built HTML runs from `file://`, with `session.js` unmodified and first in `<head>`, and `pixel-engine.js` unedited in `src/`.\n849\t- [ ] Music starts at 10% on a fresh profile and in a session.\n850\t- [ ] Grep for `TODO`, `FIXME`, `placeholder`, `stub`, `coming soon`: nothing in the game.\n851\t- [ ] Every menu item does something. Every car, track, test and event is reachable.\n852\t- [ ] You looked at the 1080p screenshots.\n853\t- [ ] About/Credits shows real answers. The exit-interview file parses as JSON. Your chat reply carries the same information.\n854\t- [ ] No stray servers or browsers of yours left running.\n855\t\n856\t---\n857\t## Appendix A: `src/game/sim/sim.js`\n858\t\n859\tTested code. It ran every car on every track, an 8-car race with contact, and the checks in Appendix B. Port it as is, then extend it."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"66624378-a7bb-42a2-8c1d-fc63666b5654","uuid":"81245995-d812-4e8e-8b02-2064bc8aaa66","timestamp":"2026-11-06T00:17:07.417Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261105-162057__x4c6ec5/work/PLAN.md","content":"| 3 | Tuning: dyno pull with before and after curve, new wing appears | Tuning | 8 |\n| 4 | Licence B-1 demonstration, medal awarded | Licences | 10 |\n| 5 | Kestrel Park: AI race, starts in chase, cuts to TV cameras, a pass | Racing, replay cameras | 20 |\n| 6 | Red Clay: the Ryu over a crest, dust, a handbrake hairpin | Rally | 12 |\n| 7 | Harbour Lights at night, then a pit stop with the crew | Night, endurance | 20 |\n| 8 | Saltline: slipstream at 300 km/h past the blimp | Speed | 10 |\n| 9 | Podium, prize car reveal | Rewards | 8 |\n| 10 | Credits card: `PRESS ANY BUTTON` | Call to action | 4 |\n\nEach race clip is a real `RaceScene` with AI on every car, `env` god settings, and hard cuts to skip dull parts. Since the player's profile isn't touched, nothing here can change the save. On a touch-only device it runs the same way.\n\n---\n\n## 13. Dev mode\n\n`DEV MODE` is an ordinary item in the pause menu (hidden only in a hub session), also opened from the Map's pause and with `?dev=1`. It's a pad-friendly panel over the running game, 10-foot like the rest, in tabs.\n\n**Live sliders** (left/right changes it at once, ✕ resets one, △ resets all, mouse drags). They write to the sim's `env` or the rules:\n\n| Slider | Default | Range | Sim hook |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Grip | 1 | 0.5–1.5 | `env.gripScale` |\n| Power | 1 | 0.5–2 | `env.powerScale` |\n| Brake strength | 1 | 0.5–1.5 | `env.brakeScale` |\n| Downforce | 1 | 0–2 | scales `clA` |\n| Tyre wear | 1 | 0–5 | `env.wearRate` |\n| Fuel use | 1 | 0–5 | `env.fuelRate` |\n| Steering rate | 3 | 1–8 | `inp.steerRate` |\n| Stability strength | 1 | 0–2 | scales `asm` |\n| Traction strength | 1 | 0–2 | scales `tcs` |\n| AI pace | event | 0.7–1.05 | `skill` |\n| AI rubber band | 0 | 0–0.03 | adds to `skill` by gap |\n| Wall speed loss | 1 | 0–2 | scales the barrier's `keep` |\n| Slipstream | 0.65 | 0.4–1 | `dragScale` |\n| Prize money | 1 | 0–5 | career |\n| Chase distance, FOV | 8.5, 58 | 4–14, 40–90 | camera |\n\n**Tools:**\n\n- Teleport along the track (scrub `s`), finish now in position N, end race, restart, reset car.\n- Time scale 0.25, 0.5, 1, 2, 4. Pause and frame step. Save state and load state (sim JSON).\n- God mode (no wall loss, no wear, no fuel use).\n- Free camera; engine debug panel (`engine.showDebug`).\n- Overlays: racing line and speed profile, wheel load and slip bars, surface tint, collision circles, AI targets and lanes, telemetry strip (speed, throttle, brake, ax, ay).\n- Give credits; unlock all cars; pass all tests with gold; win the current event; jump to any event, test or menu set; reset the save.\n- Hour of day, rain toggle, field size, change car on the spot, start attract now.\n\nSliders persist for the session and are cleared by Reset. Medals and records earned with sliders off default aren't saved, and the HUD shows a small `DEV` tag so a tester knows.\n\n---\n\n## 14. Multiplayer\n\nThe brief points to the AI Hub session protocol, in the hub repository's `lib/hub/README.md`. Read it whole. Two things to build:\n\n### In a session: ghost mode\n\nWhy ghost and not shared world: every copy runs its own physics at 240 Hz with no input lag. A shared world through a host, at up to 20 messages a second with 150 ms of latency each way, would make a precise driving game feel wrong. Ghost also needs no host, so it survives anyone leaving.\n\n**Tags,** in `<head>`, after the client:\n\n```html\n<meta name=\"hub:session\" content=\"1\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-players\" content=\"1-4\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-mode\" content=\"ghost\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-minutes\" content=\"4\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-goal\" content=\"Lowest time wins: same car, same track\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-howto\" content=\"Steer stick or A D. R2 or W go. L2 or S brake.\">\n<meta name=\"hub:webgpu\" content=\"required\">\n```\n\nThe hub's examples also carry `hub:type` and `hub:players`: use `Game`, and `1-2` for players on one device. Your exit interview's players field is `1–4`, counting sessions.\n\n**A round:**\n\n- When `AIHub.active`: no title, no menus, no attract, no Dev mode, no saves. Await `connect` before anything else, then `Engine.start` a `RaceScene` with `seed: info.seed`. If `connect` returns `null`, play as on its own.\n- `AIHub.rng('round')` picks the same round on every copy: track and car from a fixed list, tuned to about 4 minutes: Kestrel 2 laps, Cedar 2, Harbour 2, Saltline 4; cars Ginga, Ryu, Brandt 3.2, Kite. All get the same stock setup and default assists. Hour and rain also come from the same rng.\n- **Own card** (`card: true`), so the round opens in the game's world: the cars lined up at the start, each seat's colour shown, the track map, how to win, the controls for the last-used device, the countdown from the `card` event. Enter or ✕ answers Ready; △ or M answers More time (also on-screen buttons for mouse and tap). It has no other choices. Use `AIHub.rng('card')` for anything random in it. Read Enter from a `window` `keydown` listener (engine fact 7; tested).\n- Call `AIHub.ready()` as soon as the scene is built, with no key press. The harness waits 20 s for this, so build only this round's track. Keep the build fast and don't load extras in a session.\n- **Start:** on `start`, three red lights with `AIHub.time()` at −3, −2, −1, green at 0. Controls lock until then. Standing start, no AI cars.\n- **While racing:** at 10 Hz, `AIHub.send({ t: 'g', a: trackId, x, y, z, yaw, v, s, lap, steer, br }, { reliable: false })`, values rounded to keep it small. Draw others as ghost cars, interpolated through a 150 ms buffer, in seat colours with name tags. They never collide or affect anything. `AIHub.progress({ score: totalMetres, label: 'LAP 1/2 P2' })` on the same tick.\n- **Finish:** `AIHub.result({ time: Math.round(AIHub.time()), stats: { 'Best lap': seconds, 'Top km/h': n, 'Walls': n }, awards: [...] })` once. `AIHub.send({ t: 'done', time })` reliable. Then an AI drives your car while you wait, and your camera can follow another ghost.\n- **`time-up`:** if not finished, `AIHub.result({ score: totalMetres, stats })` at once.\n- **`leave`:** remove that ghost. **`end`:** stop input and all sound including `enginesynth`; the hub shows results over the game.\n- **Menu:** Options or Esc opens settings over the running race (Sound, Button glyphs, Handbook). It never pauses and offers no quit or restart. Ignore Options while Create is held.\n- **Rules:** no `alert`, `confirm`, `prompt`, `window.open`, no navigation, no other network, no chat. Names only in HUD text, with the `P1` fallback.\n- If the device is touch-only: show the required-input message, still call `ready()`, and on `time-up` report `score: 0`. A connected pad or key lifts it, as usual.\n\n### On its own\n\nYour brief's phrase \"as well as for the game on its own\" is read two ways, and this plan satisfies both. **First**, the game runs fully outside a session: `AIHub.active` false means none of the session rules apply and no network is used. **Second**, multiplayer also exists without the hub, on one screen:\n\n**Couch Duel** (Arcade Gate): two players, one TV, split screen, any track and car, with optional AI cars and real contact. Implementation (tested): `src/game/net/couch.js` makes the page two side-by-side containers and starts a second engine in the second. One `RaceSim` is shared: P1's `RaceScene.fixedUpdate` steps it, P2's only draws it. P2's engine has `settings: { button: false, key: false }`, `audio.persist = false`, muted; one mix plays both cars' engines, panned left and right. Pads are read raw, so P1 is pad 0, P2 is pad 1; with one pad, the pad is P1 and the keyboard is P2; with none, P1 is WASD and P2 is arrows, with Enter and Space remapped. Each side has its own compact HUD. On leaving, `engine2.dispose()`, hide the second container, restore the first to full width.\n\n**Also:** Time Trial's ghost can be raced in turn by whoever holds the pad (\"Pass the pad\", best time wins), using the same ghost view.\n\n---\n\n## 15. About, credits and the exit interview\n\nThe brief says exactly what this must contain. Build it once, as data, then show it two ways:\n\n1. Fill in the exit-interview file the brief names, in the exact shape the brief gives, and check it parses.\n2. `tools/build.mjs` reads that file and embeds it, so the in-game **About/Credits** screen and your final chat reply can't drift apart.\n\n**The About screen** is paged for the TV, reached from Title, Map and Settings:\n\n- **Source Information:** date, source, plan (this plan's file name), genre and perspective, tech, art direction, animation, audio, online, testing, research, environment, model, usage (`added on import`, exactly as the brief says). \"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm.\n- **Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback, Prompt Feedback:** each question from the brief, with your answer. Answer honestly from what really happened, at the end of the work.\n- **Credits:** the game's title and maker line; \"Inspired by\" the source; Pixel Engine with version; three.js r186 (MIT) and Rapier (Apache-2.0) and the Sweetie 16 palette by GrafxKid, from the kit's `CREDITS.md`, which also says what it asks you to ship: embed its text on a scrollable page.\n\nPaginate long answers. Never use a dense wall: it's still a TV.\n\nUse the same answers in your final chat reply, as the brief requires. Don't estimate usage; write what the brief says to write.\n\n**The `aiHub` block:** type `Game`, genre `Racing`, source and series for the source named in your brief, players `1–4`, controls in one line (`Stick or WASD to steer, R2 or W to go, L2 or S to brake`), and tags such as `3d`, `pixel art`, `vehicles`, `retro`, `third-person`, `versus`. The name is your title. Write the tagline and description yourself, in the catalogue voice.\n\n**Research:** the director's research on the kit, the hub protocol and the source is folded into this plan. You don't need web research unless you want it. In Source Information, report what you yourself did.\n\n---\n\n## 16. Testing\n\nThe brief's rules: one command that prints only failures and a one-line count; rerun only after changing what it covers; screenshots only for what a script can't check; no subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\n\nWrite `node tools/test.mjs [suite...]`. Suites cache by file hash, so an unchanged suite is skipped. Output: failures, then `tests: 212 passed, 0 failed`.\n\n| Suite | Runs in | Covers |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `sim` | Node, seconds | The regression table in Appendix B ±3 s; 0–100; each car laps each track; determinism (same inputs, same hash); reverse, manual gears, airborne; contact doesn't explode; wear and fuel over ten laps |\n| `data` | Node | Every id resolves; every event winnable with a car that's obtainable by then; every test has medals; all text fits the 60-character line and 4-line card |\n| `career` | Node | Money, licences, unlocks, prizes, oil, days; a save survives a version migration |\n| `headless` | Node, `Engine.headless` | Menu flows by actions: new game, buy car, test, tune, race, results, save. `engine.replay` ends on the same `fingerprint`. Use `check.mjs --headless` too. Music at 0.1 on a fresh profile; Reset sets it back |\n| `play` | Node, `Engine.headless` | **The whole game by script:** a bot presses real actions through real screens: buys the Kite, passes licences, wins events (driving through the input layer, using `aiDrive` as its brain with `skill` 1.0), tunes, buys, pits, reaches the Grand Prix, sees the ending. Dev tools off. It reports money at each step, and fails if the game can't be finished without cheating |\n| `browser` | Playwright, the built HTML from `file://` | Boots with no console errors. Pad stub (`add_init_script` replacing `navigator.getGamepads`, standard mapping) drives every top-level screen. Prompts swap on input change; Xbox glyphs setting. Mouse hover moves focus, click confirms. Attract starts at 10 s, any input exits. Dev mode sliders change `env`, Reset restores. Touch-only profile (Chromium with `has_touch`, `is_mobile`) shows the gate, the settings tap works, and attract runs. Couch Duel with two pad stubs: two engines up, then disposed, no errors. About shows every field |\n| `audio` | Playwright | `enginesynth` rendered in an `OfflineAudioContext`: non-silent, and spectral centroid rises with rpm. Analyser on the live mix is non-silent in a race. `audio.counts` shows shifts, kerbs, tyre, voice. Music at 10% produces a level well under effects |\n| `hub` | Playwright, harness served on an OS-picked port from a folder holding the HTML and `tools/hub/` | `?game=<html>&players=2&auto=1`, `&players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1`, and `&scenario=host-leave`. Pass means `window.__hubCheck.pass`. Also opens the game outside a session and checks it still reaches the title |\n\n**Machine notes.**\n\n- Chromium flags that worked for WebGPU: `--enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-vulkan=swiftshader --use-angle=swiftshader --use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader`.\n- System Python has Playwright: `from playwright.sync_api import sync_playwright` (don't add `-I`). Node needs `npm install playwright@1.63.0` in your own folder.\n- The kit's `check.mjs` picks its own port. For the harness, run `python3 -u -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` (the `-u` matters) in a folder with the game, and confirm you got your own page.\n- One or two browsers at a time. Close them. Stop only your own PIDs. Keep all files in your folder, not `/tmp`. WebKit here has no WebGPU, so it can't run this engine: note that in testing feedback rather than chasing it.\n- In tests, drive time with `engine.step(n)` rather than waiting. Treat \"Target closed\" as memory, not a bug: run fewer browsers.\n\n**Look at these at 1920×1080**, when their code changes, because no script can judge them: each car in the showroom (`check.mjs --sheets` first), each track at the start and at a corner, Map at day and night, a race HUD, a results screen, Settings, Handbook page, Tuning, the Harbour at night, the rally crest, the pit stop, the podium, Couch Duel, the attract mode's first clips. At each ask: can this be read from ten feet? Is one idea on screen?\n\n---\n\n## 17. Build order\n\nRisky and central first. After each step, the game should run. Run the matching suite before moving on.\n\n1. **Pipeline.** Kit in `src/`, `tools/build.mjs` (C1), a blank scene from `file://`, `tools/test.mjs` skeleton.\n2. **Sim in Node.** Port Appendix A and B. The `sim` suite must match the numbers. Add `RaceSim`.\n3. **One car on one track.** Track mesh, car kit, chase camera, race HUD, pad and keyboard. Spend real time here until driving feels good on the stick, the keyboard and at speed. Body motion, sound and smoke are part of feel, so do the engine sound now.\n4. **A race.** AI field, rolling start, results, replay recording and director.\n5. **All tracks, dressed.** Hour, night, rain, dirt, crests, slipstream.\n6. **Menu world.** UI widgets, glyphs and prompts, Map, Dealer Row, Garage, saves.\n7. **Career.** Licences and medals, events, tuning, setup sliders, service, prizes, podium, Museum, Handbook.\n8. **Pit stops and rally,** then the Harbour Lights 200.\n9. **Attract mode, Dev mode, Settings, touch gate, About.**\n10. **Multiplayer.** Hub round first (harness), then Couch Duel.\n11. **Whole-game playthrough script,** balance, audio mix, voices, music, polish, screenshots.\n12. **Finish.** Exit interview, final build, every suite green, chat reply.\n\n**If budget runs short,** cut content counts and never features. Floors: 10 cars covering all five layouts, 5 tracks including one dirt and one night, 9 licence tests across three licences, 5 events including rally and endurance. Every feature in this plan still works with fewer items. Never leave a stub, a placeholder or a menu item that goes nowhere: the brief forbids it.\n\n---\n\n## 18. Done checklist\n\n**Requirements, with where each is covered:**\n\n| The brief asks for | Here |\n| --- | --- |\n| Genre and perspective picked | §1 |\n| Title: playful riff, brainstormed, picked by real RNG | Yours, as the brief says; `TITLE` constant, §2 guardrails |\n| Whether to research | §15 |\n| One HTML, named as the brief says, dev files in the named subfolder | §4, C1 |\n| Build on the kit, engine unchanged | §3, §4 |\n| Art: engine's style as base, new assets for the source's feel | §8, C2 |\n| Animation: engine's system as base, new animations | §9 |\n| Audio in code; gibberish voices; music at 10% by default | §10 |\n| Online: hub protocol, in a session and on its own | §14, C4 |\n| Share the machine; `file://` first; OS-picked ports; own PIDs only | §16 |\n| Performance out of scope; add detail freely | §3 fact 17, §8 |\n| Headless tests; a scripted whole-game run; one command; one-line count | §16 |\n| No subagents to review or debate | §16 |\n| Original game, feature-complete demo, long enough to show everything | §1, §2, §6 |\n| Infrastructure for designers to continue | §4 Designer tools |\n| Dev mode from the pause menu, live sliders, reset | §13 |\n| Attract mode at 10 s, any input exits, may cheat | §12 |\n| Narrative tone like the source's | §11 |\n| 10-foot, controller-first, native keyboard and mouse, not dense | §7 |\n| Prompts follow last input; PlayStation glyphs, Xbox option | §7 |\n| Touch-only gate, settings button, attract runs | §7 |\n| No TODOs, placeholders or stubs | §17, below |\n| Source Information, Testing, Engine and Prompt Feedback in chat and on an About/Credits screen | §15 |\n| Exit interview file in the brief's exact shape | §15 |\n\n**Before you stop:**\n\n- [ ] `node tools/test.mjs` is all green, with the hub suite passing all three runs.\n- [ ] The scripted playthrough finishes the career with no Dev tools.\n- [ ] The built HTML runs from `file://`, with `session.js` unmodified and first in `<head>`, and `pixel-engine.js` unedited in `src/`.\n- [ ] Music starts at 10% on a fresh profile and in a session.\n- [ ] Grep for `TODO`, `FIXME`, `placeholder`, `stub`, `coming soon`: nothing in the game.\n- [ ] Every menu item does something. Every car, track, test and event is reachable.\n- [ ] You looked at the 1080p screenshots.\n- [ ] About/Credits shows real answers. The exit-interview file parses as JSON. Your chat reply carries the same information.\n- [ ] No stray servers or browsers of yours left running.\n\n---\n## Appendix A: `src/game/sim/sim.js`\n\nTested code. It ran every car on every track, an 8-car race with contact, and the checks in Appendix B. Port it as is, then extend it.","numLines":218,"startLine":642,"totalLines":1331}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01NkQchQgzCjEaXjzvf1CgVx","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
