{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Rur12f9F7SdWS14NQZxHJy","type":"tool_result","content":"## Appendix B: data and regression numbers\n\n### Tracks\n\n```js\n// src/game/data/tracks.js: every circuit as a closed polygon with rounded corners. Corner: [x, z, radius, y, width, bankDeg].\n// s = 0 is the middle of the edge from the last corner to the first (the start line). runoff: metres from the road edge to the barrier.\n// All six run clockwise as seen by the driver (mostly right turns), so the infield is on the driver's right: pit lanes go there, the main grandstand \n// pit: metres from the start line along the start straight. crests: [s, half length, height].\nexport const TRACKS = {\n  kestrel: { name: 'Kestrel Park', surface: 'asphalt', edge: 'grass', runoff: 9, hour: 15, pit: { in: -260, box: 0, out: 260 }, corners: [\n    [640, 0, 60, 0, 13, 0], [760, 190, 110, 2, 13, 0], [640, 330, 50, 6, 13, 0], [780, 470, 80, 8, 13, 0], [700, 720, 70, 12, 13, 0], [470, 760, 40, 1\n### Regression: spec sheet\n\nFull throttle on a long flat road, `tcs: 0.5`. Your `sim` suite should match within 5%. These are also the numbers the S\n\n| Car | 0–100 km/h (s) | 0–200 km/h (s) | Top speed (km/h) | 100–0 km/h (m) |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Pico 660 | 10.5 | - | 163 | 45 |\n| Kite 1.6 RS | 7.4 | 36.1 | 214 | 43 |\n| Ginga S | 6.6 | 24.0 | 243 | 43 |\n| Moth 1.8 | 6.7 | 31.2 | 219 | 42 |\n| Brandt 3.2 | 6.1 | 17.6 | 278 | 42 |\n| Ryu 2.0T | 4.4 | 17.3 | 243 | 41 |\n| Longhorn 428 | 6.5 | 15.9 | 285 | 43 |\n| Heck 3.6 | 4.8 | 13.8 | 301 | 40 |\n| Libeccio | 5.0 | 12.8 | 279 | 40 |\n| Kite Cup | 5.8 | 18.6 | 219 | 35 |\n| Ryu Rally | 4.0 | 13.1 | 217 | 41 |\n| Works GT | 4.4 | 9.8 | 289 | 32 |\n| Corsa | 3.8 | 8.2 | 299 | 30 |\n| Type 9 | 3.4 | 7.0 | 314 | 27 |\n\n### Regression: laps at 97% AI pace\n\nSeconds, second lap, assists as in `aiDrive`, `wearRate: 0`, `fuelRate: 0`. Your port should match within ±3 s. They als\n\n| Car | Kestrel | Cedar | Harbour | Saltline | Red Clay | Proving |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Pico 660 | 136 | 133 | 150 | 75 | 163 | 128 |\n| Kite 1.6 RS | 125 | 124 | 140 | 64 | 157 | 118 |\n| Ginga S | 122 | 120 | 135 | 59 | 146 | 115 |\n| Moth 1.8 | 123 | 121 | 135 | 61 | 145 | 115 |\n| Brandt 3.2 | 120 | 118 | 133 | 58 | 145 | 112 |\n| Ryu 2.0T | 117 | 115 | 127 | 57 | 139 | 110 |\n| Longhorn 428 | 121 | 120 | 135 | 58 | 147 | 113 |\n| Heck 3.6 | 115 | 113 | 126 | 62 | 137 | 108 |\n| Libeccio | 114 | 112 | 126 | 59 | 137 | 106 |\n| Kite Cup | 110 | 109 | 125 | 54 | 143 | 105 |\n| Ryu Rally | 116 | 114 | 126 | 58 | 115 | 109 |\n| Works GT | 104 | 102 | 115 | 49 | 142 | 97 |\n| Corsa | 101 | 99 | 111 | 54 | 137 | 94 |\n| Type 9 | 92 | 90 | 105 | 41 | 129 | 87 |\n\nHeck on Saltline is slow because the RR car overcooks at the AI's speed there; don't read too much into one outlier, and\n\n---\n\n## Appendix A: `src/game/sim/sim.js`\n\nTested code. It ran every car on every track, an 8-car race with contact, and the checks in Appendix B. Port it as is, then extend it.\n\n**How the pieces connect:**\n\n- Per fixed step (60 Hz), get one input per car, then 4 times: `stepCar` for each car, `collideCars(field)` when contact is on.\n- `env` is optional per step: `gripScale`, `powerScale`, `brakeScale`, `aeroScale`, `wearRate`, `fuelRate`, `wallLoss`, `kerb`. Dev mode's s\n- Car-level fields the race code sets: `c.dragScale` (slipstream), `c.oil` (service), `c.inPit` (pit lane surface).\n- `tr.wallL`, `tr.wallR` are per-sample barrier distances from the road edge. `tr.muScale` is rain. `tr.cornerS[n]` is corner n's apex.\n- Everything is deterministic for the same inputs.\n- Add reverse by setting `inp.reverse`. Add AI lapses and pit autopilot in `race.js`, not here.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"66624378-a7bb-42a2-8c1d-fc63666b5654","uuid":"0daac8e6-5f68-411d-afb4-6019f143ebf7","timestamp":"2026-11-06T00:17:55.345Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"## Appendix B: data and regression numbers\n\n### Tracks\n\n```js\n// src/game/data/tracks.js: every circuit as a closed polygon with rounded corners. Corner: [x, z, radius, y, width, bankDeg].\n// s = 0 is the middle of the edge from the last corner to the first (the start line). runoff: metres from the road edge to the barrier.\n// All six run clockwise as seen by the driver (mostly right turns), so the infield is on the driver's right: pit lanes go there, the main grandstand \n// pit: metres from the start line along the start straight. crests: [s, half length, height].\nexport const TRACKS = {\n  kestrel: { name: 'Kestrel Park', surface: 'asphalt', edge: 'grass', runoff: 9, hour: 15, pit: { in: -260, box: 0, out: 260 }, corners: [\n    [640, 0, 60, 0, 13, 0], [760, 190, 110, 2, 13, 0], [640, 330, 50, 6, 13, 0], [780, 470, 80, 8, 13, 0], [700, 720, 70, 12, 13, 0], [470, 760, 40, 1\n### Regression: spec sheet\n\nFull throttle on a long flat road, `tcs: 0.5`. Your `sim` suite should match within 5%. These are also the numbers the S\n\n| Car | 0–100 km/h (s) | 0–200 km/h (s) | Top speed (km/h) | 100–0 km/h (m) |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Pico 660 | 10.5 | - | 163 | 45 |\n| Kite 1.6 RS | 7.4 | 36.1 | 214 | 43 |\n| Ginga S | 6.6 | 24.0 | 243 | 43 |\n| Moth 1.8 | 6.7 | 31.2 | 219 | 42 |\n| Brandt 3.2 | 6.1 | 17.6 | 278 | 42 |\n| Ryu 2.0T | 4.4 | 17.3 | 243 | 41 |\n| Longhorn 428 | 6.5 | 15.9 | 285 | 43 |\n| Heck 3.6 | 4.8 | 13.8 | 301 | 40 |\n| Libeccio | 5.0 | 12.8 | 279 | 40 |\n| Kite Cup | 5.8 | 18.6 | 219 | 35 |\n| Ryu Rally | 4.0 | 13.1 | 217 | 41 |\n| Works GT | 4.4 | 9.8 | 289 | 32 |\n| Corsa | 3.8 | 8.2 | 299 | 30 |\n| Type 9 | 3.4 | 7.0 | 314 | 27 |\n\n### Regression: laps at 97% AI pace\n\nSeconds, second lap, assists as in `aiDrive`, `wearRate: 0`, `fuelRate: 0`. Your port should match within ±3 s. They als\n\n| Car | Kestrel | Cedar | Harbour | Saltline | Red Clay | Proving |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Pico 660 | 136 | 133 | 150 | 75 | 163 | 128 |\n| Kite 1.6 RS | 125 | 124 | 140 | 64 | 157 | 118 |\n| Ginga S | 122 | 120 | 135 | 59 | 146 | 115 |\n| Moth 1.8 | 123 | 121 | 135 | 61 | 145 | 115 |\n| Brandt 3.2 | 120 | 118 | 133 | 58 | 145 | 112 |\n| Ryu 2.0T | 117 | 115 | 127 | 57 | 139 | 110 |\n| Longhorn 428 | 121 | 120 | 135 | 58 | 147 | 113 |\n| Heck 3.6 | 115 | 113 | 126 | 62 | 137 | 108 |\n| Libeccio | 114 | 112 | 126 | 59 | 137 | 106 |\n| Kite Cup | 110 | 109 | 125 | 54 | 143 | 105 |\n| Ryu Rally | 116 | 114 | 126 | 58 | 115 | 109 |\n| Works GT | 104 | 102 | 115 | 49 | 142 | 97 |\n| Corsa | 101 | 99 | 111 | 54 | 137 | 94 |\n| Type 9 | 92 | 90 | 105 | 41 | 129 | 87 |\n\nHeck on Saltline is slow because the RR car overcooks at the AI's speed there; don't read too much into one outlier, and\n\n---\n\n## Appendix A: `src/game/sim/sim.js`\n\nTested code. It ran every car on every track, an 8-car race with contact, and the checks in Appendix B. Port it as is, then extend it.\n\n**How the pieces connect:**\n\n- Per fixed step (60 Hz), get one input per car, then 4 times: `stepCar` for each car, `collideCars(field)` when contact is on.\n- `env` is optional per step: `gripScale`, `powerScale`, `brakeScale`, `aeroScale`, `wearRate`, `fuelRate`, `wallLoss`, `kerb`. Dev mode's s\n- Car-level fields the race code sets: `c.dragScale` (slipstream), `c.oil` (service), `c.inPit` (pit lane surface).\n- `tr.wallL`, `tr.wallR` are per-sample barrier distances from the road edge. `tr.muScale` is rain. `tr.cornerS[n]` is corner n's apex.\n- Everything is deterministic for the same inputs.\n- Add reverse by setting `inp.reverse`. Add AI lapses and pit autopilot in `race.js`, not here.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01Rur12f9F7SdWS14NQZxHJy","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgkiN31fPBGmrjgJGLze6","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_018u8uMHh5sD6Bk4QMA5LAu7","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261105-162057__x4c6ec5/work/planner-exit-interview.json","content":"[\n  {\n    \"question\": \"What did you like most about this prompt?\",\n    \"answer\": \"It is concrete and checkable. The This computer section told me exactly which browsers, tools and limits I had, so I could test the engine and hub for real instead of guessing, and the hub harness gives a plain pass or fail. It also left the creative calls (genre, perspective, how much to research) to me while being strict about what done means: a dev mode with sliders, attract mode at 10 seconds, the 10-foot UI, music at 10%, and an exit interview with a fixed shape. That made the plan easy to make complete, because I could check it line by line against the brief.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"question\": \"What was confusing?\",\n    \"answer\": \"Three things. First, the Multiplayer line, 'for multiplayer integration into ai-hub as well as for the game on its own': it can mean the game must simply still run outside a session, or that it needs multiplayer without the hub. I couldn't settle it, so the plan does both, with a split-screen mode. Second, TASK.md is written to the builder while I was the planner: the title process, the file names, the exit interview and the chat reply are all addressed to someone else, and the goal says PLAN.md must cover everything TASK.md asks. I had to decide what 'cover' means for things only a builder can do, and I settled on telling the builder where each one goes. Third, some instructions pull against the materials: the brief prefers file:// while the engine guide says ES modules don't load from file://, and 'keep the engine as it ships' sits next to a single-HTML deliverable. Both only resolved once I tried bundling. The title as a riff on the source's name, in a game that must not be a recreation, also leaves open how close a riff may get.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"question\": \"What was the hardest thing to do?\",\n    \"answer\": \"Choosing how much to decide for the builders without knowing who they are or what budget they have. The plan had to work for builders I can't talk to, so anything risky needed to be settled rather than described. The hardest single part was the driving model: the engine's Vehicle is arcade-level, so I had to design a car model that makes tuning felt and keep it stable. Getting there meant finding and fixing a weave, then spins under braking, then sliding wide, before all 14 cars lapped all six tracks in a scripted test. Even so, no human has driven it, and I could not judge how it feels on a real pad, so the plan says so. The other hard part was length: every section I added made the plan safer to build from and longer to read, and it ended up large, at about 120 KB including code.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"question\": \"What would you change in this prompt?\",\n    \"answer\": \"Say who the builders are and roughly what budget they have, so a plan can be pitched at the right depth and scope. Say whether a plan may include working code and data, and give a size limit if there is one. Spell out the on-its-own multiplayer requirement in one sentence. Mark which parts of TASK.md are for the builder only, or give the planner a version with those removed, and say how 'covers everything' applies to them. Add a line to the brief or the kit saying that a bundled, inlined build is the expected way to reach a single HTML on file://, since that one question decides the whole build pipeline. Also say whether planners may leave their experiments behind for builders: I deleted mine and moved what mattered into the plan, but sharing a tested starter project would save everyone work.\"\n  }\n]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4294,"cache_read_input_tokens":608401,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4294},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"66624378-a7bb-42a2-8c1d-fc63666b5654","uuid":"8bb94c1f-1187-4645-9475-626479f2b3c3","timestamp":"2026-11-06T00:18:09.655Z","request_id":"req_011CgkiN2gpiL4rdefUBBiFV","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_018u8uMHh5sD6Bk4QMA5LAu7":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261105-162057__x4c6ec5/work/planner-exit-interview.json","content":"[\n  {\n    \"question\": \"What did you like most about this prompt?\",\n    \"answer\": \"It is concrete and checkable. The This computer section told me exactly which browsers, tools and limits I had, so I could test the engine and hub for real instead of guessing, and the hub harness gives a plain pass or fail. It also left the creative calls (genre, perspective, how much to research) to me while being strict about what done means: a dev mode with sliders, attract mode at 10 seconds, the 10-foot UI, music at 10%, and an exit interview with a fixed shape. That made the plan easy to make complete, because I could check it line by line against the brief.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"question\": \"What was confusing?\",\n    \"answer\": \"Three things. First, the Multiplayer line, 'for multiplayer integration into ai-hub as well as for the game on its own': it can mean the game must simply still run outside a session, or that it needs multiplayer without the hub. I couldn't settle it, so the plan does both, with a split-screen mode. Second, TASK.md is written to the builder while I was the planner: the title process, the file names, the exit interview and the chat reply are all addressed to someone else, and the goal says PLAN.md must cover everything TASK.md asks. I had to decide what 'cover' means for things only a builder can do, and I settled on telling the builder where each one goes. Third, some instructions pull against the materials: the brief prefers file:// while the engine guide says ES modules don't load from file://, and 'keep the engine as it ships' sits next to a single-HTML deliverable. Both only resolved once I tried bundling. The title as a riff on the source's name, in a game that must not be a recreation, also leaves open how close a riff may get.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"question\": \"What was the hardest thing to do?\",\n    \"answer\": \"Choosing how much to decide for the builders without knowing who they are or what budget they have. The plan had to work for builders I can't talk to, so anything risky needed to be settled rather than described. The hardest single part was the driving model: the engine's Vehicle is arcade-level, so I had to design a car model that makes tuning felt and keep it stable. Getting there meant finding and fixing a weave, then spins under braking, then sliding wide, before all 14 cars lapped all six tracks in a scripted test. Even so, no human has driven it, and I could not judge how it feels on a real pad, so the plan says so. The other hard part was length: every section I added made the plan safer to build from and longer to read, and it ended up large, at about 120 KB including code.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"question\": \"What would you change in this prompt?\",\n    \"answer\": \"Say who the builders are and roughly what budget they have, so a plan can be pitched at the right depth and scope. Say whether a plan may include working code and data, and give a size limit if there is one. Spell out the on-its-own multiplayer requirement in one sentence. Mark which parts of TASK.md are for the builder only, or give the planner a version with those removed, and say how 'covers everything' applies to them. Add a line to the brief or the kit saying that a bundled, inlined build is the expected way to reach a single HTML on file://, since that one question decides the whole build pipeline. Also say whether planners may leave their experiments behind for builders: I deleted mine and moved what mattered into the plan, but sharing a tested starter project would save everyone work.\"\n  }\n]\n"}}}
